1 Stunde, 47 Sekunden
world, reason about it, plan your actions, execute the plan. And there's an elegance to it. It mirrors how we
1:00:56
1 Stunde, 56 Sekunden
consciously think about solving problems. But as researchers tried to apply it to more complex, more realistic
1:01:03
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 3 Sekunden
situations, the approach buckled under its own weight. The world is too complex to model fully. There are too many
1:01:11
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 11 Sekunden
objects, too many possible states, too many things that can change between the moment you plan an action and the moment
1:01:20
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 20 Sekunden
you execute it. The robot sits and thinks. And while it thinks, someone walks through the room, the lighting
1:01:28
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 28 Sekunden
changes. An object falls off a shelf. By the time the plan is ready, the world has moved on. And this is where things
1:01:37
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 37 Sekunden
got really interesting because a provocative researcher arrived on the scene with a radically different idea.
1:01:44
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 44 Sekunden
His name was Rodney Brooks and he was an Australian-born computer scientist who came to MIT in 1984 and almost
1:01:53
1 Stunde, 1 Minute, 53 Sekunden
immediately started challenging everything the field believed about how robot intelligence should work. Brooks looked at Shaky and its descendants,
1:02:02
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
robots that spent minutes thinking before taking a single action, and declared that the whole approach was fundamentally wrong, not just
1:02:11
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 11 Sekunden
impractical, philosophically wrong. He pointed out that after 25 years and tens
1:02:18
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 18 Sekunden
of millions of dollars of research, the most sophisticated AI powered robots could barely function in a room full of
1:02:27
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 27 Sekunden
colored blocks. Meanwhile, a cockroach with a brain the size of a pin head could navigate complex terrain, avoid
1:02:35
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 35 Sekunden
predators, find food, and mate in real time without any deliberation whatsoever. Something was seriously off about the priorities of the field.
1:02:46
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
Brooks's alternative was inspired by evolution, and he called it the subsumption architecture. The idea was
1:02:54
1 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
provocative in its simplicity. Instead of building a robot with a central brain that maintains a model of the world,
1:03:02
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
reasons about it, and plans actions, the top- down approach, you build the robot as layers of independent behaviors. The
1:03:11
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 11 Sekunden
lowest layer is the most basic. Don't hit things. Above that, wander around.
1:03:17
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 17 Sekunden
Above that, explore interesting areas.
1:03:21
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 21 Sekunden
Above that, seek specific goals. Each layer is a simple circuit connecting sensors directly to actuators. There's
1:03:30
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 30 Sekunden
no central controller, no internal model of the world, no representation at all.
1:03:36
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 36 Sekunden
The intelligence, such as it is, emerges from the interaction of these simple behavior layers with each other and with
1:03:44
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
the environment. Brooks built a series of small robots based on this philosophy. They were insects,
1:03:52
1 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 52 Sekunden
essentially little six-legged walking machines that could navigate cluttered environments, climb over obstacles, and
1:04:01
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 1 Sekunde
respond to their surroundings in real time without stopping to think. His most famous early robot was Genghis, a small
1:04:10
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 10 Sekunden
hexopod about a foot long with 12 motors and a handful of sensors. Genghis could walk over rough terrain with a fluid
1:04:19
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 19 Sekunden
adaptive gate that adjusted automatically when it encountered obstacles or uneven surfaces. Each leg
1:04:26
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 26 Sekunden
had its own simple controller, and the legs coordinated through simple coupling rules rather than centralized planning.
1:04:35
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 35 Sekunden
It was fast, robust, and surprisingly lielike. And it was computationally trivial. Orders of magnitude simpler
1:04:44
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
than Shaky, yet far more effective in the real world. Brooks built Genghis for about $20,000, a tiny fraction of what Shaky had cost.
1:04:55
1 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 55 Sekunden
And it could do things in the physical world that Shaky never could. It could handle uneven surfaces, recover from
1:05:02
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
stumbles, navigate around obstacles in real time. It was fast, robust, and cheap. And its success posed an
1:05:11
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 11 Sekunden
uncomfortable question to the AI establishment. If a machine with no internal model, no reasoning, no
1:05:19
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 19 Sekunden
planning, and almost no computation could outperform robots that had been the subject of decades of research and
1:05:27
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 27 Sekunden
millions of dollars of funding. Maybe the entire approach of classical AI applied to robotics was misguided. In
1:05:35
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 35 Sekunden
1990, Brooks formalized his argument in a paper with the gloriously provocative title, "Elephants don't play chess." His
1:05:44
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
argument was blunt. The real world is its own best model. You don't need to build an internal representation of
1:05:52
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 52 Sekunden
reality if you can just react to reality directly. Animals don't maintain detailed internal models of their
1:05:59
1 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
environment. They sense and act and the ability to interact robustly with the physical world is far more important and
1:06:09
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 9 Sekunden
far more difficult than abstract reasoning. He was essentially saying that AI had spent 30 years focusing on
1:06:16
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
the wrong problems. The reaction in the AI community was explosive. Some researchers thought Brooks was
1:06:24
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
brilliant. Others thought he was a provocator throwing away everything the field had built. And in hindsight, both
1:06:32
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
sides had pieces of the truth. Modern robotics uses both approaches. fast
1:06:38
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 38 Sekunden
reactive behaviors for low-level control layered with higher level planning and reasoning when needed. The false
1:06:46
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
dichotomy between reactive and deliberative turned out to be exactly that, false. You need reflexes and you
1:06:54
1 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
need thought. But Brooks's contribution was invaluable. He forced the field to take the physical world seriously, to
1:07:03
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 3 Sekunden
stop treating intelligence as purely abstract and to grapple with the messy realtime embodied challenges of moving
1:07:12
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 12 Sekunden
through space and interacting with things. Brooks also had a remarkable instinct for what would actually sell.
1:07:20
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 20 Sekunden
In 1990, he co-founded iRoot, the company that would eventually create the Roomba robot vacuum cleaner. The first
1:07:29
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 29 Sekunden
Roomba was released in 2002, and it might be the most successful consumer robot in history. Over 40 million units
1:07:37
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 37 Sekunden
sold by the early 2020s. It's a direct descendant of Brooks's philosophy, a
1:07:43
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 43 Sekunden
small discshaped robot that uses simple behaviors. Wander, bump into something,
1:07:50
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 50 Sekunden
turn, continue to cover a floor, and clean it. No map of your house, no sophisticated planning, just reactive
1:07:59
1 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
behavior producing useful results. And the Roomba illustrates something important about robotics. The robots
1:08:07
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
that actually get deployed, the ones real people actually use are almost never the ones that capture headlines.
1:08:16
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
They're not humanoids. They're not androids. their vacuums and welding arms and warehouse sorders and surgical
1:08:24
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
tools. They work because they do specific things well, not because they try to do everything. And since we
1:08:32
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
mentioned warehouse robots a moment ago, let's talk about one of the most impressive deployments of robotics in
1:08:38
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 38 Sekunden
the 21st century because it illustrates beautifully how simple robots doing simple things in a clever system can
1:08:46
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
transform an entire industry. In 2012, Amazon acquired a small robotics company called KA Systems for $775 million.
1:08:58
1 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 58 Sekunden
KA had developed a system of small squat orange robots, each about the size and shape of an oversized Roomba that
1:09:07
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
operated in warehouses. Instead of humans walking through aisles to find products, the robots picked up entire
1:09:14
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
shelving units and carried them to human workers at packing stations. The robots navigated by reading barcodes on the
1:09:23
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 23 Sekunden
warehouse floor, communicated with a central system to avoid collisions, and could carry shelving units weighing up
1:09:30
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 30 Sekunden
to 750 lb. They weren't fancy. They weren't humanoid. They didn't have arms or cameras or language understanding.
1:09:40
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 40 Sekunden
They were essentially very strong, very organized luggage carts, but Amazon deployed tens of thousands of them
1:09:49
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 49 Sekunden
across its fulfillment centers. And the impact was enormous, reducing the time to process an order from over an hour to
1:09:57
1 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 57 Sekunden
about 15 minutes and reducing the physical toll on warehouse workers who had previously walked 10 or more miles
1:10:05
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 5 Sekunden
per shift. That's the Brooks philosophy in action. Simple robots, simple behaviors, massive practical impact, not
1:10:14
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
a humanoid thinking about philosophy. an orange disc picking up shelves and carrying them to the right spot. The
1:10:22
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 22 Sekunden
unsexy future of robotics is often the one that actually happens. But before we get to self-driving cars, let's take a
1:10:31
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
detour to two other domains where robots have been quietly spectacularly successful.
1:10:37
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 37 Sekunden
Space and surgery. Because these stories illustrate something important about where robotics actually excels. Space is
1:10:47
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 47 Sekunden
in many ways the ideal environment for robots. It's too dangerous for sustained human presence. The tasks are often
1:10:55
1 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 55 Sekunden
repetitive and precisely defined, and the cost of failure is so high that extreme reliability is justified. NASA's
1:11:04
1 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 4 Sekunden
