that proved machines could operate in complex open-ended environments is the story of self-driving cars. And it
1:17:08
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 8 Sekunden
begins in the California desert with a race that absolutely nobody was prepared for. In 2003, DARPA, the Defense
1:17:18
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 18 Sekunden
Advanced Research Projects Agency, announced the grand challenge, build a driverless vehicle that could navigate a
1:17:27
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 27 Sekunden
142m course through the Mojave Desert autonomously with no human intervention.
1:17:34
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 34 Sekunden
The prize was a million dollars. Teams from universities, companies, and garages around the country signed up.
1:17:42
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 42 Sekunden
The race took place on March 13th, 2004.
1:17:46
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
The result was, to put it gently, a catastrophe for the field's pride. 15 vehicles started on race day, and the
1:17:54
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
atmosphere was a mix of genuine excitement and nervous uncertainty.
1:17:59
1 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
Nobody really knew if any of them would finish. The course wound through the Mojave, across open desert, through
1:18:07
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
mountain passes, along narrow ridge lines, over underpasses. It was rugged, remote terrain with no clear lane
1:18:16
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
markings, and plenty of obstacles, boulders, ditches, fences, the occasional abandoned vehicle. The most
1:18:24
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
successful entrance was Sandstorm, a heavily modified Humvey fielded by Carnegie Melon University's Red Team, a
1:18:33
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 33 Sekunden
group led by Red Whitaker, a legendary roboticist who had previously built robots to explore volcanoes and clean up the damaged 3M island nuclear reactor.
1:18:45
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 45 Sekunden
and sandstorm made it exactly 7.32 miles before getting stuck on a steep embankment, its tires spinning and then
1:18:53
1 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 53 Sekunden
catching fire from the friction. Most others did worse. One vehicle flipped on its first turn. Another veered off course and got tangled in a fence.
1:19:03
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 3 Sekunden
Several failed before leaving the starting area. The prize went unclaimed and the media had a field day. The
1:19:10
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 10 Sekunden
message seemed clear. self-driving vehicles were a fantasy. But here's where DARPA did something brilliant.
1:19:18
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 18 Sekunden
They didn't cancel the challenge. They doubled the prize to $2 million, and ran it again the following year, October
1:19:27
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 27 Sekunden
2005. And this time, five vehicles completed the entire course. The winner
1:19:34
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 34 Sekunden
was Stanley, a modified Volkswagen Tore built by a team at Stanford University led by a German-B born computer
1:19:43
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 43 Sekunden
scientist named Sebastian Thrun. Stanley used GPS lidar which is like radar but
1:19:51
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 51 Sekunden
with laser light producing detailed three-dimensional point clouds of the environment cameras radar and a set of
1:19:59
1 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
machine learning algorithms that Thrun's team had developed specifically to handle the variability of desert
1:20:06
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 6 Sekunden
terrain. Patches of shadow that looked like obstacles to traditional algorithms. subtle changes in terrain
1:20:14
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
that indicated the difference between road and soft sand. Stanley's systems learned from data rather than relying
1:20:22
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 22 Sekunden
purely on handcrafted rules. It completed the course in about 6 hours and 53 minutes. And it was beautiful. A
1:20:31
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
machine navigating 132 m of desert road with no human at the wheel, making
1:20:38
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 38 Sekunden
thousands of decisions about speed, steering, obstacle avoidance, all on its own. And here's the human side of that
1:20:46
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
story that makes it even more interesting. Sebastian Thrun's interest in self-driving cars wasn't purely
1:20:54
1 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
academic. A close friend of his had died in a car accident and Thrun became almost obsessed with the idea that human
1:21:03
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 3 Sekunden
driving error, which is responsible for roughly 94% of traffic accidents, was a solvable engineering problem. About
1:21:12
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 12 Sekunden
40,000 people die in car crashes in the United States alone every year.
1:21:17
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 17 Sekunden
Worldwide, it's over a million. If autonomous vehicles could reduce those numbers even by half, it would save more
1:21:26
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 26 Sekunden
lives annually than most medical breakthroughs. That moral urgency drove Thrun and many others in the field with
1:21:34
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 34 Sekunden
a conviction that went beyond technical curiosity. Thrun went on to lead Google's self-driving car program, later
1:21:42
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 42 Sekunden
called Whimo, and the technology he pioneered became the foundation of the autonomous vehicle industry. The key
1:21:49
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 49 Sekunden
insight from Stanley was that you couldn't just program explicit rules for driving. The world has too many edge
1:21:57
1 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 57 Sekunden
cases, too many unusual situations, too much variability. You had to use machine learning systems that could learn from
1:22:06
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 6 Sekunden
data to handle conditions that no programmer could fully anticipate. Now, to give you a sense of the technology
1:22:14
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
involved, Stanley carried five LAR units on its roof, each spinning rapidly and shooting thousands of laser pulses per
1:22:23
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 23 Sekunden
second into the environment. The return time of each pulse told the system how far away the nearest surface was in that
1:22:31
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
direction. And the aggregate of all these measurements created a detailed three-dimensional map of the terrain
1:22:38
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 38 Sekunden
ahead, updated multiple times per second. But lidar alone wasn't enough.
1:22:45
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 45 Sekunden
The team discovered that in the desert, lidar had trouble with certain kinds of terrain. Tall grass could look like a
1:22:52
1 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 52 Sekunden
solid wall, and subtle BMS could be invisible until you were right on top of them. So they trained machine learning
1:23:00
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten
algorithms on data from their test runs, teaching the system to use camera images to predict terrain quality. Essentially
1:23:09
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 9 Sekunden
to look at the ground ahead and estimate whether it was safe to drive on based on patterns learned from experience. This
1:23:18
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 18 Sekunden
combination of handgineered sensors and learned perception was ahead of its time and it set the template for the
1:23:25
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 25 Sekunden
autonomous vehicle systems that followed. The 2007 DARPA urban challenge raised the stakes even further. Vehicles
1:23:35
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 35 Sekunden
had to navigate a simulated city environment, intersections, traffic, merging, parking while obeying traffic
1:23:44
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
laws and avoiding other vehicles. The winner was Boss, a modified Chevrolet Tahoe from Carnegie Melon. And it
1:23:52
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 52 Sekunden
navigated a 60-mi urban course in just over 4 hours. From there, the autonomous
1:23:59
1 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
vehicle industry exploded. Google, Tesla, Uber, Whimo, Cruz, billions of
1:24:07
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
dollars in investment. The technology improved dramatically year after year.
1:24:14
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
And yet, as of the mid 2020s, fully autonomous driving in arbitrary conditions remains unsolved. It works in
1:24:23
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 23 Sekunden
specific, well-mapped cities with good weather. It struggles with construction zones, heavy snow, unusual situations,
1:24:32
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
and the infinite creativity of human behavior on the road. The problem, as many engineers have discovered, is what they call the long tale of edge cases.
1:24:44
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
You can get a self-driving system to handle 99% of driving situations relatively well. It's that last 1%
1:24:53
1 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 53 Sekunden
that's murderous. A traffic officer waving you through a red light. A child running into the street from behind a
1:25:01
1 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 1 Sekunde
parked van. A fallen tree blocking one lane. requiring you to cross the center line into oncoming traffic, construction
1:25:10
1 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 10 Sekunden
workers with handdrawn detour signs, a funeral procession, a flock of geese
1:25:17
1 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 17 Sekunden
crossing the highway. Each of these is individually rare, but there are so many of them that collectively they happen
1:25:26
1 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 26 Sekunden
all the time, and each one requires exactly the kind of situational awareness and judgment that humans excel at. and machines still struggle with.
