honest question. Can Atlas do anything useful beyond acrobatics?
1:50:31
1 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
And the answer, as of right now, is sort of. Boston Dynamics has been testing
1:50:38
1 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 38 Sekunden
Atlas in warehouse and factory settings, having it pick up and place automotive parts, navigate work areas, and adapt to
1:50:47
1 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 47 Sekunden
changing conditions. But the demonstrations, impressive as they are, still happen in
1:50:54
1 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
carefully prepared environments. The leap from a choreographed backflip to reliably loading a dishwasher in a
1:51:02
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
random kitchen is immense because the dishwasher loading requires exactly the perception, manipulation, common sense
1:51:11
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 11 Sekunden
reasoning, and adaptability that remains the hardest part. But the humanoid isn't the only exciting direction in modern
1:51:19
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 19 Sekunden
robotics. And it's worth spending a moment on some of the alternatives because they show just how diverse and
1:51:26
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 26 Sekunden
creative the field has become. Soft robotics is one of the most intriguing.
1:51:32
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
Traditional robots are made of rigid materials, metal, hard plastic, carbon fiber. Their joints are mechanical
1:51:40
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 40 Sekunden
hinges with precisely defined axes of rotation. This makes them strong and precise, but also dangerous to work
1:51:49
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 49 Sekunden
alongside. A rigid metal arm moving at speed can cause serious injury on
1:51:55
1 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 55 Sekunden
contact. Soft robots, by contrast, are made from flexible compliant materials.
1:52:02
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
Silicons, elastimemers, sometimes even pneumatically inflated fabric. They bend and squish and conform to objects rather than forcing objects to conform to them.
1:52:15
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 15 Sekunden
Think about how your hand grips a tomato. Your fingers wrap around it, conforming to its shape, applying
1:52:23
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 23 Sekunden
distributed pressure that's firm enough to hold it, but gentle enough not to crush it. A traditional robotic gripper with rigid fingers struggles with this.
1:52:34
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 34 Sekunden
A soft robotic gripper made from flexible material that inflates and curls around the tomato can do it
1:52:41
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 41 Sekunden
naturally. Researchers at Harvard, MIT, and labs around the world have built soft robots inspired by octopus
1:52:50
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 50 Sekunden
tentacles, elephant trunks, and caterpillars.
1:52:54
1 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
These machines can squeeze through tight spaces, handle delicate objects, and interact safely with humans. If a soft
1:53:04
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 4 Sekunden
robot bumps into you, it gives way rather than injuring you. The trade-off is precision. Soft robots are inherently
1:53:12
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 12 Sekunden
less precise than rigid ones because their flexible structure is harder to model and control, but for applications
1:53:20
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 20 Sekunden
where safety and gentleness matter more than micrometer accuracy. Assistive robots for elderly care. Agricultural
1:53:29
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 29 Sekunden
harvesting of delicate fruit. Robots working in close proximity to humans.
1:53:34
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 34 Sekunden
Soft robotics is a genuinely promising direction. Then there's swarm robotics.
1:53:41
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 41 Sekunden
The idea of using many simple robots working together rather than one complex robot working alone. Think of how ants
1:53:49
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 49 Sekunden
build complex structures, how bees organize their hive, how flocking birds move in coordination, all without any
1:53:59
1 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
central controller. All emerging from simple local rules followed by each individual. Swarm robotics applies this
1:54:07
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
principle to machines. Instead of building one expensive, sophisticated robot, you build hundreds or thousands
1:54:14
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 14 Sekunden
of cheap, simple ones that communicate with their neighbors and follow basic rules of interaction. Collectively, they
1:54:23
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 23 Sekunden
can do things that no individual robot could, exploring large areas, building structures, sorting objects, even
1:54:31
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
forming temporary bridges by linking their bodies together. The Kilobot project at Harvard demonstrated this
1:54:39
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 39 Sekunden
with over a thousand simple coin-sized robots that could arrange themselves into arbitrary two-dimensional shapes by
1:54:48
1 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 48 Sekunden
communicating with their nearest neighbors via infrared signals. No central controller, no GPS, just local interactions producing global patterns.
1:55:00
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten
It's essentially Gray Walters's insight about complex behavior emerging from simple rules scaled up to a thousand
1:55:08
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 8 Sekunden
agents. Swarm robotics is still largely in the research phase, but the potential applications are tantalizing. Disaster
1:55:17
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 17 Sekunden
search and rescue using hundreds of small robots that can search rubble in parallel. Environmental monitoring using swarms of underwater or aerial drones.
1:55:28
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 28 Sekunden
and construction using teams of robots that collectively build structures too large for any individual machine. And
1:55:36
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 36 Sekunden
yet, despite all these creative alternative approaches, the investment pouring into humanoid robots right now
1:55:44
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 44 Sekunden
is staggering. Tesla announced Optimus in 2021 and by 2024 was demonstrating
1:55:52
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 52 Sekunden
units, sorting objects, and performing light factory work. Figure A startup
1:55:59
1 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
founded in 2022 has attracted billions in investment for its generalpurpose humanoid Agility Robotics Digit is being
1:56:08
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 8 Sekunden
tested in Amazon warehouses. Chinese companies like Unitry and Forier Intelligence are producing increasingly
1:56:17
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 17 Sekunden
capable and affordable humanoids. The bet these companies are making is that the combination of better hardware,
1:56:25
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 25 Sekunden
better simulation, better learning algorithms, and the knowledge embedded in large language models is about to
1:56:32
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
make generalpurpose humanoid robots practically viable. It's a big bet. It might be premature, but the trajectory
1:56:39
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 39 Sekunden
of the technology over the last few years gives it more credibility than at any previous point in history. There's
1:56:47
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 47 Sekunden
also the question of what happens to work, to jobs, to livelihoods, to the structure of economies. Remember the
1:56:56
1 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 56 Sekunden
Leon silkwavers who threw stones at Vocansong? That tension between automation and labor has never gone
1:57:04
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 4 Sekunden
away. It's just changed form. The introduction of industrial robots in the 1970s and 80s did eliminate certain
1:57:13
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 13 Sekunden
categories of jobs, particularly repetitive, dangerous ones in automanufacturing.
1:57:20
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 20 Sekunden
But it also created new jobs in robot programming, maintenance, and system integration. And it made many products
1:57:28
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 28 Sekunden
cheaper and higher quality. Economists debate the net effect endlessly. And the honest answer is that it depends on
1:57:37
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 37 Sekunden
context, on policy, on education systems, on how society manages the transition. The coming wave of more
1:57:46
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
capable, more generalpurpose robots will likely be more disruptive than anything the field has seen before, precisely
1:57:54
1 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
because these machines can perform a wider range of tasks. A traditional industrial robot replaces one specific
1:58:02
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
motion in a factory. A humanoid robot that can follow verbal instructions and
1:58:09
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 9 Sekunden
figure out novel tasks could potentially replace a much broader range of work.
1:58:15
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 15 Sekunden
Whether that's exciting or terrifying depends very much on who you are and where you sit in the economy. And the
1:58:24
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
people building these machines have a responsibility to think carefully about those implications. Not just after the fact, but in the design process itself.
1:58:35
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 35 Sekunden
And this brings us to where we should come to rest because we've been on quite a journey tonight. And there are things
1:58:42
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 42 Sekunden
worth sitting with as you drift off. We started 3,000 years ago with Greek myths about bronze giants and mechanical
1:58:51
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 51 Sekunden
handmaidaidens with the ancient human dream of building something that moves like us. We watched that dream evolve
1:58:59
1 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
through centuries through hero's programmable carts. Al Jazari's mechanical servants. Vocansson's gilded
1:59:07
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 7 Sekunden
duck. The Jacro's automata writing with quill pens. We saw the dream given a name robot from the Czech word for
1:59:16
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
forced labor and given a philosophical framework through Asimov's laws. We watched the first real industrial robots
1:59:24
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
transform manufacturing and we saw the terrible consequences when those machines operated without any awareness
1:59:32
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
of the people around them. We explored the staggering difficulty of giving robots perception. Why it took decades
1:59:40
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 40 Sekunden
to make a machine see what a baby sees in an instant. Why Moravex paradox means that the easy things in life are the
1:59:48
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 48 Sekunden
hardest things in engineering. We followed the philosophical wars between the deliberative camp and the reactive
1:59:55
1 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 55 Sekunden
camp between shaky and genis, between top-own planning and bottomup behavior.
2:00:01
2 Stunden, 1 Sekunde
We watched self-driving cars go from a laughingstock in the Mojave Desert to a technology on the cusp of deployment.
2:00:09
2 Stunden, 9 Sekunden
And we saw how machine learning, trial and error learning in simulation, imitation of human demonstration, the
2:00:17
2 Stunden, 17 Sekunden
vast common sense of language models is beginning to give robots something that looks from the outside remarkably like
2:00:24
2 Stunden, 24 Sekunden
understanding. And through all of it, one theme keeps recurring. The hardest
2:00:31
2 Stunden, 31 Sekunden
things for robots to do are the things we find easiest. Walking, reaching, grasping, seeing, understanding context.
2:00:43
2 Stunden, 43 Sekunden
The things we do without thinking, the things that feel like nothing are the products of hundreds of millions of
2:00:50
2 Stunden, 50 Sekunden
years of evolutionary optimization. and replicating them in silicon and steel is an engineering challenge that dwarfs
2:00:58
2 Stunden, 58 Sekunden
anything else we've attempted. Every robot that walks across a room is solving a problem harder than chess.
2:01:06
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 6 Sekunden
Every robot hand that picks up an egg without crushing it is performing a feat of control theory that would fill
2:01:13
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 13 Sekunden
textbooks. And yet here we are and the machines are getting better, not just incrementally. The convergence of
2:01:22
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 22 Sekunden
simulation, reinforcement learning, large language models, and cheaper, more capable hardware is producing progress
2:01:31
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 31 Sekunden
that would have seemed impossible a decade ago. Robots that learn from a handful of demonstrations rather than thousands.
2:01:40
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 40 Sekunden
Robots that understand spoken instructions and figure out how to carry them out in situations they've never
2:01:46
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 46 Sekunden
encountered. Robots that recover from mistakes gracefully, the way an animal would rather than freezing or failing.
2:01:55
2 Stunden, 1 Minute, 55 Sekunden
Think about the distance we've covered tonight. From Hero of Alexandria winding a string around axles to program a small
2:02:04
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 4 Sekunden
cart's path to reinforcement learning algorithms training in virtual worlds for thousands of simulated years. From
2:02:13
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 13 Sekunden
Vulcans's gilded duck mechanically chewing grain to robots that can hear a human say, "Clean up that mess and
2:02:21
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 21 Sekunden
figure out what to do about it." From the first Unimate blindly lifting castings in a New Jersey factory to
2:02:29
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 29 Sekunden
Atlas doing back flips and then delicately placing automotive parts in a fixture. The progression is staggering
2:02:36
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 36 Sekunden
and the acceleration is steeper than any other field of engineering I can think of. What took decades of progress in the
2:02:45
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 45 Sekunden
second half of the 20th century is now happening in years or even months.
2:02:50
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 50 Sekunden
Whether this leads to the gleaming humanoid future that the companies are betting on, or to something more modest
2:02:57
2 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 57 Sekunden
and more useful, specialized robots that do specific things extraordinarily well, nobody knows. But the dream persists.
2:03:08
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 8 Sekunden
It's the same dream that moved Heestus at his mythical forge and Al Jazari at his workbench and George Dval at that cocktail party in 1956.
2:03:20
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 20 Sekunden
The dream of extending our reach, of building something that acts in the world on our behalf, of creating from
2:03:28
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 28 Sekunden
inert materials something that moves with purpose.
2:03:33
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 33 Sekunden
And here's maybe the most remarkable thing about this story. Every breakthrough we've talked about tonight happened because someone refused to accept the limitations of their moment.
2:03:45
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 45 Sekunden
Gray Walter built thinking tortoises from war surplus electronics. Devol invented the robot arm without finishing
2:03:53
2 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 53 Sekunden
college. Brooks declared the entire AI establishment wrong and built insect robots to prove it. Sebastian Thrun
2:04:02
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
showed that a car could drive across a desert by itself. Jeffrey Hinton's stubborn faith in neural networks through decades when everyone said they
2:04:11
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 11 Sekunden
were a dead end eventually made robots that learned from experience possible.
2:04:16
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
In every case, progress came from someone who saw past what was currently possible and built toward what they
2:04:24
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 24 Sekunden
believed could be. So, as you lie there tonight, let the machines do what machines do. The warehouse robots are
2:04:32
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
sorting packages in the dark. The Mars rovers are crawling across alien landscapes. The surgical robots are
2:04:40
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 40 Sekunden
making movements too precise for human hands. The self-driving cars are navigating city streets, reading traffic
2:04:48
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 48 Sekunden
signals, yielding to pedestrians. The factory arms are welding, painting,
2:04:54
2 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
assembling one perfect motion after another after another. And somewhere in
2:05:01
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 1 Sekunde
a lab you've never heard of, a robot is picking up an object it's never seen before, using a skill it learned in
2:05:09
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 9 Sekunden
simulation, guided by a language models understanding of what humans need. And it's getting it right or almost right.
2:05:18
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 18 Sekunden
And either way, it's learning from the attempt. And there's something deeply human about this whole endeavor.
2:05:25
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 25 Sekunden
something that goes beyond the engineering and the economics and the policy debates. We build robots because
2:05:32
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 32 Sekunden
we're fascinated by movement, by agency, by the mystery of what it means to act in the world with purpose. Every child
2:05:41
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 41 Sekunden
who watches a windup toy walk across a table and laughs with delight is feeling the same wonder that moved Hero of
2:05:49
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 49 Sekunden
Alexandria to build his mechanical theaters. There's something magical about a thing that moves on its own, even when you know exactly how it works.
2:05:59
2 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 59 Sekunden
Especially when you know how it works.
2:06:02
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
Because then the magic isn't in the mystery. It's in the ingenuity. They don't dream. These machines, they don't
2:06:10
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 10 Sekunden
wonder. They don't get tired or lose hope. But they exist because we dream and wonder and refuse to give up. And
2:06:20
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 20 Sekunden
because for 3,000 years, the idea that we could build something that moves with purpose has been one of the most
2:06:28
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 28 Sekunden
persistent and beautiful obsessions of the human mind. That's what engineering is. Not just solving problems. Turning
2:06:37
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 37 Sekunden
the impossible into the inevitable, one stubborn step at a time. And right now, in this very moment, the feedback loops
2:06:46
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 46 Sekunden
are running. Sensors are feeding data to controllers. Controllers are sending commands to actuators.
2:06:54
2 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 54 Sekunden
Actuators are moving joints. Joints are moving the world. And in server farms somewhere, neural networks are
2:07:02
2 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 2 Sekunden
processing millions of simulated experiences, learning policies that will be downloaded into physical robots
2:07:09
2 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 9 Sekunden
tomorrow, giving them skills that didn't exist today. The loop keeps turning. The
2:07:16
2 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 16 Sekunden
machines keep learning. And you, you who are made of atoms, arranged by billions of years of evolution into a walking,
2:07:25
2 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 25 Sekunden
talking, dreaming feedback system more sophisticated than any robot ever built.
2:07:31
2 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 31 Sekunden
You can let all of that go for now. The story will still be here. The robots will still be learning. And in the
2:07:39
