0:000 SekundenSo, here's what I want to explore with you right now. The lamp beside you, the charger in the wall, the refrigerator
0:077 Sekundenhumming in the kitchen, all of it is being fed by spinning metal. Somewhere far away, a magnet is turning inside a
0:1616 Sekundenring of copper wire. And that motion is reaching all the way into your room. How does that actually work?
0:2525 SekundenHow does a turning shaft push electricity down a wire and into your wall? And there's a riddle hiding in
0:3232 Sekundenthere. A generator needs a magnet to make electricity, but the big ones make their magnet out of electricity.
0:4040 SekundenSo, which comes first? How does a machine like that ever wake up? Then there's a stranger question. Why does
0:4848 Sekundenswitching on your kettle make a machine hundreds of miles away just a little bit harder to turn?
0:5454 SekundenWho or what is pushing back? And the biggest one, how can thousands of generators owned by different companies
1:031 Minute, 3 Sekundendriven by water, steam, wind, and diesel all spin in exact step as if they were
1:091 Minute, 9 Sekundenbolted to one enormous shaft. Every light, every motor, and every charger in your home depends on that rhythm
1:171 Minute, 17 Sekundenholding. So what happens when one of those machines falls out of step? How close have we come to losing the whole
1:241 Minute, 24 Sekundenthing? And if it ever does go dark, how do you start a machine that size from nothing? Finally, a question about the
1:311 Minute, 31 Sekundenmachines that are supposed to save us when all of that fails? What happens when a backup generator, the kind that sits outside a hospital or behind a
1:401 Minute, 40 Sekundenhouse waiting for a storm, starts right up, runs perfectly, and delivers nothing at all? All of these questions lead to
1:481 Minute, 48 Sekundenone. What keeps this giant machine in step every second of every night, so that the light in your room never so
1:561 Minute, 56 Sekundenmuch as flickers? We're going to answer that slowly, piece by piece, starting with a wire and a magnet in a quiet room
2:042 Minuten, 4 Sekundenalmost 200 years ago. So, get comfortable. There's no rush tonight.
2:092 Minuten, 9 SekundenLet's begin in London in the late summer of 1831 in the basement laboratory of the Royal Institution.
2:202 Minuten, 20 SekundenThe man working there is Michael Faraday.
2:242 Minuten, 24 SekundenHe's about to turn 40, the son of a blacksmith, mostly self-taught, and he started his scientific life as a
2:342 Minuten, 34 Sekundenlab assistant, washing bottles and taking notes for someone more famous.
2:412 Minuten, 41 SekundenBy now, he's one of the most careful experimenters in Europe, and he's been chasing one particular idea for years.
2:522 Minuten, 52 SekundenThe idea came from something that happened back in 1820.
2:572 Minuten, 57 SekundenA Danish professor named Hance Christian Erstad had noticed that when he sent an electric current through a wire, a
3:063 Minuten, 6 Sekundencompass needle sitting nearby twitched and swung away from north.
3:133 Minuten, 13 SekundenThat was a genuine surprise.
3:163 Minuten, 16 SekundenElectricity and magnetism had always been treated as two separate curiosities.
3:233 Minuten, 23 SekundenOne made sparks and shocks. The other made needles point north and iron cling to a natural magnet.
3:333 Minuten, 33 SekundenUrstead's compass said they were connected. An electric current moving through a wire creates a magnetic field
3:403 Minuten, 40 Sekundenaround that wire. So Faraday asked the obvious next question.
3:463 Minuten, 46 SekundenIf electricity can make magnetism, can magnetism make electricity? It sounds simple. It took him about 10 years to
3:543 Minuten, 54 Sekundenanswer. Before we watch him do it, let's pause and get a clear picture of what a magnetic field actually is because
4:024 Minuten, 2 Sekundeneverything tonight rests on it. A magnet reaches out into the space around it.
4:074 Minuten, 7 SekundenYou can't see that reach, but you can map it. Take a small compass and set it near the north pole of a bar magnet. The
4:154 Minuten, 15 Sekundenneedle swings and settles pointing along some direction. Put a dot at the tip of the needle. Now slide the compass
4:224 Minuten, 22 Sekundenforward so the back of the needle sits on that dot and mark the new tip. Keep doing that dot after dot and you'll
4:304 Minuten, 30 Sekundentrace a curving line that leaves the north pole, arcs out through the air, and comes back into the south pole. Do it again from a slightly different
4:394 Minuten, 39 Sekundenstarting point and you get another curve nested beside the first.
4:454 Minuten, 45 SekundenThose curves are called field lines. You can see them all at once if you sprinkle
4:524 Minuten, 52 Sekundeniron filings on a sheet of paper laid over a magnet and tap the paper gently.
4:594 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe filings shuffle themselves into those same arching lines, crowded together near the poles and spreading out farther away.
5:105 Minuten, 10 SekundenTwo things about those lines matter for everything that follows.
5:165 Minuten, 16 SekundenFirst, outside the magnet, they always run from north to south.
5:235 Minuten, 23 SekundenSecond, where the lines are packed close together, the field is strong. And where they spread apart, it's weak.
5:335 Minuten, 33 SekundenAnd keep in mind that the paper only shows you a slice. The real field fills
5:395 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthe space all around the magnet in three dimensions, like the layers of an onion.
5:475 Minuten, 47 SekundenNow picture a loop of copper wire, just a ring sitting near that magnet.
5:545 Minuten, 54 SekundenSome of those invisible field lines pass through the open middle of the loop.
6:006 MinutenEngineers have a word for how much magnetism is threading through a loop like that. They call it magnetic flux.
6:096 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe easiest way to picture flux is to imagine counting the field lines that pass through the opening. More lines,
6:176 Minuten, 17 Sekundenmore flux. A bigger loop catches more lines, so it has more flux.
6:246 Minuten, 24 SekundenAnd the angle matters, too. If the loop faces the field squarely, so the lines pass straight through it, you get the most flux.
6:346 Minuten, 34 SekundenTilt the loop sideways and fewer lines pass through the opening. Turn it completely edge on and almost none do.
6:446 Minuten, 44 SekundenHold on to that picture. A loop with a certain number of field lines passing through it. Because this is the thing
6:516 Minuten, 51 SekundenFaraday was about to discover how to use.
6:556 Minuten, 55 SekundenOn August 29th, 1831, he took a ring of soft iron about 6 in across and wound
7:037 Minuten, 3 Sekundentwo separate coils of insulated wire around opposite sides of it. The two coils never touched each other. He
7:127 Minuten, 12 Sekundenconnected one coil to a battery through a switch. He connected the other coil to a sensitive needle that would move if any current flowed.
7:227 Minuten, 22 SekundenThen he closed the switch. The needle jumped. Then it settled back to zero.
7:307 Minuten, 30 SekundenEven though the battery was still connected and current was still flowing through the first coil.
7:377 Minuten, 37 SekundenWhen he opened the switch, the needle jumped again. this time in the opposite direction and then it settled back to zero again.
7:477 Minuten, 47 SekundenThat was the moment and it was also at first a puzzle because it wasn't what anyone expected.
7:577 Minuten, 57 SekundenA steady current in the first coil created a steady magnetic field in the iron ring and a steady magnetic field did nothing at all to the second coil.
8:098 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe needle only moved at the instant the field was appearing and at the instant it was disappearing.
8:178 Minuten, 17 SekundenFaraday had found the key.
8:208 Minuten, 20 SekundenA magnetic field that sits still does nothing. A magnetic field that changes pushes electricity through a wire.
8:318 Minuten, 31 SekundenWithin weeks, he confirmed it in an even simpler way. He took a coil of wire connected to his needle and pushed a bar
8:398 Minuten, 39 Sekundenmagnet into the middle of it. The needle swung. He held the magnet still inside the coil. The needle went back to zero.
8:498 Minuten, 49 SekundenHe pulled the magnet out. The needle swung the other way. In a push one way,
8:568 Minuten, 56 Sekundenstill nothing. Out. A push the other way.
9:019 Minuten, 1 SekundeThis is called electromagnetic induction. and it's the foundation of nearly every generator on Earth.
9:099 Minuten, 9 SekundenAcross the Atlantic, at almost the same time, an American teacher named Joseph Henry was finding the same effect in his own experiments in Albany, New York.
9:199 Minuten, 19 SekundenFaraday published first, so the discovery carries his name, but Henry's work was just as real. And today, the
9:279 Minuten, 27 Sekundenscientific unit of a coil's electrical inertia is called the Henry in his honor.
9:339 Minuten, 33 SekundenOnce again, an idea whose time had come appeared in two places at once. When the amount of magnetic flux passing through
9:419 Minuten, 41 Sekundena loop of wire changes, a voltage appears in that wire. If the wire forms a complete circuit, that voltage drives
9:499 Minuten, 49 Sekundena current. It's worth asking what that voltage actually is down at the level of the metal. Copper is a good conductor
9:589 Minuten, 58 Sekundenbecause each copper atom has one outer electron that isn't held very tightly.
10:0410 Minuten, 4 SekundenThose loose electrons drift around inside the metal like a crowd milling in a large hall. A changing magnetic field
10:1210 Minuten, 12 Sekundengives that whole crowd a shove in one direction along the wire. Shove them one way, then the other, back and forth, and
10:2010 Minuten, 20 Sekundenyou have alternating current. Keep shoving them the same way and you have direct current. Either way, what you're
10:2810 Minuten, 28 Sekundenreally doing is pushing on electrons that were already there sitting in the copper waiting to be moved. Now, three
10:3610 Minuten, 36 Sekundendetails about induction turn out to be enormously practical and every generator designer since Faraday has leaned on all three.
10:4510 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe first detail is speed. The faster the flux changes, the bigger the voltage. Push the magnet into the coil slowly and the needle barely moves.
10:5610 Minuten, 56 SekundenShove it in quickly and the needle kicks hard. The size of the push depends on how fast the magnetism through the loop is rising or falling.
11:0611 Minuten, 6 SekundenThe second detail is strength. A stronger magnet or a bigger loop that catches more field lines gives you more
11:1311 Minuten, 13 Sekundenflux to change in the first place and so more voltage. The third detail is turns.
11:2111 Minuten, 21 SekundenIf you move a magnet past a single straight wire, the voltage you get is tiny, a few millionths of a volt, far
11:2811 Minuten, 28 Sekundentoo small to light anything. But if you coil that wire around and around, so the same field lines pass through the loop
11:3711 Minuten, 37 Sekunden10 times or 100 times, each turn adds its own small push, and the pushes stack up.
11:4411 Minuten, 44 SekundenSeven turns give you roughly seven times the voltage of one. 100 turns give you roughly 100 times. Engineers call the
11:5311 Minuten, 53 Sekundentotal of all that flux, counted once for every turn it passes through, the flux linkage. It's the reason every generator
12:0112 Minuten, 1 Sekundeyou'll ever see is packed with coils of wire and never with single strands. So the recipe for a generator falls
12:0912 Minuten, 9 Sekundenstraight out of Faraday's basement. You need a strong magnetic field. You need coils with many turns. And you need to
12:1712 Minuten, 17 Sekundenkeep the magnetism through those coils changing over and over as quickly as you reasonably can. And there's one more
12:2612 Minuten, 26 Sekundencondition hiding in there that's easy to overlook. The magnet and the coil have to be moving relative to each other. The
12:3312 Minuten, 33 Sekundenmoment the motion stops, the change stops and so does the electricity.
12:3912 Minuten, 39 SekundenFaraday knew that a magnet pushed in and out of a coil by hand was never going to power anything. So in the autumn of that
12:4612 Minuten, 46 Sekundensame year, 1831, he tried something continuous. He mounted a copper disc on an axle so that
12:5412 Minuten, 54 Sekundenits edge passed between the poles of a large horseshoe magnet. He pressed one sliding contact against the rim of the
13:0113 Minuten, 1 Sekundedisc and another against the axle. Then he turned a crank. As long as the disc kept spinning, a steady current flowed
13:1013 Minuten, 10 Sekundenbetween the rim and the axle. The current flowed without a break for as long as someone kept turning.
13:1813 Minuten, 18 SekundenThat copper disc was the first electric generator. And honestly, it was a terrible one. The current was feeble.
13:2713 Minuten, 27 SekundenMost of the electricity it produced just swirled around inside the disc itself and turned into heat without ever reaching the wires.
13:3613 Minuten, 36 SekundenIt couldn't have lit a single modern light bulb. But the principle was proven. Turn something mechanical and
13:4413 Minuten, 44 Sekundenelectricity comes out. Keep turning and it keeps coming. There's a story repeated so often that nobody can quite
13:5313 Minuten, 53 Sekundenpin down where it began that a politician once asked Faraday what practical use his discovery could possibly have.
14:0114 Minuten, 1 SekundeFaraday is supposed to have answered that one day, sir, you may be able to tax it. It's probably invented, but it
14:1014 Minuten, 10 Sekundencaptures something true. Nobody in that basement could have imagined the scale of what would grow from that crank and that disc.
14:1914 Minuten, 19 SekundenOne thing was still missing. Faraday had shown that a spinning machine could produce electricity. But his disc gave a
14:2714 Minuten, 27 Sekundensmall, steady trickle, and it wasted most of its effort. The machines that came next took a different route.
14:3414 Minuten, 34 SekundenInstead of spinning a disc between the poles of a magnet, they spun the magnet itself past coils of wire so that north
14:4214 Minuten, 42 Sekundenand south poles swept by one after the other. And that simple change had a consequence that would shape the entire
14:4914 Minuten, 49 Sekundenelectrical world right down to the outlet next to your bed. Because when you spin a magnet past a coil, the
14:5714 Minuten, 57 Sekundenelectricity that comes out rocks back and forth. First one, then the other.
15:0315 Minuten, 3 SekundenAnd to understand why and why that turned out to be exactly what we needed, we have to follow the magnet as it
15:0915 Minuten, 9 Sekundenturns. Less than a year after Faraday's disc in 1832, a young Parisian instrument maker named Hippoite Pixie
15:1815 Minuten, 18 Sekundenbuilt a machine that looks to modern eyes almost like a toy. It had a heavy horseshoe magnet mounted on a spindle
15:2715 Minuten, 27 Sekundenwith its poles pointing upward. Just above the poles, held still, sat two
15:3315 Minuten, 33 Sekundencoils of wire wound on iron cores. You turned a hand crank, a set of gears spun
15:4015 Minuten, 40 Sekundenthe magnet, and its north and south poles swept past the coils over and over.
15:4915 Minuten, 49 SekundenPixie machine did something Faraday's disc never did. It produced a current that swung back and forth. And when
15:5815 Minuten, 58 SekundenPixie showed his machine to other scientists, they weren't entirely pleased with that. At the time,
16:0516 Minuten, 5 Sekundenelectricity meant batteries, and batteries gave a steady current in one direction.
16:1216 Minuten, 12 SekundenEverything people knew how to do with electricity like splitting water or plating metal was built around that one-way flow.
16:2116 Minuten, 21 SekundenSo reportedly at the suggestion of the physicist Andre Marie Aair, Pixie added
16:2916 Minuten, 29 Sekundena clever rotating switch to the shaft which flipped the connections every half turn so that the output always came out
16:3816 Minuten, 38 Sekundenpointing the same way. That switch was called a commutator and it turned his alternating current into a bumpy sort of
16:4516 Minuten, 45 Sekundendirect current. For the next 50 years, a lot of engineering effort went into exactly that. Taking the natural back
16:5316 Minuten, 53 Sekundenand forth output of a spinning machine and straightening it out. But the back and forth is the natural language of a
17:0017 Minutenspinning magnet. So, let's slow down and watch where it comes from. Imagine a single coil of wire fixed in place like
17:0817 Minuten, 8 Sekundena window frame. In front of it, a magnet spins on an axle. Let's follow the north pole. As the north pole swings toward
17:1817 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthe coil, more and more field lines pass through the window. The flux is growing and it's growing fast. So, the electrons
17:2617 Minuten, 26 Sekundenin the copper get pushed in one direction. When the north pole is pointing straight at the coil, the flux
17:3317 Minuten, 33 Sekundenis at its peak. For a brief instant, it stops growing. It's at the top of the hill. And at that instant, the push on
17:4217 Minuten, 42 Sekundenthe electrons drops to zero. Because remember, only a changing flux creates a voltage. Then the north pole swings past
17:5017 Minuten, 50 Sekundenand away. The flux starts shrinking. Now the electrons get pushed in the opposite direction. Half a turn later, the south
17:5817 Minuten, 58 Sekundenpole comes around and the field lines pass through the window the other way. The same pattern repeats, but flipped.
18:0518 Minuten, 5 SekundenPush, pause, push back. If you draw the voltage over time, you get a smooth rolling wave. It rises, peaks, falls
18:1518 Minuten, 15 Sekundenthrough zero, sinks to a low point, and rises again. That shape is called a sine wave, and it's the natural signature of
18:2318 Minuten, 23 Sekundenanything that turns in a circle. The electricity in your walls follows that same wave, rising and falling, reversing
18:3018 Minuten, 30 Sekundendirection many times every second. The number of complete waves per second is called the frequency and it's measured
18:3718 Minuten, 37 Sekundenin hertz. The unit is named after Heinrich Hertz, a German physicist who
18:4418 Minuten, 44 Sekundendecades later proved that electric and magnetic waves could travel through empty space. The discovery that eventually gave us radio.
18:5518 Minuten, 55 SekundenOne hertz means one complete wave each second. In North America, the grid runs at 60 hertz. In most of the rest of the
19:0419 Minuten, 4 Sekundenworld, it runs at 50. That means the electricity feeding your lamp reverses direction
19:1219 Minuten, 12 Sekunden120 times every second or 100 times in countries that use 50 hertz.
19:1819 Minuten, 18 SekundenThe light doesn't visibly flicker because the changes are far too fast for your eye to follow.
19:2519 Minuten, 25 SekundenNow, let's give the parts of a generator their proper names because they'll be with us all night. The part that spins
19:3319 Minuten, 33 Sekundenis called the rotor. The part that stays still is called the stator.
19:4019 Minuten, 40 SekundenYou can put the magnets on either one and the coils on the other. It works both ways because all that matters is
19:4819 Minuten, 48 Sekundenthat one is moving relative to the other. But in almost every serious generator, the coils that deliver the
19:5519 Minuten, 55 Sekundenpower sit in the stator and the magnet spins inside it. There's a practical reason for that. The coils are where the
20:0520 Minuten, 5 Sekundenbig current comes out and the big current has to reach thick, heavy cables.
20:1120 Minuten, 11 SekundenIt's far easier to bolt those cables to something that sits still than to something spinning. One modern builder
20:1920 Minuten, 19 Sekundenwho made his own generator from scratch in his workshop put it simply. He mounted the magnets on the rotor and the
20:2620 Minuten, 26 Sekundencoils on the stator because wiring coils is much easier when they don't move. So,
20:3320 Minuten, 33 Sekundenpicture a basic generator, a round frame of copper coils on the outside, a magnet spinning in the middle.
20:4120 Minuten, 41 SekundenEach time a north pole passes a coil and then a south pole passes it, that coil produces one complete wave. And this
20:5120 Minuten, 51 Sekundenbrings us to a real problem, the first of many that engineers had to solve. If you want 60 waves per second and your
20:5920 Minuten, 59 Sekundenspinning magnet has one north pole and one south pole, then the magnet has to make one full turn for every wave. 60
21:0821 Minuten, 8 Sekundenturns per second. That's 3,600 turns per minute. That's fast. But big
21:1521 Minuten, 15 Sekundensteam turbines can spin that fast, and many of them do. But what if your source
21:2221 Minuten, 22 Sekundenof motion is slow? A water wheel, a big diesel engine, a wind turbine. The answer is to give the rotor more poles.
21:3221 Minuten, 32 SekundenInstead of one north and one south, arrange two norths and two souths around the rotor, alternating as you go. Now,
21:4121 Minuten, 41 Sekundenin one full turn, each coil sees north, south, north, south. That's two complete
21:4821 Minuten, 48 Sekundenwaves per turn. So, the rotor only needs to spin half as fast. Four poles need
21:5521 Minuten, 55 Sekunden1,800 revolutions per minute for 60 hertz. Add more poles and the required speed drops further. A slow water
22:0422 Minuten, 4 Sekundenturbine at a dam might drive a generator with dozens of poles turning just a couple of hundred times a minute and still produce exactly 60 hertz.
22:1722 Minuten, 17 SekundenThis relationship is so central that the classic engineering textbooks like Steven Chapman's electric machinery
22:2522 Minuten, 25 Sekundenfundamentals put it near the very start of their discussion of generators.
22:3122 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe frequency you get depends on two things only.
22:3622 Minuten, 36 SekundenHow fast the shaft turns and how many poles the rotor has.
22:4222 Minuten, 42 SekundenSpin faster, higher frequency. add poles higher frequency.
22:4922 Minuten, 49 SekundenAnd that means that if you want the frequency to stay fixed at exactly 60, the shaft speed has to stay fixed, too.
22:5922 Minuten, 59 SekundenTuck that away. It's going to matter enormously.
23:0523 Minuten, 5 SekundenLet's look at a machine where this problem is right out in the open. A large wind turbine.
23:1223 Minuten, 12 SekundenThe blades of a big wind turbine are enormous, often longer than a football field is wide, and they turn slowly,
23:2223 Minuten, 22 Sekundenaround 18 times a minute. That's about one lazy revolution every 3 seconds.
23:3023 Minuten, 30 SekundenThey can't spin much faster than that because the tips of the blades are already moving far faster than the hub.
23:3923 Minuten, 39 SekundenIf the blades turned quickly, the tips would approach the speed of sound and the forces would tear the blades apart.
23:4823 Minuten, 48 SekundenSo, the blades turn slowly with enormous twisting force.
23:5423 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd if you've ever wondered why nearly every big turbine has exactly three blades, the reason is a balance between pull and drag.
24:0424 Minuten, 4 SekundenEach blade generates lift which turns the rotor, but each blade also creates drag which holds it back.
24:1424 Minuten, 14 SekundenEngineers have tested this with small model turbines, adding blades one at a time and measuring the voltage.
24:2224 Minuten, 22 SekundenWith one blade, the rotor is slow, wobbly, and won't even start by itself.
24:2924 Minuten, 29 SekundenTwo blades start on their own and produce far more.
24:3424 Minuten, 34 SekundenThree produce a little more still and are much harder to stop because they catch more of the wind's energy.
24:4324 Minuten, 43 SekundenFour and five add only a little. And by six, the voltage actually starts to fall
24:5024 Minuten, 50 Sekundenas the extra drag eats up the extra lift. Three blades turn out to be very stable. capture nearly as much energy as
24:5924 Minuten, 59 Sekundenany other choice and cost the least to build. But the generator needs speed.
25:0825 Minuten, 8 SekundenSo between the blades and the generator sits a gearbox, typically with three stages of gears,
25:1625 Minuten, 16 Sekundenand it multiplies the speed by roughly 100.
25:2125 Minuten, 21 Sekunden18 revolutions per minute at the hub becomes about 1,800 at the generator.
25:2825 Minuten, 28 SekundenAnd 1,800 for a four-pole generator is exactly what you need for 60 Hz.
25:3625 Minuten, 36 SekundenThe gearbox trades the blades slow, heavy twist for the fast, light spin that the generator wants.
25:4625 Minuten, 46 SekundenNow, let's add one more layer because the power in your walls and almost all the power on the grid comes from three sets of coils working together.
25:5925 Minuten, 59 SekundenGo back to the workshop builder for a moment. When he laid out the coils for his homemade generator, he made nine of them and arranged them in a ring.
26:1226 Minuten, 12 SekundenThen he split them into three groups of three.
26:1626 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd each group became what's called a phase. The coils of each phase were spaced around the ring so that the
26:2426 Minuten, 24 Sekundenspinning magnets reach them at slightly different moments.
26:2926 Minuten, 29 SekundenSo instead of one wave, the generator produces three waves. Each one a third of a cycle behind the one before
26:3726 Minuten, 37 Sekunden[clears throat] it. When the first wave is at its peak, the second is still climbing and the third is on its way down. They take turns.
26:4926 Minuten, 49 SekundenWhy go to that trouble? Because of how the power arrives with a single wave.
26:5626 Minuten, 56 SekundenThe power delivered rises and falls, dropping to nothing twice in every cycle. Like pedalling a bicycle with one leg.
27:0627 Minuten, 6 SekundenWith three waves overlapping, one of them is always near its peak. And the total power flowing out stays almost
27:1427 Minuten, 14 Sekundenperfectly smooth. It's like pedalling with three legs, each one pushing at a
27:2127 Minuten, 21 Sekundendifferent moment. Big motors run more smoothly on three-phase power. The generator itself runs more smoothly, and the wires carry power more efficiently.
27:3327 Minuten, 33 SekundenTo connect his three phases, the builder joined the start of each phase to its own terminal and tied the far ends of
27:4127 Minuten, 41 Sekundenall three phases together at a single shared point. That arrangement is called a star or a Y because of its shape on a diagram.
27:5227 Minuten, 52 SekundenThe shared center point is called the neutral.
27:5627 Minuten, 56 SekundenThree terminals for the three phases plus a neutral in the middle. That's three-phase power. The same basic
28:0528 Minuten, 5 Sekundenarrangement that runs through power plants and transmission lines around the world.
28:1128 Minuten, 11 SekundenLet's take stock. We have a spinning magnet. We have coils around it. We have a wave coming out whose frequency
28:2028 Minuten, 20 Sekundendepends on the speed of the shaft and the number of poles. We have three phases for smooth power.
28:2828 Minuten, 28 SekundenAnd yet, if you built a generator exactly like this using ordinary permanent magnets and connected it to
28:3528 Minuten, 35 Sekundenyour house, you would quickly run into trouble. Because a permanent magnet has one fixed strength, and a fixed strength
28:4528 Minuten, 45 Sekundenmeans a fixed voltage. And as we're about to see, a generator that can't adjust its own voltage is almost useless.
28:5428 Minuten, 54 SekundenBut before we can understand why and what engineers did about it, we need to look at the part of the generator you'd
29:0229 Minuten, 2 Sekundennever notice if you opened one up. The empty space, the thin gap of air between
29:0929 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthe part that spins and the part that stands still.
29:1329 Minuten, 13 SekundenIf you could slice a generator in half and look at the cross-section, most of what you'd see would be iron and copper.
29:2129 Minuten, 21 SekundenA ring of iron in the stator slotted all the way around with copper coils tucked into the slots.
29:2929 Minuten, 29 SekundenA heavy iron rotor in the middle carrying its magnetic poles.
29:3429 Minuten, 34 SekundenAnd between the two, a sliver of empty air so narrow you might not notice it at
29:4129 Minuten, 41 Sekundenall. That sliver turns out to be one of the most important parts of the machine.
29:4829 Minuten, 48 SekundenTo see why, we need to think about magnetism the way engineers do as something that flows around a path.
29:5729 Minuten, 57 SekundenRemember the iron filings arching from north to south. Those field lines always form closed loops. They leave the north
30:0630 Minuten, 6 Sekundenpole, travel around and return to the south pole. And inside the magnet they continue from south back to north
30:1430 Minuten, 14 Sekundencompleting the circle. There are no loose ends. So every magnetic field is really a loop, a circuit. A bit like the
30:2230 Minuten, 22 Sekundencircuit an electric current follows around a wire. And just as electric current prefers to flow through copper
30:2930 Minuten, 29 Sekundeninstead of through rubber, magnetic flux strongly prefers some materials over others. It moves through iron with
30:3730 Minuten, 37 Sekundenremarkable ease. It moves through air only grudgingly. Engineers call the difficulty a material puts up against
30:4430 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmagnetic flux its reluctance, which is a nicely descriptive word. Iron has very low reluctance. Air has very high
30:5330 Minuten, 53 Sekundenreluctance. How high? For the kinds of iron used in generators, air can be thousands of times more reluctant than
31:0031 Minutenthe iron around it. Textbooks like Chapman's build this into a direct comparison with an electric circuit. In
31:0831 Minuten, 8 Sekundenan electric circuit, a battery pushes current through a resistance. In a magnetic circuit, a coil carrying current pushes flux through a
31:1631 Minuten, 16 Sekundenreluctance. The push from the coil depends on how many turns it has and how much current flows through them. The
31:2431 Minuten, 24 Sekundenflux you get depends on how much reluctance stands in the way. Now, picture the flux path in a generator.
31:3331 Minuten, 33 SekundenIt starts at a north pole on the rotor, jumps across the air gap into the stator, travels through the stator's iron, jumps back across the air gap into
31:4231 Minuten, 42 Sekundena south pole on the rotor, and returns through the rotor's iron. Most of that journey measured in inches, is through
31:5031 Minuten, 50 Sekundeniron. Only a tiny fraction of it is through air. But because air is so much more reluctant, that tiny fraction of
31:5831 Minuten, 58 Sekundenair can account for most of the total resistance in the entire loop. An everyday image helps. Imagine a long
32:0632 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwide highway with one short stretch where it narrows to a single muddy lane.
32:1232 Minuten, 12 SekundenIt doesn't matter how smooth the rest of the road is. The traffic jam happens at the mud. In a generator, the air gap is
32:2032 Minuten, 20 Sekundenthe mud, and that means a small change in the width of the gap can make a big change in how much flux gets through.
32:2732 Minuten, 27 SekundenThe workshop builder we met in the last chapter saw this with his own eyes and in numbers. He was building what's
32:3432 Minuten, 34 Sekundencalled an axial flux generator. Instead of a magnet spinning inside a ring of coils, his design looked more like a
32:4232 Minuten, 42 Sekundensandwich. In the middle sat a flat round stator about a foot wide holding his coils all cast together in a solid disc
32:5032 Minuten, 50 Sekundenof resin. On either side of it spun a steel disc covered in magnets. The magnets faced the coils across the gap
32:5932 Minuten, 59 Sekundenand the field lines ran straight across parallel to the axle. The steel discs behind the magnets were there on
33:0633 Minuten, 6 Sekundenpurpose. Engineers call a piece of steel used this way a back iron. When field lines leave the back of one magnet, they
33:1433 Minuten, 14 Sekundenneed a way to get over to the neighboring magnet and complete their loop. Without the steel, they'd have to wander through the air, and much of
33:2233 Minuten, 22 Sekundentheir strength would be lost. The steel gives them an easy road, a low reluctance path, so more of the flux
33:3033 Minuten, 30 Sekundenends up where it's useful, pushed out through the front of the magnets and into the coils.
33:3733 Minuten, 37 SekundenHe also used two rotors, one on each side of the stator with the magnets lined up so that each north pole on one rotor faced a south pole on the other.
33:4933 Minuten, 49 SekundenThat way, the field lines were drawn straight through the coils from one side to the other instead of leaking off
33:5733 Minuten, 57 Sekundensideways and curling back without ever touching the copper.
34:0234 Minuten, 2 SekundenField lines that miss the coils do no useful work. and engineers call them leakage.
34:1034 Minuten, 10 SekundenThe second rotor nearly doubled the flux passing through the coils. Then he went one step further.
34:1834 Minuten, 18 SekundenBetween the main magnets, he added smaller magnets turned on their sides pointing along the disc. That
34:2634 Minuten, 26 Sekundenarrangement is called a hellback array and its effect is to strengthen the field on the side facing the coils while
34:3434 Minuten, 34 Sekundenweakening it on the back side. It's another way of sending the magnetism where you want it and nowhere else.
34:4234 Minuten, 42 SekundenEvery one of those choices, the back iron, the second rotor, the sideways magnets, serves one goal. Get as much
34:5134 Minuten, 51 Sekundenflux as possible through the copper and waste as little as possible along the way.
34:5834 Minuten, 58 SekundenBut the most striking lesson came from the gap itself. His target was to set the magnets less than a tenth of an inch
35:0535 Minuten, 5 Sekundenfrom the face of the stator, about the thickness of two credit cards, just enough room for the rotor to spin freely without scraping.
35:1635 Minuten, 16 SekundenWhile he was assembling the machine, he ran a little experiment. With one rotor set close and the other still far away,
35:2535 Minuten, 25 Sekundenhe gave the shaft a slow turn by hand and watched the meter. It peaked at about 5 1/2 V. Then he closed the gap on
35:3535 Minuten, 35 Sekundenthe far side and gave it another hand spin.
35:3935 Minuten, 39 Sekunden11 V. The magnets, the coils, and the speed were all unchanged.
35:4635 Minuten, 46 SekundenThe only difference was a fraction of an inch of empty air, and the voltage doubled. That's the air gap at work, the
35:5435 Minuten, 54 Sekundenmuddy lane on the highway. When you narrow it, the whole magnetic circuit opens up. More flux flows through the
36:0236 Minuten, 2 Sekundencoils and every other number in the machine improves.
36:0736 Minuten, 7 SekundenHis story has another lesson in it about the coils.
36:1136 Minuten, 11 SekundenEach of his coils was wound in a kind of rounded wedge shape like a slice of pie with its tip cut off. He made the long
36:2036 Minuten, 20 Sekundenstraight sides of each wedge as long and straight as he could and kept the curved ends short.
36:2836 Minuten, 28 SekundenThe reason is that almost all of the voltage is produced in those straight sections where the magnets sweep
36:3536 Minuten, 35 Sekundendirectly across the wire. The curved ends where the wire turns around add almost nothing and they still add
36:4336 Minuten, 43 Sekundenlength, weight, and resistance. So a good coil puts as much of its copper as possible where the magnets cross it. He
36:5236 Minuten, 52 Sekundenwas careful in another way too. Once the coils were sealed in resin, there would be no going back. So he tested them
37:0037 Minutenfirst. He checked that each phase's coils were connected to each other and to nothing else.
37:0737 Minuten, 7 SekundenThen he taped a couple of magnets to a bolt, spun it in a drill just above the coils and measured the voltage from each phase in turn.
37:1837 Minuten, 18 SekundenAll three gave about the same reading. Only then did he pour the resin.
37:2537 Minuten, 25 SekundenAnd then there's the question of how many coils to use and how many magnets.
37:3137 Minuten, 31 SekundenHis first design used nine coils and 10 pairs of magnets.
37:3737 Minuten, 37 SekundenThat ratio between coils and poles turns out to matter a great deal.
37:4337 Minuten, 43 SekundenSome combinations like 12 coils with 10 poles or 12 with 16 work beautifully.
37:5237 Minuten, 52 SekundenOthers, like 12 coils with 12 poles, work poorly.
37:5737 Minuten, 57 SekundenWhen the magnets line up with the coils in an unlucky pattern, the voltages in different coils can partly cancel each
38:0538 Minuten, 5 Sekundenother out and you get lower, rougher power.
38:0938 Minuten, 9 SekundenHis first version disappointed him. On its first test, it produced about 5
38:1638 Minuten, 16 Sekundenvolts at 500 revolutions per minute. His goal was about 50 volts at 3,000 revolutions per minute. And the numbers weren't quite getting there.
38:2738 Minuten, 27 SekundenSo he rebuilt it. He went to 16 poles on the rotors and 12 coils on the stator.
38:3438 Minuten, 34 SekundenHe reshaped the coils to make the straight legs straighter. He added about half again as many turns of wire. He
38:4238 Minuten, 42 Sekundenmade the stator a little thinner so the rotors could sit closer together.
38:4838 Minuten, 48 SekundenThe new version hit 40 volts at 600 revolutions per minute. At 1,000 revolutions per minute, it produced 52
38:5638 Minuten, 56 Sekundenvolts. His original goal had been about 50 volts at 3,000 revolutions per minute. He'd overshot his target by
39:0539 Minuten, 5 Sekundenroughly three times, and that success quietly exposed the central weakness of the whole design.
39:1239 Minuten, 12 SekundenSpinning at full speed, this machine would now produce well over 150 volts.
39:1839 Minuten, 18 SekundenThat was far too much for the battery charging equipment he planned to connect it to. High voltage spikes could damage it. He'd need to dial it back somehow.
39:2839 Minuten, 28 SekundenBut how do you dial back a permanent magnet? There's one more thing hiding in all that iron, and it connects right
39:3539 Minuten, 35 Sekundenback to Faraday's copper disc. Remember how most of the disc's electricity just swirled around inside the disc and
39:4339 Minuten, 43 Sekundenturned into heat? The same thing tries to happen inside a generator's iron.
39:4839 Minuten, 48 SekundenIron conducts electricity. So when the magnetic field sweeping through it keeps changing, it induces little whirlpools of current inside the iron itself.
39:5939 Minuten, 59 SekundenEngineers call them eddy currents after the swirls of water you see behind a rock in a stream. They do no useful work
40:0840 Minuten, 8 Sekundenat all. They just heat the iron and waste energy.
40:1240 Minuten, 12 SekundenThe cure described in every textbook on electrical machines is to build the iron from thin sheets instead of solid blocks.
40:2140 Minuten, 21 SekundenEach sheet is coated with a thin layer of insulation, and the sheets are stacked together like the pages of a book. The magnetic flux can still run
40:3040 Minuten, 30 Sekundeneasily along the sheets, but the whirlpools of current can't cross from one sheet to the next, so they stay small and weak.
40:3940 Minuten, 39 SekundenIf you ever see the stator of a large generator or motor up close, you'll notice its iron core is made of hundreds of these thin layers pressed together.
40:5040 Minuten, 50 SekundenHeat is the enemy everywhere in these machines, in the iron and in the copper alike. The workshop builder knew that
40:5740 Minuten, 57 Sekundenhis coils would warm up as current flowed through them. So when he cast his sta in resin, he mixed in a fine powder
41:0441 Minuten, 4 Sekundenof the same material used for sand blasting because it conducts heat much better than resin alone. That helped
41:1241 Minuten, 12 Sekundencarry the heat out of the coils and away so the machine wouldn't cook itself while it ran. Let's put what we've
41:1941 Minuten, 19 Sekundenlearned into one relationship, the way Chapman's textbook does. The voltage a generator produces depends on two things
41:2741 Minuten, 27 Sekundenmultiplied together. how much magnetic flux crosses the gap and how fast the rotor spins. Double the flux and you roughly double the voltage.
41:3841 Minuten, 38 SekundenDouble the speed and you roughly double the voltage. There are other factors built into the design like the number of turns and the arrangement of the coils.
41:5041 Minuten, 50 SekundenBut once a machine is built those are fixed. In daily life a generator has
41:5641 Minuten, 56 Sekundenjust two knobs. speed and flux. Now, remember what we learned in the last chapter. Speed also sets the frequency.
42:0642 Minuten, 6 SekundenIf a generator is feeding a home or a grid that expects 60 hertz, the shaft has to turn at one steady speed. Change
42:1542 Minuten, 15 Sekundenthe speed and the frequency changes along with the voltage. So, the speed knob is effectively locked. That leaves
42:2442 Minuten, 24 Sekundenflux. And with permanent magnets, the flux is set on the day you glue them in.
42:3042 Minuten, 30 SekundenYou can't turn it up or down. Why would you ever need to? Because a generator's voltage doesn't stay put on its own.
42:3842 Minuten, 38 SekundenWhen you connect a heavy load and draw a big current, some voltage gets lost inside the machine itself. In the
42:4742 Minuten, 47 Sekundenresistance of the copper, and in magnetic effects, we'll meet later.
42:5342 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe more current you draw, the more the voltage sags. A generator that makes exactly the right voltage with nothing
43:0243 Minuten, 2 Sekundenplugged in might sag noticeably when you run a refrigerator, a heater, and a pump
43:0843 Minuten, 8 Sekundenall at once. A mechanic on a small farm who had to repair a generator himself once put the problem in the plainest
43:1743 Minuten, 17 Sekundenterms. If you just use permanent magnets, you might end up with a generator that puts out 108 volts no
43:2543 Minuten, 25 Sekundenmatter what. And there's nothing you can do about it. What you want is 120 every
43:3243 Minuten, 32 Sekundentime under any load. To get that, you need a magnet whose strength you can adjust. A magnet with a dial on it. And that kind of magnet exists.
43:4543 Minuten, 45 SekundenWe met its ancestor at the start of the night in Urstead's twitching compass. A current flowing through a coil of wire
43:5243 Minuten, 52 Sekundencreates a magnetic field. More current, stronger field. Less current, weaker
43:5943 Minuten, 59 Sekundenfield. Wind a coil around an iron core and you have an electromagnet. A magnet you can turn up, turn down, or switch
44:0744 Minuten, 7 Sekundenoff entirely. So, the answer seems simple. replace the permanent magnet on the rotor with an electromagnet and
44:1544 Minuten, 15 Sekundencontrol its current. But the moment you do that, you walk straight into a riddle. An electromagnet needs
44:2244 Minuten, 22 Sekundenelectricity to become a magnet. And the whole point of the generator is to make electricity. So where does the rotor's first electricity come from before the
44:3144 Minuten, 31 Sekundengenerator has made any? It sounds like a question with no answer. And for a while in the 1860s, the brightest electrical mines in Europe didn't have one.
44:4244 Minuten, 42 SekundenElectromagnets were already old news by the time generators became serious machines. In 1825,
44:4944 Minuten, 49 Sekundenan English experimentter named William Sturgeon wrapped wire around a horseshoe of iron, [clears throat]
44:5744 Minuten, 57 Sekundenran a current through it, and found it could lift several times its own weight.
45:0445 Minuten, 4 SekundenA few years later, the American physicist Joseph Henry wound his magnets
45:1045 Minuten, 10 Sekundenwith many turns of insulated wire and built electromagnets that could lift a ton or more.
45:1945 Minuten, 19 SekundenSo, the idea of a magnet you could switch on with a current was well known.
45:2545 Minuten, 25 SekundenBut for the first few decades, generators mostly used permanent magnets, the kind made of hardened steel.
45:3545 Minuten, 35 SekundenThese machines were called magnetos, and they had real problems.
45:4245 Minuten, 42 SekundenSteel magnets of that era were heavy and weak. To get a decent amount of power,
45:4945 Minuten, 49 Sekundenyou needed rows and rows of them. And a machine could end up enormous for the modest current it delivered.
45:5845 Minuten, 58 SekundenThe magnets also lost strength over time, especially when they were jarred or heated.
46:0646 Minuten, 6 SekundenAnd as we've seen, there was no way to adjust them. So inventors began asking whether the permanent magnets could be replaced with electromagnets.
46:1646 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe trouble was obvious. An electromagnet needs a steady current, and in those days that meant a battery.
46:2446 Minuten, 24 SekundenBatteries of the 1850s and60s were messy jars of acid and metal plates. They wore
46:3146 Minuten, 31 Sekundenout. They needed constant attention, and they couldn't supply the kind of power a large electromagnet required for hours at a time.
46:4046 Minuten, 40 SekundenIn the mid 1860s, a Manchester engineer named Henry Wild found a clever halfway solution. He used a small permanent
46:5046 Minuten, 50 Sekundenmagnet generator to produce a current, then fed that current into the electromagnets of a much bigger generator. The little machine excited
46:5946 Minuten, 59 Sekundenthe big one. The big one with its powerful electromagnets produced far more electricity than the little one
47:0647 Minuten, 6 Sekundenever could. Wild's machines were strong enough to power search lights, and they astonished people. In a paper presented
47:1347 Minuten, 13 Sekundento the Royal Society in 1866, Wild described how an indefinitely small amount of magnetism could, with enough
47:2147 Minuten, 21 Sekundenmechanical effort, call up an enormous amount of it. He was standing right at the edge of the answer. The next step
47:2847 Minuten, 28 Sekundenwas to wonder whether the big machine could simply feed its own electromagnets, take a little of the current it produces, loop it back into its own
47:3747 Minuten, 37 Sekundenfield coils, and let it be its own exciter. No battery, no little helper machine. That step was taken by several
47:4547 Minuten, 45 Sekundenpeople at nearly the same time, which is often a sign that an idea's moment has arrived. In December of 1866, an English
47:5447 Minuten, 54 Sekundentelegraph engineer named Samuel Alfred Vley applied for a patent on a generator that fed its own field. In January of
48:0248 Minuten, 2 Sekunden1867, Verer Seamans described the same principle in Berlin. And on February 14th, 1867,
48:1048 Minuten, 10 Sekundenat a meeting of the Royal Society in London, two papers describing the self-feeding generator were read on the
48:1748 Minuten, 17 Sekundenvery same day. One was by Charles Wheatstone. The other on behalf of his brother Verer was presented by William Seamans.
48:2748 Minuten, 27 SekundenYears later, historians dug up an even earlier patent by a Danish inventor named Saurin Horth from the 1850s that
48:3548 Minuten, 35 Sekundenhad described much the same thing and been forgotten.
48:3948 Minuten, 39 SekundenVer Seammons gave the new kind of machine a name. He called it a dynamo electric machine. Soon everyone was just
48:4848 Minuten, 48 Sekundencalling it a dynamo. The name came from the Greek word for power and it fit. For the first time, the amount of
48:5748 Minuten, 57 Sekundenelectricity a machine could make was limited mainly by how hard you could turn it and how much iron and copper you could afford rather than by how many
49:0649 Minuten, 6 Sekundenheavy steel magnets you could stack together. Seamans understood right away what that meant. He wrote that the
49:1349 Minuten, 13 Sekundentechnology now had the means to produce electric currents of unlimited strength cheaply and conveniently wherever
49:2049 Minuten, 20 Sekundenmechanical power was available. That was a large claim for 1867 and within 20
49:2749 Minuten, 27 Sekundenyears it had come true. But the idea still seems to contain an impossibility.
49:3249 Minuten, 32 SekundenHow does a machine feed its own magnet when it can't make any electricity until its magnet is already working?
49:4049 Minuten, 40 SekundenImagine it plainly. You have a stator full of coils and an iron rotor wound with its own coil, the field coil. If
49:4949 Minuten, 49 Sekundenthere's truly no magnetism anywhere, you can spin that rotor as fast as you like and nothing will happen. The farm mechanic described this exact situation.
49:5949 Minuten, 59 SekundenA coil spinning inside a coil with no magnetic field anywhere produces no voltage at all. It doesn't even resist
50:0750 Minuten, 7 Sekundenbeing turned because there's nothing magnetic for anything to push against. It just spins, silent and useless.
50:1650 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe answer lies in a quiet property of iron. Inside a piece of iron are countless microscopic regions, each one
50:2550 Minuten, 25 Sekundena tiny magnet with its own north and south. Normally, they point in every direction at random, canceling each
50:3450 Minuten, 34 Sekundenother out. So the iron as a whole isn't magnetic.
50:3850 Minuten, 38 SekundenWhen you pass a current through a coil around the iron, those tiny regions swing around and line up and the iron becomes strongly magnetic.
50:5050 Minuten, 50 SekundenWhen you switch off the current, most of them tumble back into disorder, but not all of them. A small fraction
50:5850 Minuten, 58 Sekundenstay lined up as if the iron still half remembers the magnet it used to be.
51:0551 Minuten, 5 SekundenThat lingering trace is called residual magnetism.
51:0951 Minuten, 9 SekundenIt's weak, only a sliver of the iron's full strength, but it's there, and it's enough to start everything. It unfolds like this. You begin spinning the rotor.
51:2351 Minuten, 23 SekundenIts iron holds only that faint residual magnetism, but faint is more than zero.
51:3051 Minuten, 30 SekundenAs the weak field sweeps past the sta coils, it induces a small voltage. That small voltage is fed back into the field
51:4051 Minuten, 40 Sekundencoil and a small current flows. That current strengthens the rotor's magnetism a little. The slightly
51:4851 Minuten, 48 Sekundenstronger field induces a slightly bigger voltage that pushes more current into
51:5451 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthe field coil. The field grows stronger again and the whole thing builds on
52:0152 Minuten, 1 Sekundeitself like a snowball rolling downhill, gathering size with every turn. So where
52:0852 Minuten, 8 Sekundendoes it stop? Why doesn't the voltage just climb forever? The iron sets the limit. As you push more and more current
52:1752 Minuten, 17 Sekundenthrough the field coil, the iron's tiny internal magnets become more and more completely lined up until nearly all of
52:2652 Minuten, 26 Sekundenthem are pointing the same way. At that point, the iron is called saturated.
52:3252 Minuten, 32 SekundenPushing in more current barely increases the magnetism at all because there's almost nothing left to line up.
52:4052 Minuten, 40 SekundenEngineers draw this on a graph called the magnetization curve. It rises steeply at first and then bends over and
52:4852 Minuten, 48 Sekundenflattens out. The generator's voltage climbs up that curve until it reaches the point where the current the voltage
52:5652 Minuten, 56 Sekundencan push through the field coil just balances the magnetism that current can create.
53:0453 Minuten, 4 SekundenThen it settles and holds steady.
53:0853 Minuten, 8 SekundenChapman's textbook walks through this process carefully for direct current generators and it lists the ways the
53:1553 Minuten, 15 Sekundenbuildup can fail. They're worth knowing because they explain real problems in real machines.
53:2453 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe first way is simply that the residual magnetism is gone. If a generator sits unused for a very long
53:3253 Minuten, 32 Sekundentime or gets knocked around or is exposed to a strong opposing field, the iron can lose that last faint trace.
53:4353 Minuten, 43 SekundenThen there's no seed for the snowball.
53:4653 Minuten, 46 SekundenYou spin the machine and nothing happens.
53:5053 Minuten, 50 SekundenThe second way is that the field coil is connected backward.
53:5553 Minuten, 55 SekundenThen the tiny current from the residual magnetism flows through the field coil in the wrong direction and instead of
54:0454 Minuten, 4 Sekundenstrengthening the leftover magnetism, it cancels it. The snowball melts before it
54:1154 Minuten, 11 Sekundenstarts. The third way is too much resistance in the field circuit. If the field coil and its wiring resist the
54:1854 Minuten, 18 Sekundencurrent too strongly, the small starting voltage can't push enough current through to build the field up. The
54:2554 Minuten, 25 Sekundenmachine stalls at a low, useless voltage.
54:2954 Minuten, 29 SekundenAnd the fix for the first problem, the one where the machine has lost its memory, is wonderfully simple. You give
54:3654 Minuten, 36 Sekundenit a reminder. You take a battery and for just a moment connect it across the field coil. That brief current lines up
54:4554 Minuten, 45 Sekundenthe iron's tiny magnets again and leaves behind a fresh trace of residual magnetism.
54:5154 Minuten, 51 SekundenMechanics call this flashing the field.
54:5554 Minuten, 55 SekundenAfter that, the generator will build up its voltage on its own the next time it spins.
55:0255 Minuten, 2 SekundenWhat's remarkable is that VI's patent application back in 1866 already described this trick. He
55:1055 Minuten, 10 Sekundenexplained that before using his generator, you should pass a current through the electromagnets coils once to leave a small amount of permanent
55:1955 Minuten, 19 Sekundenmagnetism in their cores. The first self-feeding generator came with instructions for waking it up. That
55:2755 Minuten, 27 Sekundentrick is still in use. The farm mechanic while repairing a standby generator
55:3455 Minuten, 34 Sekundennoticed that its voltage regulator had no separate permanent magnet to start it. It relied entirely on residual magnetism in the rotor.
55:4655 Minuten, 46 SekundenAnd he pointed out that generators like this, if they sit idle for too long, can lose that magnetism and need their field
55:5455 Minuten, 54 Sekundenflashed before they'll produce any power.
55:5855 Minuten, 58 SekundenKeep that detail in mind. It's going to come back later tonight in a settings where the stakes are much higher.
56:0756 Minuten, 7 SekundenThe self-feeding dynamo changed the economics of electricity almost overnight.
56:1456 Minuten, 14 SekundenMachines no longer needed banks of heavy steel magnets or racks of acid batteries.
56:2156 Minuten, 21 SekundenYou could build a generator as large as you wanted with electromagnets as strong as your iron allowed and start it with
56:3056 Minuten, 30 Sekundennothing more than the ghost of magnetism left in its own core. Within a few years, dynamos were lighting factories,
56:3756 Minuten, 37 Sekundenlighouses, and city streets. There was one more thing the electromagnet gave engineers, and in the long run, it
56:4456 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmattered even more than the power. The field could be adjusted. Increase the current in the field coil and the
56:5256 Minuten, 52 Sekundenmagnetism grows stronger and the voltage rises. Decrease the field current and the voltage falls. At last, the
57:0057 Minutengenerator had a dial on its magnet. But having a dial raises two new problems.
57:0657 Minuten, 6 SekundenThe first is purely mechanical. In a modern alternating current generator, the field coil sits on the rotor
57:1357 Minuten, 13 Sekundenspinning at 1,800 or 3,600 revolutions per minute.
57:1857 Minuten, 18 SekundenThe current that feeds it comes from the outside world where everything is standing still. How do you get electricity to jump across the gap
57:2657 Minuten, 26 Sekundenbetween something still and something spinning?
57:3057 Minuten, 30 SekundenAnd the second problem is harder. Loads switch on and off all the time. Voltage sags and rises with every change. So
57:3857 Minuten, 38 Sekundensomebody or something has to keep turning that dial over and over every moment the generator runs.
57:4757 Minuten, 47 SekundenWhat kind of hand is quick enough to do that? Let's take the mechanical problem first because it has a satisfying
57:5557 Minuten, 55 Sekundenhandson answer. Before we solve it though, it's worth asking why the rotor's field needs to be fed with
58:0258 Minuten, 2 Sekundendirect current at all. The generator makes alternating current. Why not just send some of that back into the rotor?
58:1158 Minuten, 11 SekundenThink about what the rotor's job is.
58:1458 Minuten, 14 SekundenIt's supposed to be a magnet with a fixed north pole and a fixed south pole sweeping past the sta coils. The sta
58:2258 Minuten, 22 Sekundencoils see north, then south, then north, and that's what produces their alternating wave.
58:3058 Minuten, 30 SekundenIf you fed the rotor alternating current, its poles would flip back and forth on their own while it was also spinning. The stator would see a
58:3858 Minuten, 38 Sekundenscrambled mix of rotation and flipping, and the result would be a mess.
58:4458 Minuten, 44 SekundenThe rotor needs to hold its poles steady the way a permanent magnet would. A steady magnet needs a steady current.
58:5158 Minuten, 51 SekundenAnd a steady current flowing in one direction is direct current.
58:5758 Minuten, 57 SekundenSo we need direct current produced out here in the still world delivered into a coil that's spinning 30 or 60 times a second.
59:0659 Minuten, 6 SekundenThe farm mechanic compared this to a problem he'd faced before, repairing the swivel joint on an excavator. The upper
59:1459 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbody of an excavator rotates on its base, and yet hydraulic fluid has to flow from the base up into the arm. The
59:2259 Minuten, 22 Sekundenfluid has to cross a pivot. A generator faces the same challenge with electricity.
59:2859 Minuten, 28 SekundenThe classic solution is a pair of slip rings and a pair of brushes. The slip rings are two smooth copper bands
59:3659 Minuten, 36 Sekundenwrapped around the rotor's shaft, insulated from the shaft and from each other. Each ring is wired to one end of
59:4359 Minuten, 43 Sekundenthe field coil. The brushes are small blocks of carbon held still in the frame, pressed gently against the
59:5059 Minuten, 50 Sekundenspinning rings by springs. Current flows from a stationary wire into a brush, across the sliding contact into the
59:5859 Minuten, 58 Sekundenring, and from the ring into the rotor's coil.
1:00:041 Stunde, 4 SekundenIt's like a hand resting on a turning wheel, always touching it, never letting go.
1:00:121 Stunde, 12 SekundenThat solves the mechanical problem. And for a long time, it was the standard answer.
1:00:181 Stunde, 18 SekundenNow for the harder problem. Where does that direct current come from and who adjusts it? In many modern generators,
1:00:281 Stunde, 28 Sekundenthe story goes like this. Mounted on the same shaft as the main rotor, often at the far end, there's a small separate generator with permanent magnets.
1:00:411 Stunde, 41 SekundenIt's called the pilot exciter. The word exciter just means the thing that supplies the initial magnetism.
1:00:491 Stunde, 49 SekundenBecause it uses permanent magnets, it doesn't need any residual magnetism or any battery. The moment the engine
1:00:591 Stunde, 59 Sekundenstarts turning the shaft, the pilot exciter starts producing a small alternating current. That current goes
1:01:071 Stunde, 1 Minute, 7 Sekundeninto a box that's usually no bigger than a paperback book called the automatic voltage regulator or AVR.
1:01:161 Stunde, 1 Minute, 16 SekundenInside the AVR, the first thing that happens is that the alternating current gets straightened out. This is done by a
1:01:241 Stunde, 1 Minute, 24 Sekundenrectifier, which sounds complicated, but usually just means a set of diodes. A diode is an electronic one-way valve. It
1:01:331 Stunde, 1 Minute, 33 Sekundenlets current pass in one direction and blocks it in the other. Arrange a few of them correctly and the back and forth
1:01:411 Stunde, 1 Minute, 41 Sekundencurrent coming in turns into a one-way current going out. Alternating becomes direct.
1:01:491 Stunde, 1 Minute, 49 SekundenThe AVR then sends that direct current out through the brushes into the slip rings into the rotor's field coil. The rotor becomes an electromagnet.
1:02:001 Stunde, 2 MinutenIts field sweeps past the main sta coils and the generator begins producing power. And now comes the part that gives
1:02:091 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthe regulator its name. The AVR also watches the output. A pair of thin wires
1:02:161 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 16 Sekundenruns from the main status output back to the regulator so it can measure the voltage the generator is actually delivering.
1:02:241 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 24 SekundenThen it compares that measurement with the voltage it's been told to hold.
1:02:281 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 28 SekundenLet's say you want 120 volts and the generator is only producing 100. The regulator sees the shortfall and
1:02:361 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 36 Sekundenincreases the direct current going to the rotor. The field grows stronger. The output climbs to 110, still too low, so
1:02:461 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 46 Sekundenthe regulator pushes a little more current. The output climbs to 120. Perhaps it overshoots a bit to 125.
1:02:561 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 56 SekundenThe regulator notices that too and eases off. The voltage settles back. This goes on continuously every moment the
1:03:041 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 4 Sekundengenerator is running. The farm mechanic described it as a constant dance to keep the voltage where the regulator is set.
1:03:121 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 12 SekundenEngineers call it a feedback loop. You measure what's happening, compare it with what you want, adjust, and measure
1:03:191 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 19 Sekundenagain. The same kind of loop runs your home thermostat and a much finer version of it runs inside every big power plant.
1:03:301 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 30 SekundenThe regulator usually has a small adjustment screw, a potentiometer, that lets a technician fine-tune the target
1:03:391 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 39 Sekundenvoltage. And many regulators have a second adjustment that's just as important called stability.
1:03:481 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 48 SekundenThe stability setting decides how aggressively the regulator responds to a change.
1:03:551 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 55 SekundenImagine the generator is holding steady at 250 volts and suddenly a big motor starts up. The voltage dips to 230.
1:04:071 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 7 SekundenHow quickly should the regulator pull it back? If it reacts too slowly, the voltage stays low for a while and lights
1:04:161 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 16 Sekundendim and motors struggle. If it reacts too eagerly, it overcorrects, then
1:04:231 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 23 Sekundenovercorrects the other way and the voltage jitters up and down. The right setting sits in between.
1:04:321 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 32 SekundenOne old-fashioned way to find it is to connect an analog meter, the kind with a moving needle, to the field wires. You
1:04:401 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 40 Sekundenwatch the needle, which flickers as the regulator makes its constant small corrections, and you turn the stability
1:04:471 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 47 Sekundenscrew until the flicker is as small as you can get it. It's a surprisingly human way to tune a machine, watching a
1:04:561 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 56 Sekundenneedle tremble and turning a screw until it calms down.
1:05:001 Stunde, 5 MinutenSo that's a brushed generator with a pilot exciter, a regulator, brushes, and slip rings.
1:05:091 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 9 SekundenMany portable generators use a brushed design much like this, although the smallest ones often power their regulator from an extra winding in the
1:05:171 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 17 Sekundenstator instead of a separate pilot exciter.
1:05:211 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 21 SekundenBut brushes have a weakness. They're carbon blocks rubbing against spinning copper every second the generator runs.
1:05:301 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 30 SekundenThey wear down. They need inspection.
1:05:341 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 34 SekundenThey can spark and stick. For machines that have to run for years or sit waiting for the one night they're
1:05:411 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 41 Sekundenneeded, engineers found a way to get rid of them altogether. And the way they did it is one of the most elegant tricks in
1:05:491 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 49 Sekundenthe whole machine. The idea is to use induction itself to carry the power across the gap instead of a sliding contact.
1:05:591 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 59 SekundenKeep the pilot exciter and the regulator exactly as they were, but instead of sending the regulator's direct current
1:06:081 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 8 Sekundeninto brushes, send it into a stationary coil mounted in the frame of the generator.
1:06:151 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 15 SekundenThis coil is part of a small extra machine called the main exciter.
1:06:211 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 21 SekundenThe direct current turns that stationary coil into a steady electromagnet.
1:06:271 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 27 SekundenNow on the rotor facing that stationary electromagnet put another set of coils.
1:06:341 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 34 SekundenAs the rotor spins, those coils sweep through the stationary magnets's field and alternating current is induced in them.
1:06:431 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 43 SekundenNotice what's happened. It's the main generator turned inside out. In the main generator, the magnet spins and the
1:06:521 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 52 Sekundencoils sit still. In the main exciter, the magnet sits still and the coils
1:06:591 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 59 Sekundenspin. Either way, you get induction and either way you get alternating current.
1:07:061 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 6 SekundenBut the rotor's field coil needs direct current. So the engineers went one step further and mounted the rectifier, the
1:07:161 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 16 Sekundenset of diodes, right on the spinning shaft.
1:07:201 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 20 SekundenDiodes are small and tough. They don't mind being whirled around at high speed.
1:07:271 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 27 SekundenThe alternating current from the rotating exciter coils passes through the rotating diodes, comes out as direct
1:07:361 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 36 Sekundencurrent and flows straight into the main field coil. All without anything ever touching the rotor from the outside.
1:07:461 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 46 SekundenThe regulator still controls everything, just one step removed.
1:07:521 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 52 SekundenWhen it wants more voltage, it sends more current into the stationary exciter coil. That makes a stronger field in the
1:08:021 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 2 Sekundenexciter that induces more alternating current on the rotor. The diodes turn
1:08:081 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 8 Sekundenthat into more direct current for the main field. The main field grows stronger. The output voltage rises.
1:08:201 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 20 SekundenIt's a chain of cause and effect stretching from a little box on the side of the generator through an invisible
1:08:281 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 28 Sekundenmagnetic handoff into the heart of the spinning rotor. A generator built this way is called brushless.
1:08:371 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 37 SekundenLarger standby generators and high-end machines are usually brushless because with no brushes to wear out, they can
1:08:441 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 44 Sekundenrun for a very long time with little maintenance. If you ever open one up and see a ring of small dodes bolted to the
1:08:521 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 52 Sekundenrotor turning with the shaft, that's the telltale sign. And what about the workshop builder whose permanent magnet
1:09:001 Stunde, 9 Minutengenerator made three times the voltage he wanted with no field to adjust? He had other options. He could run it
1:09:081 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 8 Sekundenslower, set the gap a little wider, or wind the coils with fewer turns. And his plan all along was to send the output
1:09:161 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 16 Sekundenthrough a rectifier, turning its alternating current into direct current and then into charging equipment that manages what reaches his batteries.
1:09:261 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 26 SekundenThat's how many small permanent magnet generators are used today. Small wind turbines, for example, are usually
1:09:341 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 34 Sekundendirect drive with no gearbox, and they often use permanent magnet generators.
1:09:401 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 40 SekundenWhen you can't adjust the magnet, you adjust the electricity after it leaves the machine.
1:09:461 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 46 SekundenFor the big machines that hold the grid together, though, the adjustable field is still the main tool. Now, there's a
1:09:541 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 54 Sekundensmall cousin of all this that you've probably ridden with every day. A car engine has an alternator driven by a belt whose job is to keep the battery
1:10:031 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 3 Sekundencharged. A big diesel generator has the same kind of thing. On a 500 kovolt amp diesel generator powerful enough to run
1:10:111 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 11 Sekundena small factory, there's a little beltdriven charging alternator mounted on the side of the engine. It works on
1:10:191 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 19 Sekundenexactly the principles we've been talking about. It produces alternating current internally, rectifies it into
1:10:261 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 26 Sekundendirect current, and feeds the starting batteries.
1:10:301 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 30 SekundenMechanics call it by several names. the charging alternator, the dynamo, the DC generator, but it's all the same idea on
1:10:391 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 39 Sekundena small scale. On that big diesel machine, the batteries are a pair of 12V units, each rated at 200 amp, connected
1:10:491 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 49 Sekundenin series, positive to negative. So, their voltages add together to make 24 V. Their only job is to crank the engine
1:10:591 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 59 Sekundenand get it started. Once the engine is running, the little charging alternator tops them back up so they're ready the
1:11:071 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 7 Sekundennext time. We'll come back to that engine later. It turns out to play a surprisingly important role in the
1:11:141 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbiggest story of all. So now our generator is complete in principle. It has a spinning rotor with an adjustable
1:11:231 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 23 Sekundenmagnet. It has a stator full of copper coils in three phases. It has an exciter
1:11:301 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 30 Sekundenand a regulator that keep the voltage steady, correcting it constantly as conditions change. Set it spinning with
1:11:381 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 38 Sekundennothing connected, and it's almost effortless. The engine barely works. The shaft turns freely. The voltage sits
1:11:481 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 48 Sekundenright where the regulator wants it. Then you plug something in, a heater, say, and something odd happens. The voltage
1:11:571 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 57 Sekundendips for a moment and the regulator catches it. But the engine also changes its sound. It labors. It has to work
1:12:061 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 6 Sekundenharder to keep the same speed. The shaft, which spun so easily a moment ago, is suddenly harder to turn, as if an invisible hand had grabbed it.
1:12:181 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 18 SekundenNothing new has touched that shaft. No brake has been applied. There's no extra friction. The only thing that changed is
1:12:261 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 26 Sekundenthat current started flowing through the copper coils.
1:12:301 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 30 SekundenSo where is that invisible drag coming from?
1:12:341 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 34 SekundenAnyone who has held a small generator by hand has felt this. The team behind a well-known engineering channel demonstrated it with a little model wind turbine.
1:12:451 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 45 SekundenSpin the generator shaft with nothing connected and it turns easily and produces a voltage.
1:12:521 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 52 Sekundenconnect a load and the shaft becomes much harder to turn. The blades slow down and that's why a real wind turbine
1:13:011 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 1 Sekundewon't start producing power until the wind reaches a certain minimum speed called the cut-in speed. Below that, the
1:13:091 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 9 Sekundenwind simply isn't strong enough to overcome the drag the generator creates the moment it starts delivering power.
1:13:181 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 18 SekundenThat drag has a name, or rather the rule behind it does.
1:13:251 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 25 SekundenIn 1834, 3 years after Faraday's discovery, a physicist named Hinrich Lent working in
1:13:331 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 33 SekundenSt. Petersburg, stated a principle about the direction of induced currents.
1:13:401 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 40 SekundenWhen a changing magnetic field induces a current in a wire, that current flows in
1:13:471 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 47 Sekundenwhichever direction creates a magnetic field that opposes the change. Let's make that concrete.
1:13:561 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 56 SekundenPush the north pole of a magnet toward a coil. The flux through the coil increases. So, a current is induced.
1:14:061 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 6 SekundenWhich way does it flow?
1:14:081 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 8 SekundenIt flows in the direction that turns the coil itself into a magnet with its own north pole facing your approaching north pole.
1:14:191 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 19 SekundenTwo north poles repel.
1:14:221 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 22 SekundenSo the coil pushes back against the magnet you're pushing in.
1:14:281 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 28 SekundenNow pull the magnet away. The current reverses and the coil becomes a magnet with a south pole facing your retreating
1:14:381 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 38 Sekundennorth pole. North and south attract. So now the coil pulls back on the magnet
1:14:451 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 45 Sekundenyou're pulling away. Whatever you do, the induced current resists you. At
1:14:511 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 51 Sekundenfirst, this can sound like bad luck, as if nature had chosen to make generators harder to run than they should be. But
1:15:001 Stunde, 15 Minutenimagine for a moment that Lens's rule went the other way. Suppose the induced current helped the magnet along instead
1:15:091 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 9 Sekundenof resisting it. Then you'd push the magnet a little. The current would push it further which would induce more
1:15:171 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 17 Sekundencurrent which would push it even further. The magnet would accelerate on its own faster and faster and the coil
1:15:261 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 26 Sekundenwould pour out electricity the whole time with nobody supplying any effort.
1:15:321 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 32 SekundenYou would have built a machine that creates energy from nothing. People have dreamed of machines like that for
1:15:391 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 39 Sekundencenturies. Wheels with shifting weights, self-filling water mills, magnetic contraptions of every description. None
1:15:471 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 47 Sekundenof them has ever worked. And Lens's rule is part of the reason why energy is never created for free. If electricity
1:15:551 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 55 Sekundencomes out of a generator, the same amount of effort or more has to go in.
1:16:001 Stunde, 16 MinutenAnd the push back that you feel on the shaft is exactly how nature collects that payment. Every watt of electrical
1:16:071 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 7 Sekundenpower flowing out of the coils shows up as a matching drag on the rotor. And the engine or the turbine has to supply the
1:16:161 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 16 Sekundenforce to overcome it. There's a simple way to see this that engineering textbooks love because it strips the machine down to almost nothing.
1:16:251 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 25 SekundenChapman's textbook uses it in its very first chapter.
1:16:291 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 29 SekundenImagine two long parallel metal rails lying flat with a magnetic field pointing straight down through the space
1:16:361 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 36 Sekundenbetween them. Lay a metal bar across the rails so it can slide along them touching both. Connect the far ends of
1:16:441 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 44 Sekundenthe rails to a battery. Now if you push the bar along the rails, it's a conductor moving through a magnetic field. So a voltage is induced across
1:16:531 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 53 Sekundenit. That voltage can drive a current through the bar and around the circuit.
1:17:001 Stunde, 17 MinutenThe bar is acting as a generator. But the moment current flows through the bar, the bar is a current carrying wire
1:17:071 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 7 Sekundensitting in a magnetic field and that creates a force on it. Which way does the force point? backward against your
1:17:161 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 16 Sekundenpush. The harder you make the bar work as a generator, the harder it pushes back against you. Now try the reverse.
1:17:261 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 26 SekundenLeave the bar alone and let the battery push a current through it. That current flowing through a wire in a magnetic
1:17:341 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 34 Sekundenfield creates a force and the bar starts to slide along the rails on its own.
1:17:401 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 40 SekundenNow the bar is acting as a motor and as it moves it's once again a conductor moving through a field. So a voltage is
1:17:491 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 49 Sekundeninduced in it and that voltage pushes against the batteries current. The motor fights back against its own supply just
1:17:571 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 57 Sekundenas the generator fought back against its own driver. Push the bar and electricity comes out. Feed it electricity and it
1:18:061 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 6 Sekundenmoves. A generator and a motor are one machine run in opposite directions.
1:18:131 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 13 SekundenThe world discovered this in a very practical way by accident.
1:18:181 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 18 SekundenIn 1873 at the World's Fair in Vienna, the Belgian inventor Zenob Graham was
1:18:251 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 25 Sekundenexhibiting his dynamo, one of the first generators good enough for real industrial work. His business partner,
1:18:331 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 33 SekundenHipoli Fontaine, was setting up the display.
1:18:371 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 37 SekundenAccording to the story, he connected the wires from a working dynamo to a second Graham machine that happened to be sitting idle a few hundred yards away.
1:18:471 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 47 SekundenThe second machine's shaft began to spin by itself. It was a generator being fed electricity and it was behaving like a
1:18:551 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 55 Sekundenmotor. Fontaine had it drive a water pump. It was one of the first times an electric motor did genuinely useful work
1:19:041 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 4 Sekundenat an industrial scale. And it showed that electricity could carry power from one place to another, turning a shaft
1:19:121 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 12 Sekundenhere into a shaft over there. Now, let's go back inside our alternating current
1:19:191 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 19 Sekundengenerator and see what that push back looks like when the rotor is spinning.
1:19:251 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 25 SekundenWith nothing plugged in, the rotor's magnetic field sweeps around. The sta
1:19:321 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 32 Sekundencoils produce voltage, but no current flows through them, so they don't create any field of their own. Nothing resists
1:19:411 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 41 Sekundenthe rotor. Plug in a load and current starts flowing through the stator coils.
1:19:481 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 48 SekundenAnd current in a coil, as Urststead showed us long ago, creates a magnetic field. The stator's three phases
1:19:581 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 58 Sekundencarrying their three staggered currents together produce a magnetic field of their own. And that field rotates around
1:20:071 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 7 Sekundenthe stator at exactly the same speed as the rotor. So now there are two magnetic fields in the machine, both spinning
1:20:141 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 14 Sekundentogether. the rotor's field and the status field. Those two fields pull on each other like two magnets. And because
1:20:231 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 23 Sekundenof Lens's rule, the status field is always arranged so that it pulls backward on the rotor. The rotor's poles
1:20:311 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 31 Sekundenend up leading slightly ahead, and the status field trails behind, tugging on them. Picture two people walking side by
1:20:401 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 40 Sekundenside, holding opposite ends of a stretched spring. The one in front is the rotor pulled forward by the engine.
1:20:471 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 47 SekundenThe one behind is the status field created by the load. The spring between them is the magnetic pull. The more load
1:20:561 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 56 Sekundenyou connect, the stronger the status field, and the more the spring stretches, and the harder the engine has
1:21:031 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 3 Sekundento pull to keep going. Engineers measure how far ahead the rotor's field is leading by an angle. Chapman calls it
1:21:111 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthe torque angle. With a light load, the angle is small. With a heavy load, it's larger. And there's a limit. The power
1:21:201 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 20 Sekundenthe generator delivers grows as the angle opens up, but only up to a point.
1:21:271 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 27 SekundenIf the rotor gets too far ahead, about a quarter of a turn of the magnetic pattern, the pull between the fields reaches its maximum.
1:21:381 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 38 Sekundenpush any harder and instead of pulling harder, the magnetic spring slips.
1:21:451 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe rotor's pole jumps past the status field and snaps forward to the next one.
1:21:521 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 52 SekundenEngineers call this slipping a pole. And in a big machine, it's violent with huge
1:22:001 Stunde, 22 Minutensurges of current and a terrible jolt through the shaft.
1:22:051 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 5 SekundenRemember that limit? It's the breaking point of the invisible spring and it's going to be the central character in the most dramatic chapter of our story.
1:22:171 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 17 SekundenFor now, in normal operation, the spring holds, and the engine simply has to work harder to supply the power.
1:22:271 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 27 SekundenSomething has to decide how hard.
1:22:311 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 31 SekundenEngines and turbines have a device called a governor whose job is to hold the speed steady.
1:22:381 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 38 SekundenWhen the load increases, the shaft starts to slow down very slightly. The governor senses that and opens up the supply of energy.
1:22:501 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 50 SekundenMore fuel into a diesel engine. More steam into a turbine. More water through
1:22:571 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 57 Sekundena hydroelect electric gate. The shaft speeds back up. When the load drops, the
1:23:051 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 5 Sekundenshaft starts to speed up and the governor cuts back.
1:23:101 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 10 SekundenNotice the order of events. When you switch on a heater, the extra power appears instantly
1:23:191 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 19 Sekundenbefore the governor has had any chance to react because the status field immediately grabs the rotor and draws
1:23:281 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 28 Sekundenenergy out of its motion. The rotor slows just a hair. Only then does the governor notice and respond.
1:23:391 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 39 SekundenFor that first instant, the energy that heats your room comes out of the spinning mass of the machine itself.
1:23:481 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 48 SekundenLike a little bit of momentum being spent.
1:23:521 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 52 SekundenSo now we have the answer to one of the questions we asked at the very beginning. When you switch on your kettle, the machine far away becomes
1:24:011 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 1 Sekundeharder to turn because the current flowing to your kettle also flows through that machine's sta coils. And
1:24:091 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthose coils, by Lens's rule, build a magnetic field that pulls back on the spinning rotor. Nobody decides to push
1:24:171 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 17 Sekundenback. The push back is simply what induced current does every time, everywhere. The only question left is
1:24:251 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 25 Sekundenhow the machine answers it. That's worth pausing on because it's the single most important idea tonight. The speed of a
1:24:341 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 34 Sekundengenerator's shaft is a direct physical measure of the balance between what's going in and what's coming out. More
1:24:421 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 42 Sekundenpower going out than coming in and the shaft slows. More coming in than going out and the shaft speeds up. And since
1:24:501 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthe speed sets the frequency, the frequency of the electricity is a measure of that balance, too. Let's make this real with the big diesel generator.
1:25:011 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 1 SekundeSuppose it's rated at 500 kov volt amps.
1:25:031 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 3 SekundenWhen its electrical load rises, the status field drags harder on the rotor.
1:25:091 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe governor feeds more fuel and the engine works harder. To burn more fuel, the engine also needs more air and its
1:25:171 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 17 Sekundenturbocharger packs extra air into the cylinders. We'll look inside that engine properly later tonight. For now, what
1:25:241 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 24 Sekundenmatters is where its energy goes. The force of combustion drives the pistons, turns the crankshaft, spins the heavy
1:25:321 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 32 Sekundenflywheel, and through a coupling plate turns the alternator's rotor. And all along that path, energy leaks away as
1:25:391 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 39 Sekundenheat. Chapman's textbook draws this as a flow diagram with power coming in at one end and useful electricity coming out at
1:25:481 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 48 Sekundenthe other and a series of little side streams branching off along the way.
1:25:531 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 53 SekundenSome energy is lost to friction in the bearings and to the drag of air on the spinning parts. Some is lost inside the
1:26:001 Stunde, 26 Minuteniron as it's magnetized first one way and then the other 60 times every second on a 60 Hz machine. Some is lost in the
1:26:081 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 8 Sekundencopper, heating the wires as current flows through them. None of that energy disappears.
1:26:161 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 16 SekundenIt all turns into heat and the heat has to be carried away.
1:26:221 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 22 SekundenSo, the engine has a closed cooling system.
1:26:261 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 26 SekundenA circulation pump pushes coolant through the engine where it picks up heat, then through the radiator where a
1:26:341 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 34 Sekundenfan blows air across it and back into the engine again, around and around in a
1:26:411 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 41 Sekundensealed loop. In that loop sits a thermostat, a small valve that opens by itself when the coolant gets hot enough.
1:26:521 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 52 SekundenOn this engine, it stays closed until the coolant reaches about 187° F.
1:27:011 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 1 SekundeBelow that, it keeps the coolant circulating inside the engine so the engine can warm up. above it. The valve
1:27:101 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 10 Sekundenopens and sends the coolant out to the radiator and the generator's controller keeps watch over all of it. If the oil
1:27:191 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 19 Sekundenpressure falls, if the coolant gets too hot, if the voltage drifts too high or
1:27:261 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 26 Sekundentoo low, the controller shuts the engine down to protect it. It also watches the
1:27:331 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 33 Sekundenload. If someone tries to pull 550 or 600 kov volt amps from a 500 kovolt amp
1:27:431 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 43 Sekundenmachine, the controller trips it offline before the magnetic spring and the engine are pushed beyond what they can safely handle.
1:27:541 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 54 SekundenSo a single generator with a single engine feeding its own load is a manageable thing. It has a governor for
1:28:031 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 3 Sekundenspeed, a regulator for voltage, and a controller standing guard. When the load
1:28:101 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 10 Sekundenrises, it works harder. When the load falls, it relaxes.
1:28:171 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 17 SekundenIt's a closed little world.
1:28:201 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 20 SekundenBut one generator is never enough for a city. The obvious next step is to
1:28:271 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 27 Sekundenconnect two of them together, sharing the same wires. so they can share the load. It sounds like the easiest thing
1:28:351 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 35 Sekundenin the world. On a summer day in 1882 in lower Manhattan, Thomas Edison tried
1:28:431 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 43 Sekunden[clears throat] it. And what happened next was so chaotic that most of the people in the room ran for the door.
1:28:511 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 51 SekundenBy the summer of 1882, Thomas Edison had already invented a practical light bulb.
1:28:581 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 58 SekundenWhat he wanted now was to sell light to a whole neighborhood the way gas companies sold gas.
1:29:061 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 6 SekundenHe chose a crowded district of lower Manhattan about half a square mile around Wall Street and bought two old
1:29:131 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 13 Sekundenbuildings on Pearl Street to house his power station.
1:29:171 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 17 SekundenHis crews dug miles of trenches in the streets to lay copper cables underground.
1:29:231 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 23 SekundenAt the heart of the station stood his dynamos. Each one weighed about 27 tons, and they were so large that Edison's
1:29:311 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 31 Sekundenteam nicknamed them jumbos after a famous circus elephant owned by PT Barnum.
1:29:391 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 39 SekundenEach jumbo was driven directly by its own steam engine and could light about 1,200 lamps.
1:29:461 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 46 SekundenOn July 6th, 1882, Edison ran his first big test. One engine and one dynamo
1:29:541 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 54 Sekundenworked fine. Then he started a second engine and connected the two dynamos together in parallel so that they would share the load.
1:30:051 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 5 SekundenHe later wrote about what happened and his description is hard to improve on.
1:30:111 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 11 SekundenOf all the circuses since Adam was born, he said they had the worst one then.
1:30:181 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 18 SekundenOne engine would stop and the other would race up to a thousand revolutions per minute.
1:30:241 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe machines lurched back and forth, speeding and slowing, trading the load between them.
1:30:321 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 32 SekundenSparks flew. Most of the people in the room fled. Edison stayed and shut everything down.
1:30:401 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 40 SekundenWhat was going on? When two generators share a load, each one supplies part of the current. How much each one supplies
1:30:491 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 49 Sekundendepends on its voltage. If one machine is pushing a slightly higher voltage than the other, it takes a bigger share
1:30:571 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 57 Sekundenof the load. And as we learned in the last chapter, taking more load means more drag on the shaft. So that machine
1:31:051 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 5 Sekundenslows down. Meanwhile, the other machine relieved of its share speeds up. Now its
1:31:121 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 12 Sekundenvoltage is higher, so it grabs more of the load and it slows down while the first one speeds up again. If the
1:31:191 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 19 Sekundengovernors on the two engines respond quickly and smoothly, this back and forth dies out and the two machines
1:31:261 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 26 Sekundensettle into sharing the load evenly. If the governors are sluggish or badly matched, the back and forth grows
1:31:331 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 33 Sekundeninstead. One machine takes nearly everything and bogs down. The other, carrying almost nothing, runs wild. Then
1:31:421 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 42 Sekundenthey swap. Edison's team eventually traced the problem to exactly that. the governors that were supposed to control
1:31:481 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 48 Sekundenthe engine speeds. It took some engineering, but they sorted it out. On September 4th, 1882, at 3:00 in the
1:31:571 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 57 Sekundenafternoon, Edison gave the word. A switch was thrown, and his lamps glowed to life across the district. The station
1:32:051 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 5 Sekundenran for years. Edison's system used direct current at a little over 100 volts. That low voltage was safer for
1:32:131 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 13 Sekundencustomers and it matched his lamps. But it came with a stubborn limitation.
1:32:181 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 18 Sekunden[clears throat] Electricity flowing through long wires loses energy as heat.
1:32:231 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 23 SekundenAnd at low voltage, you need a lot of current to deliver a given amount of power. And a lot of current means a lot
1:32:321 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 32 Sekundenof loss. So a direct current station like Pearl Street could only serve customers within about a mile or so.
1:32:411 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 41 SekundenBeyond that, the cables would have to be impractically thick. That meant every neighborhood needed its own power station.
1:32:511 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 51 SekundenCities in the 1880s began to fill up with them. Each one a separate little island of light with its own dynamos,
1:33:001 Stunde, 33 Minutenits own engines, its own coal pile, and its own boundary where the service simply stopped.
1:33:081 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 8 SekundenThe way out was alternating current and the reason was a device called the transformer.
1:33:161 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 16 SekundenA transformer is two coils wound on a shared iron core and it works entirely
1:33:231 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 23 Sekundenby Faraday's principle. An alternating current in the first coil creates a constantly changing magnetic field in
1:33:301 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 30 Sekundenthe iron and that changing field induces a voltage in the second coil. If the second coil has more turns than the
1:33:381 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 38 Sekundenfirst, the voltage goes up. If it has fewer, the voltage goes down. That's the whole trick. And it was a game changer.
1:33:471 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 47 SekundenWith alternating current, you could generate power at a moderate voltage, step it up to a very high voltage for the long journey, and step it back down again near the homes that would use it.
1:33:581 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 58 SekundenHigh voltage means small current for the same power, and small current means small losses. So, electricity could now
1:34:061 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 6 Sekundentravel for many miles. Transformers only work with alternating current because they depend on a field that keeps changing.
1:34:151 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 15 SekundenA steady direct current produces a steady field and a steady field, as Faraday's needle showed, does nothing.
1:34:221 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe late 1880s saw a bitter commercial fight between the backers of direct current and the backers of alternating
1:34:301 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 30 Sekundencurrent. On the alternating current side stood George Westinghouse, an industrialist and Nicola Tesla, a
1:34:381 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 38 Sekundenbrilliant inventor who had worked out how to use several alternating currents offset from each other in time to create
1:34:451 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 45 Sekundena rotating magnetic field. That's the same idea behind the three staggered waves of the three-phase generator.
1:34:531 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 53 SekundenTesla's polyphase motors and generators made alternating current practical for running machinery as well as for
1:35:001 Stunde, 35 Minutenlighting. His key idea was the rotating magnetic field. Feed three coils around a ring with three staggered currents and
1:35:091 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthe combined field inside the ring turns smoothly around like a spinning magnet with nothing physically spinning. put
1:35:171 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 17 Sekundeniron or copper inside that turning field and it gets dragged along which makes a motor. It's the same turning field that
1:35:251 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 25 Sekundenforms in a loaded generator's sta and tugs back on the rotor. Tesla showed it could just as well pull a motor forward.
1:35:331 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe decisive demonstration came at Niagara Falls in 1895. A hydroelectric station called the Adams power plant
1:35:421 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 42 Sekundenbegan generating electricity from the falls using Westinghouse generators built on Tesla's principles. In November
1:35:501 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 50 Sekundenof 1896, that power was sent to the city of Buffalo about 26 mi away over high
1:35:561 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 56 Sekundenvoltage lines. Power made in one place was now being used in another miles across the countryside.
1:36:051 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 5 SekundenBut alternating current brought a new question that direct current had never had to answer. How fast should the waves
1:36:121 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 12 Sekundenbe? In the early years, every company picked its own frequency. The Niagara generators ran at 25 hertz which suited
1:36:221 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 22 Sekundenbig, slow water turbines, while other systems chose 40, 60, or more. For a while, that patchwork didn't matter
1:36:301 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 30 Sekundenbecause each system lived on its own island.
1:36:341 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 34 SekundenIt started to matter when those islands wanted to connect.
1:36:401 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 40 SekundenLinking two systems together had huge advantages.
1:36:441 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 44 SekundenIf one power plant broke down, its neighbors could cover the load. If one city's demand peaked in the evening
1:36:511 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 51 Sekundenwhile anothers peaked at noon, they could share generators instead of each building enough for its own peak.
1:36:591 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe bigger the connected system, the more efficiently and reliably it could run.
1:37:061 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 6 SekundenBut you can only connect systems that share the same frequency. A 60 Hz system and a 25 Hz system can't be tied
1:37:151 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 15 Sekundendirectly together. Any more than two people can walk hand in hand while one takes two steps for every step the other takes.
1:37:241 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 24 SekundenOver the following decades, the industry gradually settled on standards. North America converged on 60 hertz.
1:37:341 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 34 SekundenMost of Europe and much of the world settled on 50. Once neighboring systems spoke the same frequency, they could
1:37:421 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 42 Sekundenbegin to link up. At this point, alternating current turned out to be far more demanding than Edison's direct
1:37:491 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 49 Sekundencurrent. With direct current, when you connect two machines in parallel, you only need to worry about their voltages
1:37:561 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 56 Sekundenand their speeds sharing the load sensibly.
1:38:001 Stunde, 38 MinutenWith alternating current, the voltage coming out of each machine is a wave.
1:38:051 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 5 SekundenRising and falling 60 times a second. To connect two of them, matching the size and the speed of the waves is only the
1:38:141 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbeginning. The waves also have to be rising and falling together. Peak meeting peak and trough meeting trough
1:38:211 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 21 Sekundenat the very instant you close the switch. Think of it this way. It's like trying to step onto a merrygoround that's already spinning. Except the
1:38:301 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 30 Sekundenmerrygo round weighs hundreds of tons and turns 60 times a second and you have to land with your feet moving at exactly
1:38:381 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 38 Sekundenits speed at exactly the right spot. If you get it wrong, the merrygoround doesn't forgive you.
1:38:451 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 45 SekundenSo, what are the rules for stepping on and what actually happens inside the machines when you break them? Let's
1:38:531 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 53 Sekundenimagine we're standing in a power plant control room and we have a generator that's been running on its own, spinning, producing voltage, but not yet
1:39:021 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 2 Sekundenconnected to anything. On the other side of an open circuit breaker is the grid, alive, carrying power to millions of people. Our job is to join the two.
1:39:131 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 13 SekundenChapman's textbook lays out the conditions very clearly and there are four of them. First, the voltages must
1:39:201 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 20 Sekundenmatch. Our generator's output voltage must be the same as the grid's voltage.
1:39:261 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 26 SekundenIf ours is higher, then the instant we connect, current will rush from our machine into the grid. If ours is lower,
1:39:341 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 34 Sekundencurrent will rush the other way. We adjust this using the field current, turning our generator's magnetic dial up
1:39:411 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 41 Sekundenor down until the voltage on our side of the breaker equals the voltage on the grid side. Second, the phase sequence
1:39:491 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 49 Sekundenmust match. Remember, there are three phases and they reach their peaks in a
1:39:561 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 56 Sekundenfixed order, one after another. On the grid, it might be phase one, then phase
1:40:031 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 3 Sekunden2, then phase three. Our generator has to peak in that same order. If two of
1:40:101 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 10 Sekundenour phases were accidentally swapped, our sequence would run 1 3 2. Then, even
1:40:171 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 17 Sekundenif everything else were perfect, one phase would line up while the other two were badly out of step. This is usually
1:40:251 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 25 Sekundenchecked once when a machine is first installed and then it stays correct.
1:40:321 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 32 SekundenThird, the phase angles must match. At the moment we close the breaker, each of our three waves has to be at the same
1:40:401 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 40 Sekundenpoint in its rise and fall as the corresponding wave on the grid. Peak
1:40:461 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 46 Sekundenwith peak, zero with zero. Fourth, the frequencies must match or nearly. And
1:40:541 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 54 Sekundenhere there's a subtle twist. Engineers usually bring the incoming generator up to a speed just a hair faster than the
1:41:021 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 2 Sekundengrid. There's a good reason for that. If our generator is running slightly fast when we connect, then the grid will
1:41:101 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 10 Sekundengently hold it back and it will immediately start pushing a little power into the grid, acting as a generator
1:41:181 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 18 Sekundenfrom its very first moment. If it were running slightly slow, the grid would pull it along and for a moment, our
1:41:271 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 27 Sekundengenerator would draw power from the grid and act as a motor.
1:41:321 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 32 SekundenYou don't want your brand new generator starting its working life by being dragged.
1:41:391 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 39 SekundenHow do you actually see whether the waves line up? The oldest method is beautifully simple. You connect three
1:41:471 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 47 Sekundenlight bulbs across the open breaker. One bulb for each phase between our generator's side and the grid side. When
1:41:561 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 56 Sekundenour wave and the grid's wave are perfectly lined up, there's no difference between them. So, no voltage
1:42:041 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 4 Sekundenappears across the bulb and it stays dark. When they're completely opposite, the difference is as big as it can be
1:42:121 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 12 Sekundenand the bulb glows brightly. Since our generator is running at almost but not exactly the grid's frequency, our waves
1:42:221 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 22 Sekundenslowly drift in and out of alignment with the grid's waves.
1:42:271 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 27 SekundenSo the three bulbs slowly brighten and dim, brighten [clears throat] and dim.
1:42:331 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 33 SekundenIf all three brighten and dim together, the phase sequence is correct.
1:42:401 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 40 SekundenIf they brighten one after another in a circle, the sequence is wrong and two wires need to be swapped.
1:42:491 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 49 SekundenAnd the moment to close the breaker is right in the middle of the dark when all three bulbs have gone out together. And
1:42:581 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 58 Sekundenour waves and the grid's waves are riding along side by side.
1:43:041 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 4 SekundenLarger plants use an instrument called a synchroscope.
1:43:081 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 8 SekundenIt has a dial with a single pointer that rotates.
1:43:131 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 13 SekundenIf our generator is running a little faster than the grid, the pointer turns slowly clockwise, slower, and it turns counterclockwise.
1:43:241 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe speed of its rotation shows how different the frequencies are, and its position shows how far apart the waves
1:43:331 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 33 Sekundenare right now. The operator adjusts the engine or turbine. So, the pointer drifts slowly clockwise and closes the
1:43:411 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 41 Sekundenbreaker just as it passes straight up at 12:00.
1:43:461 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 46 SekundenToday, most of this is done by automatic synchronizers that measure everything electronically and close the breaker at
1:43:531 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 53 Sekundenexactly the right instant, but the principle is the same as the three bulbs. It's worth picturing an operator
1:44:011 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 1 Sekundedoing this by hand in a big plant decades ago. Behind the control panel, a turbine the size of a bus is spinning
1:44:091 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 9 Sekundenup, the building humming with it. The operator watches the synchroscope's pointer creep around the dial, one slow
1:44:171 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 17 Sekundenrevolution after another, and gently trims the turbine speed until the pointer barely crawls.
1:44:251 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 25 SekundenA hand rests on the breaker switch. The pointer approaches the top of the dial.
1:44:311 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 31 SekundenThere's a short pause. The switch is turned. A heavy clunk sounds somewhere in the switch gear and the needles on the panel settle.
1:44:411 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 41 SekundenThat machine is now part of something vastly larger than itself. What happens if you close the breaker at the wrong
1:44:491 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 49 Sekundenmoment? Then the two sets of waves are fighting each other. Enormous currents flash through the stator coils. The
1:44:581 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 58 Sekundenstator's magnetic field and the rotors field lurch against each other and the whole machine receives a violent twist
1:45:061 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 6 Sekundenas if a giant had grabbed the shaft and yanked it. In a big generator, a badly timed connection can damage the
1:45:141 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 14 Sekundenwindings, the shaft, the couplings, and the engine or turbine behind them. It's the electrical version of dropping a car's clutch at the wrong speed.
1:45:271 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 27 SekundenNow suppose we do it right. The bulbs go dark. We close the breaker and nothing
1:45:341 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 34 Sekundendramatic happens at all. Our generator is now part of the grid. And from this moment its world is completely
1:45:421 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 42 Sekundendifferent. When our generator was running alone, if we opened the throttle on its engine, it sped up and its
1:45:501 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 50 Sekundenfrequency rose. If we turned up its field current, its voltage rose. It was
1:45:571 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 57 Sekundenthe master of its own little system. Now it's connected to a grid that includes thousands of other generators.
1:46:041 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 4 SekundenChapman describes a grid like this as an infinite bus, meaning a system so large
1:46:111 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthat no single machine can change its voltage or frequency in any noticeable way. Our generator is one voice in an
1:46:211 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 21 Sekundenenormous choir and it can't change the key. So what happens now if we open the throttle? Our engine pushes harder on
1:46:301 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 30 Sekundenthe shaft, but the shaft can't speed up because it's magnetically locked to the grid's frequency. The extra push goes
1:46:391 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 39 Sekundeninto opening up the torque angle, stretching that magnetic spring between the rotor and the status field. The
1:46:471 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 47 Sekundenrotor edges a little further ahead and our generator delivers more power into the grid. From now on, the throttle
1:46:561 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 56 Sekundendecides how much of the load our machine carries, while the speed stays locked to everyone else's.
1:47:041 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 4 SekundenThat magnetic pull, the same one that tugs back on a loaded generator, is the invisible thread that ties every
1:47:121 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 12 Sekundengenerator on the grid together. Each rotor is held to the grid's rotating field by that magnetic pull. If a rotor
1:47:211 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 21 Sekundentries to fall behind, the pull drags it forward. If it tries to race ahead, the pull holds it back.
1:47:301 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 30 SekundenEvery generator connected to the grid is spinning in exact step with every other one.
1:47:361 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 36 SekundenA steam turbine in one state, a hydroelect electric generator at a dam hundreds of miles away, and a gas
1:47:441 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 44 Sekundenturbine near the coast are all turning in lock step. Their rotors locked in place relative to each other as though
1:47:511 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 51 Sekundenthey were mounted on one immensely long, invisible shaft.
1:47:571 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 57 SekundenOne detail keeps that image honest. The machines aren't all turning at the same number of revolutions per minute. A slow
1:48:051 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 5 Sekundenhydroelect electric generator with dozens of poles might turn a couple of hundred times a minute, while a two-
1:48:121 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 12 Sekundenpole steam turbine generator turns 3,600 times.
1:48:181 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 18 SekundenWhat they share is the electrical rhythm. Each one produces exactly 60 waves per second, and each one's
1:48:261 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 26 Sekundenmagnetic poles hold a fixed position relative to the grid's waves. In that electrical sense, they move as one.
1:48:361 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 36 SekundenThat's what it means to say the grid is one machine. In North America, the main eastern and western grids and the grid
1:48:431 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 43 Sekundenof Texas are each one synchronized system across a whole region. Every generator is holding hands. What about
1:48:531 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 53 Sekundenthe field current? If we turn up our generator's magnetic dial after it's connected, we can't raise the grid's
1:49:011 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 1 Sekundevoltage either. What changes instead is something called reactive power. That's a part of the current that sloshes back
1:49:091 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 9 Sekundenand forth without doing useful work at the far end. But it's needed to build up the magnetic fields inside every motor and transformer on the system.
1:49:201 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 20 SekundenGenerators with stronger fields supply more of it and that helps hold up the voltage across the network. Operators
1:49:281 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 28 Sekundenadjust the fields of the big machines to keep voltages healthy throughout the grid. Now we can answer a question that might have been bothering you. If
1:49:361 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthousands of generators all share one frequency and none of them can change it, how does the grid decide which
1:49:441 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 44 Sekundengenerator carries how much load? And what happens when you switch on your kettle? The answer is a clever piece of
1:49:521 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 52 Sekundenengineering called droop. The governor on each generator is set so that it doesn't hold its speed perfectly
1:49:591 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 59 Sekundenconstant. Instead, it's designed to let the speed fall slightly as the load increases, typically by a few% from no
1:50:081 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 8 Sekundenload to full load. That sounds like a flaw, but it's what makes sharing possible. Follow it step by step. You
1:50:161 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 16 Sekundenswitch on your kettle. A typical electric kettle draws about 1,500 W. A single large power plant generator can
1:50:251 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 25 Sekundenproduce a,000 megawatt or more, which is more than 600,000 times as much. So your kettle's pull spread across every
1:50:341 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 34 Sekundengenerator on the grid is almost unimaginably small for each one of them.
1:50:401 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 40 SekundenBut it's real and it's felt. That tiny extra load is felt instantly by every generator on the grid all at once.
1:50:521 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 52 SekundenBecause they're all connected, each rotor gets a little more drag. The whole grid's spinning mass slows down by an
1:51:011 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 1 Sekundeamount far too small to notice. And so the frequency dips by a tiny fraction of a hertz.
1:51:091 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 9 SekundenEvery governor on the system sees that same tiny dip in frequency and each one responds by adding a little more energy in proportion to its size.
1:51:211 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 21 SekundenThe big plants add a bit more, the small ones a bit less.
1:51:271 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 27 SekundenThe load is shared automatically without anyone having to send a single message. The frequency itself is the
1:51:361 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 36 Sekundenmessage. That's why operators watch the grid's frequency so closely. It's the heartbeat of the whole machine. If
1:51:451 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 45 Sekundendemand runs ahead of supply, the frequency falls. If supply runs ahead of
1:51:511 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 51 Sekundendemand, it rises. Operators and automatic controls then adjust the plants to nudge it back to 60 all day and all night.
1:52:031 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 3 SekundenFor much of the 20th century, that heartbeat was steady enough to keep time. Many electric clocks, the kind on
1:52:111 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 11 Sekundenkitchen walls and on the fronts of old ovens, had no timekeeping mechanism of their own. Inside was a small motor that turned in step with the grid's waves.
1:52:211 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 21 SekundenSo, the clock was literally counting cycles, 60 to the second. When the grid ran slightly slow for a few hours on a
1:52:291 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 29 Sekundenday of heavy demand, those clocks fell a few seconds behind. So operators would later run the grid just a tiny bit fast
1:52:371 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 37 Sekundenon purpose until the clocks had caught up again. Millions of people told time by a machine they never saw and the
1:52:451 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 45 Sekundenpower companies kept that machine honest down to the second. Wind turbines have to join this choir too and they face a
1:52:531 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 53 Sekundenspecial difficulty. The wind doesn't care about 60 hertz. It gusts and fades and the blades speed up and slow down
1:53:011 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 1 Sekundewith it. A generator whose rotor was simply locked to the grid would fight the wind at every gust. So the most
1:53:081 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 8 Sekundencommon design for large wind turbines uses a remarkable trick called a doubly fed induction generator. In this
1:53:161 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 16 Sekundenmachine, the rotor has its own three-phase coils fed through slip rings by an electronic controller. By feeding
1:53:241 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 24 Sekundenthe rotor coils with alternating current of a chosen frequency, the controller makes the rotor's magnetic field spin around the rotor itself, forward or backward, at whatever speed it chooses.
1:53:361 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 36 SekundenThe magnetic field that the stator actually sees is the combination of two motions. The rotor's physical spin plus
1:53:441 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 44 Sekundenthe field spin around the rotor. Let's use real numbers. For a four- pole machine, 1,800 revolutions per minute
1:53:521 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 52 Sekundencorresponds exactly to 60 hertz. Suppose a lull in the wind slows the rotor to 1600 revolutions per minute. On its own,
1:54:021 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 2 Sekundenthat would correspond to only about 53 and a3 hertz. So, the controller feeds the rotor coils with about 6 and 2/3
1:54:101 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 10 Sekundenhertz, spinning the rotor's field forward to make up the difference.
1:54:151 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 15 Sekunden53 and a3 + 6 and 2/3 is 60 and the stator delivers exactly 60 hertz to the
1:54:221 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 22 Sekundengrid. Now suppose a gust pushes the rotor up to 2,000 revolutions per minute which would correspond to about 66 and
1:54:311 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 31 Sekunden2/3 hertz. Too fast. So, the controller reverses the order of the phases and
1:54:381 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 38 Sekundenspins the rotors field backward at 6 and 2/3 hertz, subtracting the excess.
1:54:451 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe stator still sees exactly 60. And if the rotor happens to be spinning at exactly 1,800 revolutions per minute,
1:54:541 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthe controller feeds it plain direct current 0 herz and the machine behaves like an ordinary generator. The rest of
1:55:031 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 3 Sekundenthe turbine is working just as hard to stay in its lane. Experiments with small model turbines show why the angle of the
1:55:111 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 11 Sekundenblades matters so much. Turn the blades to face the wind squarely and they catch its full force but produce no lift at
1:55:201 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 20 Sekundenall. The rotor doesn't turn. No voltage appears and all that force simply shves against the tower.
1:55:311 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 31 SekundenTurn them edge on parallel to the wind and they slip through the air turning slowly and producing only a trickle of voltage.
1:55:421 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 42 SekundenSomewhere in between is the angle where lift is greatest and the hub spins fast
1:55:501 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 50 Sekundenand designers find it on a chart for each blade shape. The generator sets an upper limit too.
1:55:591 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 59 Sekundenspin one too fast for too long and it overheats and can destroy itself.
1:56:061 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 6 SekundenSo a turbine rated at say 2 megawatt tilts its blades to keep from pushing past that rating.
1:56:151 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 15 SekundenSo the blades can rotate on their mounts, tilting to catch more wind or spill it, which controls how fast they
1:56:241 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 24 Sekundenturn and keeps the generator below its rated power.
1:56:291 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 29 SekundenIf the wind blows dangerously hard above what's called the cutout speed, the
1:56:361 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 36 Sekundenblades feather themselves edge on and brakes hold the rotor still. On top of the housing, a wind vein reads the
1:56:441 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 44 Sekundenwind's direction, and motors turn the whole head of the turbine to face it. A small counter tracks how many times the
1:56:521 Stunde, 56 Minuten, 52 Sekundenhead has turned because the power cables run down through the tower. And if the head kept turning the same way, those cables would twist until they snapped.
1:57:021 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 2 SekundenSo every so often, the turbine unwinds itself. Small turbines solve the steering problem more simply with a tail
1:57:101 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 10 Sekundenfin that points them into the wind like a weather vein. Large ones can't because a fin big enough to steer them would be
1:57:171 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 17 Sekundenenormous and heavy, and it would swing around in gusty wind, straining the bearings, the blades, and the whole structure.
1:57:251 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 25 SekundenAll of that effort, the tilting blades, the turning head, the electronic juggling of frequencies, has one
1:57:311 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 31 Sekundenpurpose, to keep this one turbine in perfect step with every other generator on the grid. And that's the great quiet achievement of the power system.
1:57:431 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 43 SekundenThousands of machines of every type and size, all turning in step, holding each other up, sharing every change in load
1:57:511 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 51 Sekundenby feeling it through the frequency they have in common. But a spring can only stretch so far.
1:57:571 Stunde, 57 Minuten, 57 SekundenWe've seen that if the torque angle opens too wide, the magnetic pull reaches its limit and a rotor slips. And we saw in the Texas story at the very
1:58:061 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 6 Sekundenbeginning that when supply falls short of demand, the whole grid slows down together. What happens when one of those
1:58:131 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 13 Sekundenlimits is reached not in one machine but across a whole region in a matter of minutes?
1:58:201 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 20 SekundenNovember 9th, 1965 was a cold Tuesday in the northeastern United States and Ontario. As the evening rush hour began,
1:58:291 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 29 Sekundenpeople were coming home, switching on lights, turning up heat, and starting dinner. The interconnected power system
1:58:361 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 36 Sekundenof the region, which by then linked utilities across several states and southern Canada, was running close to its limit. Near Niagara Falls on the
1:58:451 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 45 SekundenCanadian side stood a large hydroelect electric plant called Sir Adam Beck station number two.
1:58:531 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 53 SekundenSeveral transmission lines carried its power north toward Toronto.
1:58:581 Stunde, 58 Minuten, 58 SekundenEach of those lines was guarded by a protective relay, an automatic switch designed to disconnect the line if the
1:59:071 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 7 Sekundencurrent through it became dangerously high. the way a circuit breaker protects the wiring in your house.
1:59:151 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 15 SekundenA few days earlier, one of those relays had been set to trip at a level that was too low. It was a small error in a setting, a number on a dial.
1:59:271 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 27 SekundenNobody noticed because on ordinary days, the current through that line never reached it. At 16 minutes 5 that
1:59:371 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 37 Sekundenevening, a small brief surge of power arrived from a generating plant across the river in New York.
1:59:451 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 45 SekundenIt was the kind of minor fluctuation that happens all the time.
1:59:511 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 51 SekundenBut it pushed the current on that one line just past the relay's mistaken setting.
1:59:571 Stunde, 59 Minuten, 57 SekundenThe relay did exactly what it had been told to do. It opened and the line went dead.
2:00:042 Stunden, 4 SekundenThe power that had been flowing on that line instantly shifted onto the other lines heading north. And those lines were already heavily loaded.
2:00:142 Stunden, 14 SekundenNow they were overloaded and their own relays correctly set tripped them one after another.
2:00:222 Stunden, 22 SekundenIn a matter of seconds, the Beck station was cut off from the Toronto area entirely.
2:00:302 Stunden, 30 SekundenA mistake of a few notches on a dial made days earlier by someone doing routine maintenance had just opened the first gap in the machine.
2:00:402 Stunden, 40 SekundenNow think about what that meant for the machines using everything we've learned tonight.
2:00:462 Stunden, 46 SekundenThe generators at Beck were still spinning, still being pushed by falling water, but the load they had been feeding in Ontario was suddenly gone.
2:00:572 Stunden, 57 SekundenWith no electrical drag holding them back, their rotors began to speed up.
2:01:032 Stunden, 1 Minute, 3 SekundenTheir power, with nowhere else to go, surged south into the network in New York. And that sudden flood overloaded
2:01:112 Stunden, 1 Minute, 11 Sekundenmore lines which tripped. Meanwhile, in the regions that had been depending on that power, the opposite was happening.
2:01:192 Stunden, 1 Minute, 19 SekundenLoad was still there, lights still on, motors still turning, but the supply had vanished.
2:01:262 Stunden, 1 Minute, 26 SekundenThe generators in those regions felt the extra drag all at once and their rotors began to slow. So in different parts of
2:01:342 Stunden, 1 Minute, 34 Sekundenthe same interconnected system, some machines were racing and others were dragging.
2:01:402 Stunden, 1 Minute, 40 SekundenMachines in different areas began to drift out of step with one another.
2:01:452 Stunden, 1 Minute, 45 SekundenProtective systems sensing speeds, currents, and frequencies far outside limits did what they were designed to
2:01:522 Stunden, 1 Minute, 52 Sekundendo. They disconnected the generators to protect them from damage. And every generator that dropped off put even more strain on the ones that remained.
2:02:032 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 3 SekundenWithin about 10 minutes, the lights were out across roughly 80,000 square miles.
2:02:092 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 9 SekundenAround 30 million people were in the dark. In New York City, hundreds of thousands of commuters were trapped in
2:02:162 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 16 Sekundensubway trains stuck in tunnels. In some places, power stayed off for up to 13 hours.
2:02:242 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 24 SekundenIt's worth noticing that every device in this story did what it was designed to do.
2:02:312 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe relay opened because its setting told it to. The other lines relays opened because they really were overloaded.
2:02:412 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe generators disconnected because their protection saw real danger.
2:02:472 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 47 SekundenEach piece acted sensibly on its own and together they took down a region. The
2:02:542 Stunden, 2 Minuten, 54 Sekundenlesson was hard. Linking systems made them stronger in most situations, but it also made one machine large enough for a
2:03:042 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 4 Sekundensingle small mistake to travel through it from relay to relay like a falling row of dominoes.
2:03:122 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 12 SekundenIn the years that followed, utilities formed organizations to coordinate reliability across whole regions, and
2:03:212 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 21 Sekundenthey rethought how protective relays should work together.
2:03:262 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 26 Sekunden38 years later, it happened again on an even larger scale.
2:03:332 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 33 SekundenAugust 14th, 2003 was a hot afternoon in northern Ohio.
2:03:392 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 39 SekundenAir conditioners were running hard. When current flows through a power line, the line heats up and hot metal expands.
2:03:492 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 49 SekundenOn a hot day, carrying a heavy load, a long transmission line gets longer, and it sags lower between its towers.
2:03:592 Stunden, 3 Minuten, 59 SekundenThat afternoon, one heavily loaded line after another sagged down into trees that had grown too tall beneath them.
2:04:082 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 8 SekundenEach time a line touched a tree, it shorted out and its protection disconnected it. Normally, operators
2:04:162 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 16 Sekundenwould have seen these failures on their screens and acted before the next line overloaded, but the alarm system at the utilities
2:04:252 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 25 Sekundencontrol center had failed silently earlier that afternoon.
2:04:312 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe screens looked calm. The operators didn't know that [clears throat] their system was coming apart around them. As
2:04:382 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 38 Sekundenmore lines dropped out, the power that had been flowing on them shifted to the remaining lines, which overloaded in
2:04:452 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 45 Sekundenturn. At about 10 minutes past 4 in the afternoon, the process accelerated into
2:04:522 Stunden, 4 Minuten, 52 Sekundena full cascade. In just a few minutes, more than 250 power plants disconnected from the grid. About 50 million people
2:05:002 Stunden, 5 Minutenlost power across eight states in the province of Ontario. Some of them waited days for it to come back. And then of
2:05:082 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 8 Sekundencourse there was the night in Texas. The story we began with in February of 2021.
2:05:142 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 14 SekundenA severe winter storm swept across the state. Temperatures fell into the single digits in Austin and below zero around
2:05:222 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 22 SekundenDallas. People turned up their heat and demand for electricity soared. At the same time, power plants of nearly every
2:05:302 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 30 Sekundenkind began failing in the cold. Natural gas supplies froze. equipment at coal plants, a nuclear plant, and wind farms
2:05:392 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 39 Sekundeniced up. By 1:23 in the morning on February 15th, about a third of the state's generating capacity had dropped
2:05:462 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 46 Sekundenoff. Texas runs its own grid, one synchronized machine covering most of the state, only lightly connected to the
2:05:542 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 54 Sekundengrids around it. That meant it had very little help to draw on from neighbors.
2:05:592 Stunden, 5 Minuten, 59 SekundenWith everything we've covered, the inside of that machine is easy to imagine. Demand was running ahead of supply. The electrical drag on every
2:06:082 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 8 Sekundenrotor in the state was greater than the push from the engines and turbines behind them. [clears throat] And so every generator in Texas began to slow
2:06:152 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 15 Sekundendown together because they were all locked together. The frequency, the heartbeat of the grid began to fall. At
2:06:252 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 25 Sekunden151 in the morning, it dropped below 59.4 hertz. That doesn't sound like much of a change from 60, but it's a critical
2:06:342 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 34 Sekundenthreshold. Big generators are designed to run within a narrow band of speeds.
2:06:392 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 39 SekundenRun them too slowly for too long and their turbine blades can begin to vibrate in harmful ways and parts of the machinery can overheat. So, they're
2:06:482 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 48 Sekundenprotected. Under the rules that Texas followed, if the frequency stayed below
2:06:542 Stunden, 6 Minuten, 54 Sekunden59.4 four for more than 9 minutes, generators would begin to disconnect automatically to save themselves.
2:07:042 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 4 SekundenAnd that's where the real danger lay.
2:07:072 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 7 SekundenEvery generator that disconnected would remove more supply, which would make the imbalance worse, which would slow the
2:07:152 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 15 Sekundenremaining generators further, which would trip more of them. The grid would unravel from the inside, one machine
2:07:232 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 23 Sekundenprotecting itself after another until nothing was left spinning at all. The operators had one tool that could work
2:07:312 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 31 Sekundenquickly enough. They could reduce demand. They ordered utilities to cut power to customers in blocks across the
2:07:392 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 39 Sekundenstate. Thousands of megawatts of load were disconnected, then thousands more.
2:07:462 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 46 SekundenAt 155, the frequency reached its lowest point, about 59.3
2:07:532 Stunden, 7 Minuten, 53 Sekundenhertz. The operators kept shedding load, 10,500 megawatt in total, enough to power
2:08:002 Stunden, 8 Minutenmillions of homes. Slowly, the drag on the rotors eased. The machines stopped slowing down.
2:08:102 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 10 SekundenAfter 4 minutes and 23 seconds below the threshold, the frequency began to climb again. By about 2:00 in the morning, it
2:08:182 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 18 Sekundenwas back to 60. If it had stayed down for another 4 minutes and 37 seconds, the officials said later, the grid could
2:08:262 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 26 Sekundenhave collapsed entirely, and restoring it might have taken weeks or longer.
2:08:332 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 33 SekundenIt's important to be honest about what that rescue cost. The grid survived, but it survived by cutting millions of
2:08:402 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 40 Sekundenpeople off in the middle of a brutal cold snap. and many of them stayed without power for days. People died in
2:08:482 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 48 Sekundenthe cold. The machine was saved by sacrificing much of what had existed to serve. And that was a tragedy of its own.
2:08:592 Stunden, 8 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe physics is the same in all three stories. Nothing in Texas exploded, and no machine broke all at once. The grid
2:09:082 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 8 Sekundencame close to collapse because the balance between what was going in and what was coming out tipped too far and
2:09:152 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 15 Sekundenthe frequency that shared heartbeat showed it. Past a certain point, the machines stop helping each other and start dropping away to save themselves.
2:09:252 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 25 SekundenNow consider the outcome Texas narrowly avoided with every generator stopped and every line dead. That raises an
2:09:332 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 33 Sekundenunsettling problem. A large power plant needs electricity to run. Its pumps, its fans, its fuel handling, its control
2:09:412 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 41 Sekundensystems, and the field coils on its own rotors all need power. In a total blackout, the plant can't get that power from the grid because there is no grid.
2:09:522 Stunden, 9 Minuten, 52 SekundenIt's the chicken and egg riddle of the self-starting dynamo grown to the size of a whole state. So if the entire
2:10:002 Stunden, 10 Minutenmachine goes dark, where does the first spark come from? Engineers have a name for the process of restarting a power
2:10:072 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 7 Sekundensystem from nothing. They call it a black start. And the answer to the riddle turns out to be the same one we
2:10:142 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 14 Sekundenfound for a single dynamo. You start small and you let the system build on itself. A few power plants on every grid
2:10:232 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 23 Sekundenare specially chosen and equipped to start without any help from the outside.
2:10:282 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 28 SekundenThey're called black start units. Often they're hydroelect electric plants because all you need to do is open a
2:10:352 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 35 Sekundengate and let the water fall through the turbine. Others are gas turbines or diesel generators which can be cranked
2:10:432 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 43 Sekundenby their own batteries. Each of these machines has a way to wake up on its own and a way to excite its own field from a
2:10:522 Stunden, 10 Minuten, 52 Sekundenbattery bank, from a small permanent magnet exciter, or from the faint residual magnetism in its own iron. Once
2:11:002 Stunden, 11 Minutena black start unit is running and stable, it sends power out along a carefully chosen transmission line to a
2:11:072 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 7 Sekundenlarger power plant nearby. That power runs the big plant's pumps and fans and controls so that it can start its own generators.
2:11:182 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 18 SekundenNow there are two plants running.
2:11:202 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 20 SekundenTogether they can power a little more of the network. Operators then reconnect customers bit by bit in small blocks. It
2:11:292 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 29 Sekundenhas to be done slowly and carefully. And you already know why. Each block of load that gets switched on is a sudden new
2:11:362 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 36 Sekundendrag on the few rotors that are spinning. take on too much at once and the frequency of that small fragile
2:11:432 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 43 Sekundensystem plunges and the protection trips it all off again. So the operators feed the load in one neighborhood at a time
2:11:522 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 52 Sekundenletting the machines catch their breath between each step. Separate regions are brought back as separate islands each
2:11:592 Stunden, 11 Minuten, 59 Sekundenwith its own spinning generators and eventually the islands have to be joined back together into one machine. How do
2:12:072 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 7 Sekundenyou do that? Exactly the way a single generator joins the grid. You match the voltages, check the phase sequence,
2:12:152 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 15 Sekundenadjust the speeds until the waves drift slowly into alignment, and close the breaker when the synchroscope's pointer
2:12:222 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 22 Sekundenpasses 12:00. The same rules that apply to one generator joining the grid apply to an entire island of generators joining another.
2:12:332 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 33 SekundenThroughout the process, every step follows the same patient logic. The operators keep an eye on the frequency
2:12:422 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 42 Sekundenof each small island the way a nurse watches a pulse. If it sags, they wait
2:12:492 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 49 Sekundenand let the governors catch up before adding the next block of customers.
2:12:552 Stunden, 12 Minuten, 55 SekundenIf voltage drifts on a long lightly loaded line, they adjust the fields of the running generators to bring it back.
2:13:042 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 4 SekundenOperators generally try to restore critical services like hospitals and water systems as early as they can.
2:13:132 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 13 SekundenWhole cities can take hours to restore and a larger blackout can take days.
2:13:202 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 20 SekundenBecause this careful rebuilding can't be rushed without risking a second collapse.
2:13:272 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 27 SekundenSo the whole enormous machine in the end is restarted from a handful of small dependable machines that can start
2:13:362 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthemselves. And one of the most common of those small dependable machines is a
2:13:422 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 42 Sekundentype you may walk past every day without noticing. the diesel standby generator.
2:13:502 Stunden, 13 Minuten, 50 SekundenThey sit outside hospitals, data centers, water treatment plants, office towers, and airports waiting.
2:14:002 Stunden, 14 MinutenLet's walk around one the way a technician would because it pulls together nearly everything we've talked about tonight.
2:14:092 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 9 SekundenWe'll take the 500 kov amp machine we met earlier.
2:14:142 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 14 SekundenIt's housed in a steel enclosure called a canopy.
2:14:182 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 18 SekundenInside there are two main parts coupled together.
2:14:232 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 23 SekundenOn one side is the diesel engine producing mechanical power at its flywheel.
2:14:302 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 30 SekundenOn the other side is the alternator which converts that mechanical power into electricity.
2:14:372 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 37 SekundenThe engine's flywheel is bolted to the alternator's coupling plate. So when the engine turns, the rotor turns with it.
2:14:472 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 47 SekundenOn the front of the canopy is a control panel and at its heart is a small computer called an automatic mains
2:14:562 Stunden, 14 Minuten, 56 Sekundenfailure controller. Mains is another word for the utility grid. This controller watches the grid's power day
2:15:032 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 3 Sekundenand night. On its screen, little symbols show the state of things. a transmission tower for the grid, a wave symbol for
2:15:112 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthe generator, and indicator lights for whether the grid is live, whether the generator is running, and which one is
2:15:182 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 18 Sekundenfeeding the load. Now, suppose the grid fails. The controller senses the loss of power and it acts on its own. First, it
2:15:282 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 28 Sekundensends power from the starting batteries to the starter motor. Those are the two 12vt batteries we met before, wired in
2:15:352 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 35 Sekundenseries to make 24 volts. The engine's frame serves as the negative side of that circuit connected by a common
2:15:432 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 43 Sekundencable. The starter motor's gear meshes with the flywheel and cranks the engine.
2:15:492 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 49 SekundenAs the engine turns, it starts to breathe. Fresh air is drawn in through a filter, which catches the dust. The
2:15:582 Stunden, 15 Minuten, 58 Sekundenclean air flows into a turbocharger, a clever device with two fan wheels on a single shaft. One wheel sits in the
2:16:052 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 5 Sekundenstream of hot exhaust leaving the engine, and the exhaust spins it. The other wheel on the same shaft sits in
2:16:132 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 13 Sekundenthe stream of fresh incoming air, and it compresses that air, packing more of it into the engine. A partition keeps the exhaust and the fresh air from mixing.
2:16:232 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 23 SekundenThe turbocharger's shaft spins so fast that it needs a steady flow of oil from the engine to keep it cool. Squeezing air heats it up and hot air is thin air.
2:16:342 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 34 SekundenSo, the compressed air next passes through an aftercooler which looks like a radiator but carries air instead of water. Cooled and dense, the air enters
2:16:442 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 44 Sekundenthe cylinders. Fuel is injected and the engine fires. It catches and runs up to speed and its governor holds it there.
2:16:532 Stunden, 16 Minuten, 53 SekundenMeanwhile, inside the alternator, the story of the self-exiting dynamo is playing out in a few seconds. The rotor
2:17:002 Stunden, 17 Minutenstarts spinning. Its exciter, whether through a permanent magnet pilot exciter or the residual magnetism in its iron,
2:17:082 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 8 Sekundenprovides the first seed of a field. The voltage builds. The automatic voltage regulator takes charge and raises the
2:17:162 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 16 Sekundenfield until the output reaches its target. Then starts making its small steady corrections.
2:17:242 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe controller checks everything, the voltage, the frequency, the oil pressure, the coolant temperature. When
2:17:322 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 32 Sekundenall of it is healthy, it signals the transfer switch to move the building's load from the dead grid to the generator.
2:17:402 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 40 SekundenThat transfer switch is usually a separate panel and it's what makes the whole system automatic.
2:17:462 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 46 SekundenWithout one, an operator would have to start the generator and switch over the load by hand. The power flows out
2:17:542 Stunden, 17 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthrough the main circuit breaker, a heavy switch sized to match the generator's current. It lets the operators connect or disconnect the load
2:18:022 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 2 Sekundenby hand. And it also trips automatically if there's a short circuit or a dangerous overload.
2:18:102 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 10 SekundenLook down near the base and you'll see the engine and alternator sitting on rubber or spring mounts called antiibration mounts. A running diesel
2:18:192 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 19 Sekundenengine shakes and those mounts soak up the vibration so it doesn't travel into the frame and the building.
2:18:282 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 28 SekundenFor the same reason, where the exhaust pipe leaves the engine and connects to the silencer, which is bolted rigidly to
2:18:352 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 35 Sekundenthe canopy, there's a short section of flexible ribbed metal pipe called a bellows.
2:18:432 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 43 SekundenThe engine can shake freely on its mounts and the bellows flexes along with it so the exhaust system doesn't crack.
2:18:532 Stunden, 18 Minuten, 53 SekundenAnd on the side of the engine, the little beltdriven charging alternator is already busy recharging the starting
2:19:012 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 1 Sekundebatteries. So, the machine will be ready to start again next time if the grid returns.
2:19:092 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe controller notices, waits to be sure the grid is stable, switches the load back, and lets the generator cool down
2:19:172 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 17 Sekundenfor a few minutes before shutting it off. Then it goes back to waiting and watching.
2:19:252 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 25 SekundenThere's even a test button. Press it and the controller pretends the grid has failed. So the operators can check that
2:19:322 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthe whole sequence works from batteries to starter to engine to exciter to
2:19:392 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 39 Sekundenregulator to transfer switch without waiting for a real emergency.
2:19:462 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 46 SekundenThat test button exists for a reason. A standby generator is a strange sort of machine. It might sit idle for months,
2:19:552 Stunden, 19 Minuten, 55 Sekundeneven years, and then be asked to work perfectly at the worst possible moment during a storm in the middle of the night when everything else has failed.
2:20:062 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 6 SekundenAnd in that long idle stretch, all kinds of things can quietly go wrong. Fuel can
2:20:132 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 13 Sekundengo stale, batteries can weaken, seals can leak. the residual magnetism in the
2:20:192 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 19 Sekundenrotor, the seed of the whole process, can fade and animals looking for a warm,
2:20:262 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 26 Sekundendry, sheltered place, can move in. Which brings us to the last story of the night. It's about one of these standby
2:20:342 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 34 Sekundengenerators, a smaller one that had been installed at a job site. One day, it stopped making power. The engine still
2:20:432 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 43 Sekundenstarted. It still ran, but no electricity came out. So, it sat unused until somebody decided to throw it away.
2:20:542 Stunden, 20 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd that's when it ended up for free in the hands of a man who likes to find out why things stop working.
2:21:032 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 3 SekundenThe man is a farmer and a tinkerer who runs a workshop on his property and fixes whatever breaks from tractors to excavators.
2:21:142 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 14 SekundenA friend of his, the same friend who had poured the concrete slab for his shop years earlier, had been working at a job
2:21:222 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 22 Sekundensite where a propane powered standby generator was installed.
2:21:272 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 27 SekundenThe owners said it had quit making power. The engine still ran, but no electricity came out. It had been
2:21:362 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 36 Sekundensitting idle ever since. They gave it away.
2:21:412 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe friend took it and brought it over to see if it could be saved.
2:21:452 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 45 SekundenIt's a midsized machine rated at 15 kW, enough to run a good part of a house.
2:21:542 Stunden, 21 Minuten, 54 SekundenIt's driven by a threecylinder engine built in Japan running on propane. And it arrived the way uninstalled machines
2:22:022 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 2 Sekundenusually arrive with every wire that had connected it to the building simply snipped off.
2:22:082 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 8 SekundenWhat follows is a kind of detective story. And the lovely thing about it is that each clue sends us back through one layer of the machine we've spent the night exploring.
2:22:182 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 18 SekundenHe works through the engine controls and the fuel first, then the sta, the rotor, and the exciter, and finally the
2:22:252 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 25 Sekundenregulator, the magnetism, and the load, which is everything we've talked about in the order a repair person actually
2:22:322 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 32 Sekundenmeets it. The first clue is that the engine won't even start. It cranks, but there's no spark at any of its three
2:22:392 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 39 Sekundenspark plugs. He clips on testers that light up when a spark passes, and they stay dark. A good mechanic starts with the simplest explanations.
2:22:502 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 50 SekundenOne of the most common reasons for no spark on a generator engine is a low oil sensor. If the oil level drops, the
2:22:582 Stunden, 22 Minuten, 58 Sekundenengine's protection kills the ignition so it can't destroy itself. That's the same kind of guardian we saw in the big diesel controller. He checks the oil.
2:23:082 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 8 SekundenIt's full and clean as if the engine had barely run. [clears throat] Coolant fine. Battery new and strong.
2:23:192 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 19 SekundenThe starter spins the engine eagerly.
2:23:222 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 22 SekundenSo he turns to the control panel. It has a switch with three positions marked for test, off, and standby.
2:23:322 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 32 Sekundenand a row of indicator lights for low oil, high temperature, and over crank, which means the engine was cranked too long without starting.
2:23:432 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 43 SekundenThere's also a small battery charger that normally keeps the starting battery topped up from the grid. His suspicion
2:23:512 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 51 Sekundenis that the control board having no power is holding the ignition in a kind of kill mode.
2:23:592 Stunden, 23 Minuten, 59 SekundenMany engines are designed that way on purpose. If the control system isn't powered and satisfied that everything is safe, the ignition stays off.
2:24:102 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 10 SekundenSo, he looks for where the control board gets its power and finds a terminal strip with numbered connections.
2:24:182 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 18 SekundenTerminal one is labeled start. Terminal 3 is labeled as the negative side of the
2:24:242 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 24 Sekunden12V supply, which is ground. Terminal 4 is labeled as the positive 12 V. Then
2:24:322 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthe machine plays its first trick on him. The wire going to terminal 4, the positive, is green. The wire going to
2:24:402 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 40 Sekundenterminal 3, the ground, is red. The colors are backward from any normal convention, and the colors even change
2:24:472 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 47 Sekundenpartway along the wires. His conclusion, spoken half to himself, is one every repair person eventually learns.
2:24:572 Stunden, 24 Minuten, 57 SekundenYou assume things are logical and that's your first mistake.
2:25:022 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 2 SekundenHe reabels the wires. So red means positive and green means ground, connects the control board to 12 V and
2:25:112 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 11 Sekundenswitches to test mode. A relay clicks inside the panel. The board is awake. He
2:25:182 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 18 Sekundencranks the engine and the spark testers flash. Spark. But the engine still won't
2:25:242 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 24 Sekundenfire. And the reason is almost funny. It has no fuel. A quick spray of starting fluid into the air intake and the engine
2:25:342 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 34 Sekundencoughs and runs. That's the first layer solved, the controls. Now for the
2:25:402 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 40 Sekundensecond, fuel. The engine runs on propane. And propane has a quirk that
2:25:472 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 47 Sekundentrips up a lot of people. It's stored as a liquid under pressure, but engines
2:25:532 Stunden, 25 Minuten, 53 Sekundenburn it as a gas, a vapor. The vapor comes from the liquid boiling off inside the tank. And when a liquid boils, it
2:26:022 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 2 Sekundenabsorbs heat. So, the tank gets colder as it supplies vapor. A small tank, like the kind you'd use for a backyard grill,
2:26:112 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 11 Sekundencan't produce vapor fast enough to feed an engine this size. It gets colder and colder, sometimes frosting over on the
2:26:192 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 19 Sekundenoutside, and the vapor slows to a trickle. The engine starves, so he connects it to a much larger tank, an old 120gal tank he'd set up in his shop.
2:26:322 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 32 SekundenBigger tanks have more liquid surface for the vapor to boil off from, which is also why you often see propane tanks
2:26:402 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 40 Sekundenlying on their sides. When he turns on the gas, he hears a rush of escaping propane from the generator's regulator.
2:26:482 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 48 SekundenHe shuts it off immediately. Then he rigs away to fill the fuel line with ordinary air with a pressure gauge in line so he can hunt for leaks safely.
2:26:592 Stunden, 26 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe main culprit turns out to be a fitting he'd assumed was just a swivel.
2:27:042 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 4 SekundenIt was actually a lefthand thread sealed with an O-ring, and it wasn't tight.
2:27:102 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 10 SekundenWith that fixed, a bit of soapy water brushed over every joint shows tiny bubbles only at the connections of his
2:27:172 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 17 Sekundenown test setup. Once those are tightened, the fuel system holds pressure. Now the engine starts on
2:27:242 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 24 Sekundenpropane and runs smoothly. Second layer solved. And now with a meter on the output comes the question that matters
2:27:332 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 33 Sekundenmost. Is this generator making any electricity? It is. But the numbers are wrong.
2:27:412 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 41 SekundenBetween the two output legs, where he should see around 240 volts, he reads about 70. From each leg to ground, where
2:27:502 Stunden, 27 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthere should be about 120 on each side, he reads 19 on one leg and 54 on the other. Low and lopsided.
2:28:012 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 1 SekundeSomething inside is unhappy. So, the detective moves into the machine itself, and he works exactly the way this chapter's first layers suggested.
2:28:122 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 12 SekundenRule out the big parts first. Starting with the sta, the stator's coils, remember, are long lengths of copper
2:28:202 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 20 Sekundenwire coated with a thin layer of enamel insulation wound around and around. The
2:28:272 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 27 Sekundenenamel keeps each turn from touching its neighbors.
2:28:322 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 32 SekundenWhen a stator fails, it's usually in one of two ways. Either a wire breaks somewhere, which cuts the circuit, or
2:28:402 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 40 Sekundenthe machine overheats, the enamel melts, and turns of wire short against each other or against the iron frame. A simple resistance meter can reveal both.
2:28:532 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 53 SekundenHe measures from the first output leg to neutral about 3 ohms. from the second
2:28:592 Stunden, 28 Minuten, 59 Sekundenleg to neutral about 3 ohms. The two halves of the winding match. That's good
2:29:062 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 6 Sekundennews. A broken wire would read as infinite resistance, an open circuit. A
2:29:132 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 13 Sekundenbadly shorted coil would read noticeably lower than its partner.
2:29:192 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 19 SekundenThen he checks from each leg to the metal case of the machine. There should be no connection at all because the copper should be completely insulated
2:29:272 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 27 Sekundenfrom the iron. At first, his meter shows a number in the millions of ohms that keeps climbing, which is just the meter
2:29:362 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 36 Sekundencharging up the natural capacitance of the long coils.
2:29:402 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 40 SekundenThen it settles on nothing, no connection.
2:29:442 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 44 SekundenHe also checks that separate windings have no connection to each other where there shouldn't be one. The sta is healthy.
2:29:522 Stunden, 29 Minuten, 52 SekundenThen his eye catches something else. One of the output wires has a spot where the insulation is gone and the bare copper
2:30:002 Stunden, 30 Minutenis showing, right where it could have touched the frame. Nearby, a tiny fuse holder looks scorched inside. He peers
2:30:092 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 9 Sekundenat it through a magnifying app on his phone because, as he says, he can't see like he used to. He tests the fuse.
2:30:172 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 17 SekundenInfinite resistance. It's blown. And when he digs further into the housing, he finds a small pile of acorn shells.
2:30:272 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 27 SekundenSomething has been living in this generator.
2:30:302 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 30 SekundenSomething with teeth, a chewed wire, and a blown fuse feel like a solved case.
2:30:362 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 36 SekundenBut he's too careful to stop there. He wants to rule out the rotor, too. This generator, he can tell at a glance, is
2:30:452 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 45 Sekundenthe brushless kind, the design with dodes riding on the shaft. On the end of the rotor, spinning with the shaft, is a
2:30:542 Stunden, 30 Minuten, 54 Sekundenring of small diodes. There are no brushes and no slip rings. The exciter passes its energy across the gap by
2:31:022 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 2 Sekundeninduction, and the rotating diodes turn it into direct current for the main field. A failed diode is a classic cause
2:31:112 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 11 Sekundenof a brushless generator losing its output. So, he checks each one with his meter's diode test. A healthy silicon
2:31:192 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 19 Sekundendiode shows a small consistent reading in one direction around half a volt on this kind of test and blocks current in the other. Every diode reads the same.
2:31:312 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 31 SekundenThen he measures the rotor windings. He doesn't know what the exact numbers should be because he doesn't have the manufacturer's specifications, but he finds something almost as useful.
2:31:432 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 43 SekundenEvery measurement between matching points is the same as its partners around 10 ohms across each of the outer connections.
2:31:532 Stunden, 31 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe rotor is symmetrical with no single coil behaving differently from the others. In a machine built from
2:32:002 Stunden, 32 Minutenidentical repeating parts, symmetry is a strong sign of health.
2:32:062 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 6 SekundenSo, he orders a replacement fuse, the right type, a slow blow fuse designed to tolerate brief surges without blowing.
2:32:152 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 15 SekundenIt takes a frustratingly long time to arrive. While he waits, he pulls apart an old portable generator of his own, a
2:32:232 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 23 Sekunden5,000W machine, and finds the other kind of design inside. A brushed generator with copper slip rings and carbon
2:32:322 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 32 Sekundenbrushes and no electronic voltage regulator at all. Just a capacitor and a few diodes doing a rough job of it. He runs it and the difference is obvious.
2:32:442 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 44 SekundenThe voltage and the frequency both wander up and down. Without a proper regulator making those constant
2:32:522 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 52 Sekundencorrections, the power is rough. What mechanics call dirty.
2:32:582 Stunden, 32 Minuten, 58 SekundenThe fuse arrives. He puts it in, reconnects everything, starts the engine, and watches.
2:33:062 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 6 SekundenThe new fuse blows.
2:33:092 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 9 SekundenNow the detective narrows the field. He disconnects everything except the bare essentials. The wires that carry power
2:33:172 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 17 Sekundento the rotor's field and the wires that let the regulator sense the output voltage. And he notices something about
2:33:242 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 24 Sekundenthis particular machine. It's wired with a universal voltage regulator, one that can be used on many different
2:33:322 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 32 Sekundengenerators. It has just four working connections. Two feed the field, two connect to the stator output, and those
2:33:402 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 40 Sekundendo double duty. They supply the regulator's own power and let it measure the voltage it's trying to control.
2:33:482 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 48 SekundenThere are two more wires unused that he believes belong to a pilot exciter. So in this setup, there's no permanent
2:33:562 Stunden, 33 Minuten, 56 Sekundenmagnet helping the machine wake up. It relies entirely on the residual magnetism in its own rotor to get started. the faint memory in the iron that Vley wrote about back in 1866.
2:34:092 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 9 SekundenHe mentions in passing that generators like this can lose that magnetism if they sit too long and need their field
2:34:182 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 18 Sekundenflashed with a battery. That wasn't the problem here, but it's exactly the kind of thing that can silently disable a standby machine while it waits.
2:34:302 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 30 SekundenHe puts in another new fuse and starts the engine. This time he sees it happen.
2:34:382 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 38 SekundenA spark flies out from the voltage regulator.
2:34:422 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 42 SekundenHe removes the regulator and looks closely.
2:34:462 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 46 SekundenThere on the circuit board next to one of its power transistors are the unmistakable marks of arcing.
2:34:552 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 55 SekundenNow the whole story comes together.
2:34:582 Stunden, 34 Minuten, 58 SekundenA mouse moved into the generator, drawn by the warmth and shelter. It chewed through the insulation on a wire. The bare wire shorted.
2:35:102 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 10 SekundenThat short blew the fuse and damaged the voltage regulator. The little electronic brain responsible for feeding the
2:35:182 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 18 Sekundenrotor's field. With a damaged regulator, the rotor's field was weak and erratic.
2:35:252 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 25 SekundenThe engine kept running perfectly. The shaft kept spinning. The stator was fine. The rotor was fine.
2:35:342 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 34 SekundenBut the magnet at the center of the machine could no longer be set to the right strength.
2:35:392 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 39 SekundenAnd so the generator made nothing useful at all. He orders a new regulator.
2:35:462 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 46 SekundenIt's a redesigned board with different heat sinks and different mounting holes, but the same connections.
2:35:542 Stunden, 35 Minuten, 54 SekundenField positive, field negative. and the sensing wires. He adapts the mounting
2:36:002 Stunden, 36 Minutenplate, replaces the old ring terminals with push-on connectors to fit the new board. And before doing any tidy
2:36:082 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 8 Sekundenfinishing work, he runs a test because there's no point perfecting the mounting if the fix doesn't work. He starts the
2:36:182 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 18 Sekundenengine and the voltage comes up clean and strong on both legs. It works. It works perfectly.
2:36:282 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 28 SekundenThen come the finishing touches, and they're exactly the adjustments we learned about. His local grid tends to
2:36:352 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 35 Sekundenrun a little high, around 250 volt. So, he turns the regulator's voltage setting
2:36:412 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 41 Sekundenup to match. He checks the frequency, 60 Hz. Then he adjusts the stability
2:36:482 Stunden, 36 Minuten, 48 Sekundensetting using an analog meter on the field wires, watching the needle's small flicker and turning the adjustment until the flicker is as small as it can get.
2:37:012 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 1 SekundeHe also realizes the regulator was sitting in a spot that blocked air flow through that compartment. So he cuts a
2:37:102 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 10 Sekundenclearing to let the air move and help the electronics stay cool. and he finds
2:37:162 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 16 Sekundenthe real culprit's front door, an opening in the housing just big enough for a mouse.
2:37:242 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 24 SekundenHis advice is to cover it with 1/4 in hardware cloth, a stiff wire mesh that keeps animals out, but still lets plenty
2:37:342 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 34 Sekundenof air through, unlike a fine window screen that would choke the airflow the machine needs.
2:37:412 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 41 SekundenFinally, the load test. He shuts off the grid pair to his whole shop and runs the
2:37:482 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 48 Sekundenshop entirely from the repaired generator. Then he starts piling on load. Two portable power stations
2:37:562 Stunden, 37 Minuten, 56 Sekundencharging at about 3,300 W together. Then banks of space heaters, 3,000 W here,
2:38:062 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 6 Sekundennearly 3,000 more there. The total climbs to about 11,000 W on a machine rated for 15,000.
2:38:172 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 17 SekundenThe generator takes it without a stumble. He notes that he'll want to fine-tune the engine speed and maybe
2:38:252 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 25 Sekundentrim the voltage once it's properly installed and serving its real load so it sits right at 60 hertz under typical conditions.
2:38:362 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 36 SekundenBut the verdict is clear. It's fixed. At the end, he offers a comment that fits
2:38:432 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 43 Sekundenthis whole night. After all that research and all those measurements, he jokes that generators are really simple.
2:38:532 Stunden, 38 Minuten, 53 SekundenAnd then admits the real lesson of the repair. Generators are complicated.
2:39:012 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 1 SekundeThere are many designs and a lot to know. He's right. And yet look back at
2:39:072 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 7 Sekundenwhat the machine was hiding. Beneath the tangle of wires, the backward colors, the leaking fitting, and the acorn
2:39:152 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 15 Sekundenshells, it was running on the same handful of ideas we started with tonight. A magnet which has to be made
2:39:222 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 22 Sekundenand controlled, coils of copper which have to stay whole and insulated, motion which has to be steady, and a push back
2:39:302 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 30 Sekundenfrom the load which the engine has to answer. All the mouse managed to break was the one small part that kept the magnet at the right strength, and that was enough to silence the whole machine.
2:39:422 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 42 SekundenSo, let's gather up the questions we started with and see how far we've come.
2:39:472 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 47 SekundenHow does a spinning shaft push electricity into your wall?
2:39:532 Stunden, 39 Minuten, 53 Sekundenthrough Faraday's discovery that a changing magnetic field pushes on the electrons in a wire. Spin a magnet past
2:40:002 Stunden, 40 Minutencoils of copper and the magnetism threading through those coils rises and falls and the electrons in the wire are pushed back and forth 60 times a second.
2:40:102 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 10 SekundenMore turns, a stronger field, a narrower air gap, and a faster change all make a bigger push. How does a generator with
2:40:182 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 18 Sekundenan electric magnet ever wake up from a memory? The iron at its heart keeps a faint trace of magnetism after it's been
2:40:262 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 26 Sekundenmagnetized. And that trace is enough to produce a trickle of current which strengthens the field which makes more current until the machine has built
2:40:352 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 35 Sekundenitself up to full strength. And when that memory fades, a moment's touch from a battery restores it just as Vley described back in 1866.
2:40:452 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 45 SekundenWhy does your kettle make a distant machine harder to turn? Because of Lens's rule. The current flowing out of a generator creates its own magnetic
2:40:542 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 54 Sekundenfield and that field always pulls back against the motion that created it.
2:40:592 Stunden, 40 Minuten, 59 SekundenEvery watt you use is paid for by a push on a shaft somewhere and the engine, the turbine or the falling water behind that
2:41:072 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 7 Sekundenshaft has to supply it. How do thousands of generators move as one? Each rotor is held to the grid by an invisible
2:41:162 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 16 Sekundenmagnetic spring, pulling it forward when it lags and holding it back when it races.
2:41:242 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 24 SekundenEvery governor watches the same shared frequency and answers every change in load together, each taking its
2:41:322 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 32 Sekundenproportion with no messages needed. The whole system turns like a single machine
2:41:392 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 39 Sekundenspread across a continent. What happens when that machine falls out of step?
2:41:452 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 45 SekundenWe've seen it. A relay set too low near Niagara. Lines sagging into trees on a hot Ohio afternoon.
2:41:552 Stunden, 41 Minuten, 55 SekundenA frozen night in Texas when every rotor in the state began to slow at once. The
2:42:012 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 1 Sekundesprings stretch. The machines start protecting themselves and one failure can pull down the next. And when
2:42:102 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 10 Sekundeneverything goes dark, the whole system has to be coaxed back to life from a few small machines that can start
2:42:182 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthemselves. The same way a single dynamo builds itself up from the ghost of its own magnetism.
2:42:262 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 26 SekundenAnd what keeps it all in step every second of every night? Look back over the stories and you'll notice that
2:42:332 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 33 Sekundennearly every one of them had people at its center. Edison refusing to run from his roaring dynamos. A relay set wrong
2:42:422 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 42 Sekundennear Niagara. An alarm that failed in Ohio. Operators shedding load at 2 in the morning in Texas. Physics provides
2:42:502 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthe springs. But people provide everything else. The engineer who set the governor's droop. The operator
2:42:582 Stunden, 42 Minuten, 58 Sekundenwatching the frequency on a screen at 3:00 in the morning. The technician who presses the test button on a standby generator once a month. The mechanic who
2:43:072 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 7 Sekundenmeasures a status resistance and then takes the time to look for acorns.
2:43:122 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 12 SekundenThe person who remembers to cover a hole with wire mesh. The giant machine holds together because thousands of people keep taking care of its smallest parts.
2:43:232 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 23 SekundenThink of the path we've traveled. From Urstead's twitching compass needle to Faraday's iron ring and his copper disc
2:43:302 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 30 Sekundento Pixie's hand crank. From Vi's patent, which came with instructions for reminding the iron how to be a magnet,
2:43:382 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 38 Sekundento the Graham machine that started spinning by itself at a fair in Vienna.
2:43:442 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 44 SekundenFrom Edison's circus of runaway dynamos on Pearl Street to the waterfalls at Niagara sending power to Buffalo.
2:43:532 Stunden, 43 Minuten, 53 SekundenFrom three light bulbs slowly going dark together in a control room to a wind
2:44:002 Stunden, 44 Minutenturbine quietly adding six and 2/3 hertz to its own rotor so that it can keep
2:44:072 Stunden, 44 Minuten, 7 Sekundentime with everyone else right now. As you listen to this, that machine is running somewhere far away.
2:44:172 Stunden, 44 Minuten, 17 SekundenWater is falling through a turbine.
