Introduction to Microbiology and Microscopy
0:00
Lesson one, introduction to microbiology and microscopy. Let us begin with the
0:05
definition of our subject. Microbiology is the scientific study of
0:11
microorganisms. Those living things that are too small to be seen with the naked eye. It is a
0:17
discipline that encompasses a vast diversity of life forms including bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa, and
0:26
microscopic algae. It may be difficult to comprehend, but the visible world,
0:31
the trees, the animals, the people you see everyday makes up only a tiny fraction of the biomass on Earth. The
0:39
true majority of life is microscopic. You are currently resting in a room that
0:44
is teeming with invisible life, floating in the air, resting on surfaces, and
0:49
thriving on your skin. Far from being something to fear, this is simply the
0:54
nature of our reality. We live in a microbial world. To study this invisible
1:00
universe, human beings had to invent a way to extend our senses. We had to
1:06
invent a second eye, one capable of peering into the very fabric of life.
1:11
This brings us to our primary tool, the microscope. Visualize a standard
1:16
compound light microscope. It is a sturdy instrument, usually made of metal, sitting on a laboratory bench. At
1:24
its base is a light source, a simple bulb. This light travels upward, passing
1:30
through a condenser lens that focuses the beams into a sharp cone of light. This cone strikes the specimen, perhaps
1:37
a thin slice of tissue or a drop of pond water on a glass slide. The light
1:42
travels through the specimen and enters the objective lens, which performs the
1:47
first stage of magnification. Finally, the light travels up the tube to the ocular lens, the eyepiece where
1:54
you place your eyes, magnifying the image once more. But magnification alone
2:00
is not enough. Imagine holding a magnifying glass up to a blurry photograph.
2:07
You make the image larger, yes, but you also make the blur larger. You do not
2:13
gain new information, you only gain size. In microscopy, the true limit of what we
2:20
can see is not magnification, but resolution. Let that word settle in your
2:25
mind. Resolution. Resolution is the ability of the lenses to distinguish two
2:31
points as separate and distinct. Think of driving a car down a dark highway at night. In the distance, you see a car
2:38
approaching. At first, it looks like a single bright light. As it gets closer,
2:45
that single blur splits into two distinct headlights. That moment when
2:50
the one becomes two is the limit of resolution. Without good resolution, a
2:56
bacterium is just a smudge. With good resolution, it is a defined shape with walls, internal structures, and purpose.
3:04
The story of how we discovered this world is a story of curiosity. It begins
3:09
in the mid600s. Picture a man named Robert Hook. He was an English scientist with a keen
3:16
interest in the new technology of lenses. In 1665, he placed a thin slice of cork under his
3:23
crude microscope. Cork is the bark of a tree, dead and dry. Looking through the
3:29
lens, Hook saw a network of tiny box-like compartments. They reminded him
3:35
of the small bare rooms that monks lived in, which were called cells.
3:40
So he coined the term cell. He was looking at the rigid cell walls of dead
3:46
plant tissue, establishing the name for the fundamental unit of life. However,
3:52
Hook did not see living things. That discovery fell to a Dutch merchant named
3:58
Anthony Van Leewan Hook. Lewen Hook was not a formerly trained scientist. He was
4:04
a draper, a man who sold cloth, but he was a master at grinding lenses. He
4:09
built simple handheld microscopes that were far more powerful than hooks.
4:15
Imagine the scene. It is the late 17th century. Leoan Ho takes a drop of
4:20
rainwater from a clay pot in his garden and places it behind his lens. He holds
4:26
it up to the light. He expects to see nothing, perhaps just clear water or dust. Instead, he sees a chaotic,
4:34
bustling city. He sees tiny creatures swimming, darting, and spinning. He
4:40
called them animal cues or little animals. He was the first human being in
4:46
history to lay eyes on bacteria and protozoa. He looked at the plaque from his own teeth and found it teeming with
4:53
life. He wrote letters to the Royal Society in London describing these
4:58
wretched beasties opening the door to the microbial world. For a long time
5:03
after Leu and Hog, scientists knew these tiny creatures existed, but they did not
5:09
know where they came from. This led to one of the great debates in the history of science, the debate over spontaneous
5:15
generation. For centuries, people believed that life could arise from non-living matter. They believed that if
5:22
you left meat out, it created maggots. They believed that if you left grain in
5:27
a dark corner, it created mice. They believed that bacteria simply appeared
5:33
in broth like clouds appearing in the sky. This idea is called spontaneous
5:39
generation. It took a giant of science to disprove this and establish the
5:45
foundation of modern microbiology. That man was Louis Pastor. In the mid 1800s,
5:52
Pastor designed an elegant experiment to prove that life only comes from existing
5:57
life. Visualize this experiment for it is beautiful in its simplicity. Pastur
6:03
took glass flasks and filled them with a nutrientrich broth. He then heated the
6:08
necks of the flasks and bent them into a long shape like the neck of a swan. He
6:14
boiled the broth to kill any microbes already inside. This is the process of sterilization.
6:20
Now here is the genius of the design. The shaped neck allowed air to enter the
6:26
flask because air is vital for life. But dust particles and microbes floating in
6:32
the air would get trapped in the bottom curve of the swan neck. They could not travel up the steep slope to reach the
6:38
broth. The result, the broth remained clear. It remained sterile. No life
6:44
appeared. Even though the flask was open to the air, the vital force did not
6:50
create life. But if pastor tilted the flask so the broth washed into the neck
6:56
and picked up the trapped dust, the broth quickly became cloudy with bacterial growth. This experiment proved
7:03
the theory of biogenesis. Living cells arise only from pre-existing living cells. It changed
7:10
medicine forever. If microbes do not just appear but are transmitted, then we
7:16
can stop them. We can wash our hands. We can sterilize our instruments. We can
7:21
pasteurize our milk. The invisible world has rules. And because of pastur, we
7:27
began to learn how to follow them. As you drift deeper into relaxation,
7:33
consider the magnitude of this shift. For thousands of years, humanity lived
7:38
in ignorance of the causes of disease and spoilage. We were blind to the ecosystem that operates on our skin and
7:45
in our gut. Today we have the window of the microscope. We have the map of the
7:50
cell. We have defined our subject. We have polished our lenses. We have
7:55
established that life is continuous passing from one cell to the next in an unbroken chain stretching back billions
8:03
of years. You are now standing at the entrance of this vast microscopic
8:08
landscape. In our next lesson, we will step through the glass. We will move
8:14
from the history of the tools to the architecture of the life they reveal. We will examine the difference between the
8:20
ancient simple cells of bacteria and the complex structured cells that make up
8:26
your own body. Let this introduction settle in your memory. The concept of magnification, the clarity of
8:33
resolution, the discovery of the animal cule, the swan neck flask. These are the
8:38
pillars upon which our knowledge rests. Lesson two. Proarotic versus ukareotic
Prokaryotic vs. Eukaryotic Cell Structure
8:45
cell structure. In our first lesson, we built the window through which we observe the world to the microscope. We
8:53
established that life is not spontaneous. It is a continuous chain of inheritance.
8:59
Now we must look at the links in that chain. We must examine the fundamental
9:04
unit of life, the cell. As you lie there resting, visualize the cells in your own
9:11
body. They are working tirelessly consuming energy, repairing damage, and
9:17
sending signals. But the cells that make up a human being are vastly different from the cells of the bacteria we
9:23
studied in the previous lesson. In the grand scheme of biology, there is a great divide. Every living thing on this
9:30
planet, from the smallest germ to the largest blue whale, falls into one of two primary categories based on how
9:38
their cells are built. These two categories are the proariots and the ukareotes. Let us begin with the
9:45
ethmology for the names reveal the secret. The word comes from the Greek
9:50
word caron which means nut or kernel. In biology this kernel refers to the
9:56
nucleus the dense central structure that holds the genetic blueprint. The first
10:02
category is the proariote. Pro means before. These are the organisms that
10:08
existed before the nucleus evolved. They are the before kernel cells. This group
10:15
includes all bacteria and the ancient archa. The second category is the
10:20
ukareote. Au means true or good. These are the organisms possessing a true
10:26
kernel or a true nucleus. This group includes you. It includes all animals,
10:33
plants, fungi and protozoa. Let us deepen our understanding of the
10:38
proariote. First, visualize a proarotic cell. It is incredibly small, usually
10:44
between 1 and 10 micrometers in size. It is ancient, rugged, and efficient. Think
10:50
of a proariote like a one room studio apartment. In a studio apartment, there
10:55
are no walls separating the kitchen from the bedroom or the living room. Everything happens in one open shared
11:01
space. In the bacterial cell, this open space is the cytoplasm.
11:07
Floating within this jellyike substance is the most precious treasure of the organism, its DNA. But unlike in your
11:15
cells, this DNA is not locked away in a vault. It floats freely in a region
11:20
called the nucleoid. The DNA is usually a single circular loop of genetic
11:26
instructions, naked and accessible. Because the procariote is like a studio
11:31
apartment. It lacks internal rooms. It has no membranebound organels. It has no
11:38
mitochondria to generate power. It has no GGI apparatus to package proteins. It
11:44
must perform all the functions of life, generating energy, making proteins and
11:50
replicating right there in the open cytoplasm or along its outer membrane.
11:55
This simplicity allows bacteria to reproduce at incredible speeds. They are
12:01
stripped down survival machines. Now let that image fade and replace it with the
12:07
image of a ukareote. If the procariot is a studio apartment, the ukareotic cell
12:13
is a sprawling mansion. It is generally much larger, 10 to 100 times the size of
12:20
a bacterium. It is complex, compartmentalized and highly organized.
12:26
The defining feature of this mansion is the master suite, the nucleus. In a
12:32
ukareotic cell, the DNA is not a simple loop. It is organized into multiple
12:38
linear chromosomes. To protect this vast library of information, the cell wraps
12:43
it in a double layer of membrane called the nuclear envelope. This is the true kernel. The DNA is safe inside,
12:51
separated from the chaos of the rest of the cell. But the complexity does not stop at the nucleus. The ukareotic
12:59
cytoplasm is filled with specialized structures called organels. Think of these as the separate rooms of
13:05
the mansion, each with a specific job. Visualize the mitochondria. These are
13:11
the power plants of the cell. They take sugar and oxygen and convert them into ATP, the energy currency of life.
13:19
Visualize the endopplasmic reticulum or ER. It creates a maze of tunnels near
13:25
the nucleus. This is the manufacturing floor where proteins and lipids are constructed. Visualize the Golgi
13:32
apparatus. It looks like a stack of flattened pancakes. This is the post office. It receives proteins from the
13:40
ER, tags them, packages them, and ships them to their destination. Visualize the
13:46
loss. These are the recycling centers filled with digestive enzymes that break
13:52
down waste and old parts. This compartmentalization allows ukarotic
13:57
cells to be incredibly complex and specialized, eventually allowing them to come together to build multisellular
14:04
organisms like humans. But how did this leap occur? How did life go from the
14:09
simple studio apartment to the complex mansion? The answer lies in one of the most fascinating theories in all of
14:16
science, the endo symbiotic theory. Let us travel back in time, billions of
14:22
years. The world is ruled by simple proarotic life. The theory suggests that
14:27
a large predatory proariote engulfed a smaller proariote. It tried to eat it,
14:33
but for some reason, the digestion failed. The small bacterium survived
14:38
inside the larger one. Instead of being digested, the small bacterium began to
14:43
help its host. It had a special talent. It could use oxygen to generate massive
14:48
amounts of energy. It became a power plant. The host provided protection and
14:53
