Law 10 The Law of Envy - Beware the Fragile Ego
1:11
"Everytime a friend succeeds, I die a little" -Gore Vidal
1:16
Envy is the trickiest and most  elusive of all human emotions. To decode envy, you must become  a master of human nature.
1:24
You need to be able to distinguish  passive envy from active envy   before you can understand its nuances.
1:31
We all suffer from envy from time to  time as we unconsciously observe the   people around us and feel that they  may have more in life than ourselves.
1:40
The need to compare may  motivate some of us to excel.
2:00
Signs of Envy It is difficult to detect subtle signs of envy,  but if you pay attention, you can detect them.
2:07
Microexpressions It is the eyes that are most associated with envy.   It's often the first impression that's the most  accurate, so you should pay more attention to it.
2:17
The envier will momentarily look at you with  disdain and hostility. Sometimes, the look is   only noticeable by accident, but if you notice  such looks in your first encounter with someone,  
2:26
and they happen more than once, look out for a  dangerous envious person lurking into your life.
2:32
The Schopenhauer method for eliciting envy. Tell suspected enviers some good  news about yourself—a promotion,  
2:40
a new and exciting love interest....You  will notice a very quick  expression of disappointment. Their  tone of voice as they congratulate 
2:46
you will betray some tension and  strain. Equally, tell them some  misfortune of yours and notice the  uncontrollable micro expression of 
2:54
joy in your pain, what is  commonly known as schadenfreude.
2:59
Poisonous Praise Beware of people who give you  confusing and paradoxical praise,  
3:05
they will find ways to praise you, but mix  the praise with sarcasm and offhand remarks  
3:10
that are designed to make you feel  uncomfortable, and make you doubt your abilities.
3:16
Back-biting Gossip often serves as a cover for envy,   a means of venting frustration through  sharing malicious rumors and stories.
3:25
If you happen to hear of a negative story  spread about you, that should have enough   impact to raise your awareness. A sign of active  envy could be the fact that your friend feels the  
3:33
need to vent his or her underlying hostility to a  third party rather than to keep it to themselves.
3:40
Push and pull If people are envious of you, the  people with the closest proximity,   that know all your secrets and weaknesses, are  often in the best position to act upon this envy.
3:52
“Enviers often use friendship and intimacy as  the best way to wound the people they envy.”
3:58
Envier Types Here are five common varieties of envious people,  how they disguise themselves, and how they attack.
4:07
The Leveler: Levelers are always on the lookout for  injustice and unfairness in this world.  
4:12
Despite their ability to put others down, they  do not easily take a joke at their own expense.  
4:18
It is their goal to bring everyone  down to their level of mediocrity.   Levelers cannot identify or  value excellence in anyone.
4:26
Be aware of these people, especially in the  workplace. Such individuals will try to make   you feel guilty for being ambitious to try  and cover up their own ambitions to succeed.  
4:36
Levelers are people that want to bring you  down when they see you rise above them.
4:41
The Self-entitled Slacker: People today rightfully feel  entitled to success and a good life,  
4:48
but they recognize that they will need  to sacrifice their time and work hard.   Self-entitled slackers believe that they are  entitled to success and the finer things in life.  
4:58
These people are plagued by narcissism, and  insecure about their ability to get what they  
5:03
want. Keep an eye out for those who prefer  to maintain their positions through charisma   rather than by putting in the  hard work to get things done.
5:11
The Status Fiend: Humans are very sensitive   to their rank and position within social groups.  Status for some people, however, is more than a  
5:20
way to measure social position -- it is the most  important factor in determining how valuable  
5:25
they perceive themselves to be. Status fiends will  ask about your income, whether you own your home,  
5:31
and what type of neighborhood you live in. It  is common for such types to demonstrate their   status in an opposite direction, portraying  themselves to be monk-like, but in reality  
5:41
they secretly yearn for the luxury they cannot  afford. When you are around this type of envier,  
5:46
try to hide, or at least downplay, anything  that might trigger envy around such types. The Attacher:
5:52
These types are attracted to  the successful or powerful,   not in admiration, but out of envy. They will do  anything they can to make themselves useful. They  
6:02
lack their own purpose, but they want to get some  of that attention and success the high-achiever   is getting, without all the hard work. They hate  the fact this person is getting more than them.
6:14
The Insecure Master: For some people, having a high position  increases their insecurities. They fear  
6:21
subordinates who may possess more talent  than them with an envious gaze. They may  
6:26
fire people for strange reasons or  discredit your outstanding projects. 
6:32
Keep an eye out for signs of insecurity  or envy among those above you.
6:38
Envy Triggers While certain personality types tend to be more  prone to envy than others, you should be aware  
6:45
that certain situations can cause envy in just  about anyone. Older people and people whose  
6:51
professions are fading tend to have fragile  egos and can easily feel envious of others.
6:58
When a woman achieves high levels of  success, other women and men feel inadequate,   leading to jealousy and animosity instead  of appreciation. In order to deflect envy,  
7:08
it becomes even more important for  successful women to play the humble card.
7:14
The best way to deal with an envy attack  is to control your emotions. As a result of   social media, envy is more common today than ever  before. We all have a low level of envy within us,  
7:25
which can be exacerbated by reading something  or seeing something that makes us feel insecure.
7:32
Beyond Envy Bring yourself closer to what you envy.  Envy thrives in environments where  people see each other every day,  
7:37
whether they are colleagues in a corporation,  family members, neighbors, or peers in any group.
7:44
Considering that our brains cannot stop  comparing, we should redirect this mechanism   into something constructive and creative. Human greatness is to be admired. 
7:53
Admiration is the opposite of envy. We  acknowledge people's accomplishments,   celebrate them, without feeling insecure. 
8:01
Our species' potential is to be celebrated  when we recognize someone's greatness.
8:06
“Gratitude is the best antidote to envy.” Engage in downward comparisons. We often compare  ourselves with people above us, and we become  
8:16
envious. Instead of always looking upwards, look  downwards from time to time. Compare yourself to  
8:22
people who are lower than you socio-economically,  not in a superior manner, but in a way that gives  
8:27
you gratitude for the things you do have  in your life, that so many others do not.
8:32
Schadenfreude, the experience of gaining  pleasure from another person's suffering,   is closely related to envy. Instead,  
8:40
follow what Friedrich Nietzsche's called  Mitfreude, which means "joying with."
8:45
Instead of simply congratulating someone on their  success, you should instead attempt to actively  
8:50
feel their joy, try to feel what they must be  feeling in their moment of success. Practicing  
8:55
mitfreude increases your ability to feel this  emotion in relation to your own experiences.
Law 11 - Grandiosity
9:02
Law 11 The Law of Grandiosity - Know your limits “The gods are merciless with  those who fly too high on the 
9:09
wings of grandiosity, and they  will make you pay the price.” Throughout history, humans have had a deep  need to consider themselves important.
9:18
We get a little bit of success and  we let it go straight to our heads.   We feel superior to others. We amplify our  successes and we take all the credit. We  
9:28
downplay any role that luck or the contributions  of others may have played in our success.
9:33
As soon as the perception of our greatness  and brilliance differs too much from reality,   we become grandiose. Seek out signs of  excessive grandiosity in others and in yourself.
9:44
Grandiosity has become  exponentially more prominent today   as a result of the following factors:
9:50
The number of people who received   pampering attention as a child  is greater now than ever before.
9:55
A growing number of people are showing little or  no respect for authority or experts in general.
10:01
Social media is amplifying grandiosity. We allow  the attention of others to inflate our egos  
10:08
and Social media gives us almost limitless  capability to expand our presence, and to  
10:13
create the illusion that we can attract thousands  or even millions of adoring followers to us.
10:18
“More than anything it is social media  that spreads the grandiosity virus.”
10:24
The Grandiose Leader It is possible for people with  high levels of grandiosity to  
10:29
attain positions of power if they also  possess some talent and assertive energy.  
10:34
The grandiose leader distorts reality  to fit their perceived greatness.
10:39
Here are six common illusions  grandiose leaders like to create. “Greatness is my destiny”
10:46
It is common for grandiose leaders to  portray themselves as destined for greatness.   The stories they tell about their childhood  and youth demonstrate their uniqueness,  
10:56
as if fate had chosen them. “I’m the common man/woman”
11:01
Public figures must present themselves to the  public as representatives of the average citizen.  
11:06
A grandiose leader's trick is to put  emphasis on their cultural tastes,   and not the class they were raised in.
11:13
“I will be your savior” In times of crisis and trouble, these types  rise to power. These individuals will be the  
11:20
ones to deliver the people from the  difficulties they are experiencing.   They need to make large yet vague promises  to do this. Messages must be simple  
11:28
to understand, yet stir the emotions. When followers become part of the cult,   they are doubly mesmerized and ready  to excuse any faults of the leader.
11:38
“I am able to rewrite the rules” Humans have a secret desire to do away  with the conventions and rules in place  
11:45
in every field, and to be empowered just  by following their intuitions. Secretly,  
11:50
we are excited and wish to believe grandiose  leaders when they proclaim to have such powers.
11:56
“Everything I touch turns to Gold” In order to create the legend  that they have never failed,  
12:01
those with grandiose tendencies  will exaggerate their success.  If they had setbacks or failures in their  career, other people were always to blame.
12:11
“I’m invulnerable” The grandiose leader likes to take risks, but  this boldness is often reckless and mismanaged.  
12:18
In essence, their decisions are  influenced not by rational considerations,   but by the need for fame and attention, and  the reality eventually catches up with them.
12:28
Practical Grandiosity  Grandiosity is a form of  primal energy we all possess.  
12:34
Here are five basic principles that are essential  for attaining the high level of fulfillment.
12:40
Be honest with yourself  about your grandiose desires.  You must be honest with yourself and admit that we  all want to feel important and noticed by others.  
12:48
Only then you can channel your energy  into something useful and productive. Focus your energy.
12:54
Avoid scatterbrain thinking and  moving from one idea to another.   Don't try to solve too many problems at once  and break your goals into smaller steps.  
13:03
Your goals should be attainable  in a matter of months, not years. Keep in touch with reality.
13:09
Let your imagination run free, but  don't expect your ideas to be perfect.   You will be able to create something practical,  powerful, and realistic by listening to criticism  
13:18
and maintaining an ongoing dialogue between  your imagination and reality (feedback).
13:24
Make sure you challenge yourself  according to your abilities. Continuously be seeking challenges just above  your current skill level. Attempting projects  
13:33
below or at your ability level will make  you easily bored and you will lose focus.
13:38
Don't hold back your grandiose energy. “Think of your energy as a wild animal  that needs to roam free now and then  
13:45
or it will go mad from restlessness.” Try learning a new skill in a different field,  
13:50
or start the business you once considered  as unattainable or a distraction.
Law 12 - Gender Rigidity
13:57
Law 12 : The Law of Gender Rigidity - Reconnect  to the Masculine or Feminine Within You
14:04
“Do not be afraid to bring out the more  sensitive or ambitious sides to your character.   These repressed parts (masculinity and  femininity) of you are yearning to be let out”.
14:12
We all have a mixture of masculine and feminine  qualities which are due to our genetics and also  
14:17
the influence of the parent of the opposite sex.  But in order to play the gender role expected of   us, we tend to repress some of these qualities,  and we lose important dimensions of our character.
14:28
You need to look within yourself and discover   those feminine or masculine traits  that are repressed and underdeveloped.
14:34
Psychologist Carl Jung identifies these  different dimensions of our character as: the anima - which is the unconscious  feminine part of a male, and
14:42
the animus - the unconscious  masculine part of a female. When we fall In love, we become victims of  emotions that we cannot control. We make decisions  
14:51
about partners we cannot rationally explain. Our bad choices of partners are often repeated  
14:57
as if we are driven by some demon deep within  ourselves. It is common for relationships to fall  
15:02
apart due to misreadings and miscommunications on  both sides, and without knowing the reasons for   the breakdown, we will go through exactly  the same process with the next person.
15:12
Gender Projection—Types The variation of gender projections   are infinite, but here are the six most common  types. As we go through these projections,  
15:22
try to recognize any tendency toward one of these  forms of projection within yourself. Furthermore,  
15:28
watch out for how people will project  their desires and fantasies on to you.
15:34
The Devilish Romantic: The woman who is usually drawn to a man who is  older and successful and who can lavish her with  
15:42
attention and gifts. In order to attract  his attention, she decides to seduce him.  
15:48
After time, however, she realizes that his  masculine, powerful, and romantic qualities   fall short of what she imagined. The attention  she gets is not sufficient or does not last long.  
16:00
She is powerless to change him, and he will not  change, so he walks away from her. A woman in  
16:06
this scenario has become trapped by the attention  paid to her by her father in early childhood. It  
16:12
becomes her lifelong goal to reclaim that  attention and the power that comes with it.
16:18
To attract the attention they desire, they  have to constantly be flirtatious and charming.
16:24
The Elusive Woman of Perfection: The man who thinks he has found the perfect woman.  
16:30
Although he hasn't had many  encounters with the woman in question,   he can imagine all kinds of positive  encounters with her in the future.  
16:38
Despite his best efforts, he will quickly  become disenchanted. Her character is not what  
16:43
he thought it would be; she deceived him. He then  proceeds to project his fantasy on the next woman.
16:50
Often, men susceptible to this projection  grew up without a supportive mother.   A mother of this sort might have expected  her son to give her the attention  
16:58
and support she was lacking from her husband.  Due to this role reversal, the boy who becomes  
17:04
a man feels emptiness inside and a need to fill  the attention he didn’t receive in childhood.
17:11
The Lovable Rebel: The woman who is attracted to men  that have a disdain for authority.  
17:17
This man, unlike the Devilish Romantic, is  usually young and unsuccessful. Moreover,  
17:22
he tends to be outside her usual circle of  friends. Even though the relationship breaks   apart, the fantasy remains intact. Women with this  projection often have a distant and strict father  
17:34
who is strong and patriarchal. As a girl, she  dreamed of being independent and assertive, but  
17:40
was restricted to following her father's orders  and playing the role of the obedient daughter.
17:47
The Fallen Woman: The man who is attracted to the opposite of what  he used to be: good, obedient, and trustworthy.  
17:56
This type of man often had strong mother  figures as children. Subconsciously,  
18:01
they gravitate towards women who are imperfect,  bad, or from different cultures and/or social  
18:07
classes. His goal is to rescue her, but over time  he realizes she isn't what he had expected. These  
18:14
men have a passive and masochistic anima, and they  are often merely used as pawns by these women.
18:21
The Superior Man: The woman who is drawn to the superior man.   His confidence and power radiate through him. He  could be a successful businessman or a professor. 
18:32
Among women, this type of projection is very  common and is due to feelings of inferiority. 
18:37
Being unable to develop her  own confidence or strength,   she tends to search for these traits in men and  exaggerate their appearance in her imagination. 
18:46
She often attracts men who are  attracted to younger women,   whom they can dominate (the professor  seducing the student scenario). 
18:54
Typically, these men fail to be as clever or  brilliant as she imagines, so the woman either  
18:59
leaves disappointed or gets stuck with her low  self-esteem and bends to his manipulations.
19:06
She should cultivate genuine self-confidence so  that she can evaluate the value and character   of the men she meets before being misguided.
19:15
The Woman to Worship Him: The man who seeks a woman who will worship him.  
19:21
His life is hard, but he is driven and ambitious.  His life seems to be lacking something. A woman  
19:27
comes along who is warm, attentive, and engaging  to him. When he pursues certain types of women,  
19:33
he subtly positions them to play the mother role  so they can support, adore, and enhance his ego.
19:40
Generally, this male projection stems  from his relationship with the mother,   who adores him and lavishes him with attention.  She has empowered the boy, and he becomes addicted  
19:51
to that kind of attention. He seeks and expects  that same attention from the women he meets.
19:57
This man should see the pattern of  these relationships throughout his life.   He must be able to take care of himself.
