“Authority is the delicate art of creating  the appearance of power, legitimacy,   and fairness while getting people to identify  with you as a leader who is in their service.”
31:35
Strategies for Establishing Authority Having authority means people follow you  willingly. They listened to your advice and  
31:40
followed your words. In your efforts to overcome  people's natural ambivalence and resistance,  
31:45
you must continuously seek ways  to mobilize their willpower.   These strategies aim to accomplish  just that. Put all of them to use.
31:56
Authenticity : Find your authority style The authority you create must flow naturally  
32:02
from your character and specific strengths. Think of certain archetypes of authority   from our culture : One of the  archetypes will best suit you. 
32:11
The Deliverer - The person who delivers people  from evil. Think Moses or Martin Luther King Jr. 
32:18
The founder - They establish a new  order in business or politics that   diverges from the status quo. The visionary artist - they  
32:27
defy the norms - Think picasso The Healer - The one who can unify people. 
32:34
The teacher - The person who can initiate action  and get people to learn from their mistakes.
32:41
Focus outwardly: the Attitude. Humans are selfish by nature,   spending most of our time focusing  on our emotions, our struggles,  
32:50
and our fantasies. Focus on how you can  serve others. Become a good listener,  
32:55
and see your position of leadership as  a responsibility and not a privilege. The Vision : Cultivate the third eye Individuals, caught up in the moment, are  
33:05
prone to overreact, panic, and see only a limited  part of the situation in front of them. Develop  
33:10
your long-term vision, and don’t get caught up  in day to day drama or insignificant events.
33:16
The Tone : Lead from the front As a leader, you must show   everyone that you work as hard, if not  harder, than they do. Setting the right  
33:24
tone is essential. To gain your approval, the  members will want to rise up to your level.
33:30
Stir conflicting emotions: the Aura.  People tend to be too predictable. In order  to fit in and feel like part of the group,  
33:38
they adopt a persona that they think is pleasing  and consistent with the group. As the leader,  
33:44
it is important to establish an aura of mystery.  Mixing signals and displaying qualities that seem  
33:49
inconsistent, causes people to think about you  and pause before they try to categorize you.
33:55
Never appear to take, always to give The act of taking something away  from someone they assumed they owned,  
34:02
be it time, rights, or money can create a sense  of insecurity, and will call into question your  
34:07
authority and all your accomplishments. Always  be seen to give and never take from the group. Rejuvenate your authority: Adaptability. The newer generations will inevitably become  
34:17
immune to your charm and aura. Therefore, Your style of leadership needs to adapt   to the moods and epochs of the time. Don’t let  your leadership style remain stuck in its ways.
Law 16 - Aggression
34:40
Law 16 : The Law of Aggression - See  the Hostility Behind the Friendly Façade
34:48
“Men are not gentle, friendly  creatures wishing for love,   who simply defend themselves if  attacked … A powerful desire for  
34:56
aggression has to be reckoned was part  of their … endowment.”—Sigmund Freud 
35:05
On the surface people around  you appear polite and civil,   but underneath the mask we all  have an aggression side in our DNA.
35:13
People have acted violently and  aggressively in the past and present,   but we dislike to think of ourselves as  someone who can act in that same manner.
35:21
Human nature is deeply misunderstood in this  way. Our species is wired for aggression  
35:26
and it is latent in every human. Aggression can be used for good. However, it can  also lead to antisocial behavior and violence.  
35:35
Despite some individuals clearly  being more aggressive than others,   we are all capable of slipping towards  the negative side of aggression.  
35:43
Human aggression is a continuum,  and we all exist somewhere on it.
35:49
The Source of Human Aggression “Human aggression stems from  an underlying insecurity,  
35:56
as opposed to simply an impulse  to hurt or take from others.”
36:02
Only humans are able to grasp their own  mortality. At any moment, we could lose   our jobs or be separated from our partners,  which can make us feel frustrated and helpless.
36:14
People who are chronically  aggressive, on the other hand,   are more likely to feel the helplessness  and frustration we might feel on occasion.  
36:21
These individuals experience  chronic insecurity and fragility,   and work incredibly hard to hide this  by overpowering and controlling others.
36:29
If you are around a chronic aggressor, you  must understand this behavior is stemming   from an underlying insecurity, a deep  wound, almost always from early childhood.
36:40
We also have to realize that aggressors  see others as objects to control.
36:46
Two myths to dispel The first myth says that humans used to  live in harmony with nature and with each  
36:52
other. But anthropologists have proven beyond a  shadow of a doubt that pre-civilization warfare   was every bit as murderous and brutal  as anything we are experiencing today.  
37:02
There was little peace between us. The second myth claims that human beings may  have by and large been violent and aggressive  
37:09
in the past, but we have evolved since then  to become more compassionate, enlightened,  
37:14
and led by our better angels. Still, the signs of  human aggression persist as they did in the past.  
37:21
Present day warfare, genocide,  and degradation of the environment   paint a different picture from this myth.
37:31
Redirect your aggressive energy  There are usually three main ways in  which aggressive energy is expended.
37:37
You can channel this energy into  your work (controlled aggression) You can channel it towards others through  your behavior (passive-aggression)
37:47
It can be turned inward in  the form of self-loathing,   which directs our anger and  aggression at our own shortcomings.
37:56
How do you usually handle this energy? Observe  how you respond to frustration and uncertainty  
38:01
as a way to judge yourself. Are you prone to lash  out, become angry, and do things you later regret?  
38:07
Or do you internalize the anger  and grow depressed?   Passive Aggression—Its Strategies  and How to Counter Them 
38:16
Our default setting is to avoid direct  confrontations and appear sociable and polite.   However, it is not always possible to  get what we want without making an effort  
38:24
to assert ourselves. This is  called passive-aggression. While passive aggression is sometimes necessary  in order to not be walked all over by others,  
38:33
some people have a chronic passive aggression   problem. Here are some strategies  that passive aggressors often use.
38:40
The Subtle-Superiority Strategy: The person who always comes late,   providing an excuse and an insincere apology.
38:49
The Sympathy Strategy:  The person who plays the victim but in reality  loves the drama, the pain, and the attention.
38:56
The Insinuating-Doubt Strategy: Someone, maybe even a friend,   who makes an offhand remark that makes  you question yourself and your abilities. 
39:05
“You did quite well on the project,  for someone at your level”   Was he/she subtly insulting  me? You may ask yourself.
39:12
A key point of this strategy is to make  you feel bad such that you find yourself   thinking about it for days afterward. Their  goal is to bring down your self-esteem.
39:21
The most common reason for their actions is envy.  It is best to show that their insinuations have no  
39:26
impact on you. Their goal is to get a reaction  from you, and you will not give it to them.
39:32
The Blame-Shifter Strategy: The person who is confronted and immediately  begins shifting responsibility and blame back to  
39:39
the person who is irritated. They grow silent when  you confront them, wearing a hurt or disappointed  
39:45
look while their tone of voice subtly conveys a  sense of disbelief that they did anything wrong.  
39:51
It leaves you feeling as if you might  have overreacted, now you feel guilty.  The intention is to cover up any  unpleasant behavior, deflect criticism,  
39:59
and make people refrain from calling  them out on their mistakes in the future.   This tactic can be countered if you remain  objective and unaffected by their blame-shifting.
40:10
The Passive-Tyrant Strategy:  The person who is impossible to please. Usually,  powerful people use this strategy toward their  
40:17
underlings, but it can be applied by  individuals in relationships as well.  Strategizing against these types is very  difficult because they are usually your superiors  
40:27
and possess real power over you. There's really  only one counter: Quitting and recovering.
40:34
Controlled Aggression Your aggression should   not be repressed or turned inward.  Channel this energy towards real  
40:41
problems that you can solve. Towards  your ambitions to get what you want.
Law 17 - Generational Myopia
40:47
Law 17 : The Law of Generational  Myopia - Seize the Historical Moment
40:54
“You are a product of the times as much  as anyone; the generation you were born   into has shaped your thoughts and values,  whether you are aware of this or not.”
41:04
In more ways than you can imagine, you are born  into a generation that defines your identity.  
41:09
There is a strong desire in your generation to  separate itself from the previous generation   and forge a new direction for  the world. New values are formed,  
41:18
which are absorbed into your thinking as an  individual. With age, generational ideas and  
41:23
values tend to close you off to other  points of view, limiting your thinking.   In order to free yourself from the mental  restrictions placed on you by your generation,  
41:32
you must understand as deeply as possible  this powerful influence on who you are.
41:38
The Generational Phenomenon Each generation has its own symbols,   myths, beliefs, and mindset which constitute  its generational spirit. A person born in the  
41:48
1890s would be different from someone born  in the 1950s, and a person born in the 1950s  
41:55
would have a different "generational spirit"  from a person born in the 1990s, etc.
42:01
Generational Patterns Each generation inherits beliefs and ideas from  their parents, but seeks to find something new.  
42:08
The 14th century historian  Ibn Khaldun believed that   history occurs in cycles over four generations.
42:15
The 1st generation: revolutionaries make  a radical break to establish new values. 
42:22
The 2nd generation: seeks to establish some order  and discipline after the revolutionary chaos. 
42:28
The 3rd generation : aims to build a  comfortable life through pragmatic means.  The 4th generation: are cynical and  lost in a society that has stagnated.  
42:38
New revolutionaries are now primed to usher  in a new order, and the cycle begins again.
42:44
You need to change the way you  look at your own generation.   You should attempt to understand how deeply  the spirit and the culture of your generation  
42:51
have affected the way you view the world. Identify the characteristics of your generation,  
42:57
in order to gain an understanding of its  spirit in the present and to exploit it.
43:02
Think about where your generation fits within  the tension between early and middle adulthood.
43:08
“We think we are judging the younger  generation in an objective manner,   but we are merely succumbing  to an illusion of perspective.”
43:16
How to capitalize on the spirit of the times  The following are some strategies to  make the most of generational dynamics.
43:24
Challenge your past, including your  parents’ values, and try to see things   from a different perspective. Take on the  generations zeitgeist in a unique way:
43:33
don't let your age limit your curiosity, you  can be a child-like wonderer as a grownup 
43:39
Increase your interactions  with the younger generation  read the works of previous generations influence the younger generation,  
43:46
mostly your children, to shape the future LAW 18 : The Law of Death Denial  - Meditate on Our Common Mortality
Law 18 - Death Denial
43:57
“By connecting to the reality of death,   we connect more profoundly to the  reality and fullness of life.”
44:05
Near death experiences actually have a paradoxical  effect that makes us feel more alive and awake.  
44:11
You can make your life more meaningful and  productive by being aware of your mortality: 
44:17
Become viscerally aware of death. Take a moment  to reflect on death. Visualize yourself at the  
44:22
moment you die, and try to picture what you  are doing or where you might be at the time. 
44:29
Remember that your remaining years on this planet  are a deadline for completing some life project.  
44:35
While you still have time, work  on what matters most to you,   including your relationships and your goals. 
44:41
Observe mortality. Consider the billions  of people who have come before you,   and realize that most of the people on Earth now  will no longer be alive in the next 60 years.
44:51
“We are all a part of the  brotherhood and sisterhood of death.” Embracing death will make it easier to cope  with all other pain and hardships in your life.  
45:00
Adversity is an opportunity  to grow and enlighten us.  Take time to contemplate the sublime. The infinity  of time and space makes our daily struggles seem  
45:11
trivial. Faced with the vastness of nature,  our ego seems insignificant. Being aware of  
45:18
your mortality will free you of insignificant  worries, will allow you to concentrate solely  
45:24
on your life's work, and will help you to  explore every possibility to its fullest.

