1 Antwort

@samlebon9884
vor 2 Monaten
In classical Greece, knowledge grew through speculation and philosophical reasoning, not through anything resembling the scientific method. Greek thinkers built extraordinary intellectual frameworks — logic, geometry, metaphysics, early biology — but their approach to nature remained non‑experimental. Plato distrusted sensory evidence, Aristotle generalized from limited observations, and even Hippocratic medicine lacked controlled testing or reproducibility. Greece laid conceptual foundations, but it never developed the methodological engine that defines modern science.

That breakthrough appears a millennium later in the Islamic Golden Age, most clearly in the work of Ibn al‑Haytham (Alhazen, 965–1040). In his Book of Optics, he outlines a disciplined approach built on controlled experiments, repeatability, hypothesis testing, mathematical analysis, and skepticism toward authority — principles that align directly with the modern scientific method. His work was translated into Latin and shaped European thinkers like Kepler, Bacon, and Descartes, even if later Western narratives downplayed his role. The historical record is clear: the scientific method was not a Greek or Western invention, but an achievement forged in the intellectual laboratories of the Islamic Golden Age.



