1:54:31
of the story, we now know that they are not the final ultimate truth. They are
1:54:36
approximations, astonishingly good approximations, but approximations all the same. Because in
1:54:43
the early 20th century, that young patent clerk we keep almost meeting, Einstein, showed that Newton's laws
1:54:51
quietly break down when things move extremely fast, close to the speed of light, or when gravity gets extremely
1:54:58
strong near something like a black hole. In those extreme realms, Newton's clean
1:55:04
picture of absolute space and absolute time dissolves and something stranger
1:55:11
and deeper takes its place. The warping of space and time itself. And at the
1:55:17
same time, other physicists discovered that when you go down to the scale of atoms and the particles inside them,
1:55:25
Newton's laws break down again in an entirely different way, replaced by the
1:55:31
strange probabilistic rules of quantum mechanics, where particles don't have
1:55:36
definite positions until you look and the whole comfortable certainty of the clockwork universe evaporates into a
1:55:44
haze of possibility. Two great revolutions, one for the very fast and the very
1:55:51
heavy, one for the very small. And each is its own long story for another quiet
1:55:58
night. But here's the beautiful part, the part that ties the whole thing
1:56:03
together, and that I think is genuinely one of the most reassuring truths in all
1:56:09
of human knowledge. When Einstein's relativity and quantum mechanics came along, they did not throw
1:56:16
Newton's laws in the bin. They contained them. Newton's laws turn out to be
1:56:22
exactly what you get from the deeper theories when things are moving at ordinary speeds and are of ordinary
1:56:28
size, which is to say the world you and I live in, the world of falling apples
1:56:34
and thrown balls and orbiting moons. The deeper theories fold Newton's laws
1:56:40
inside themselves as a special case, a limiting case, the shape the truth takes
1:56:46
at human scale. Nothing was wasted. Nothing was wrong. Exactly. Just
1:56:52
incomplete. Newton didn't get overturned. He got enveloped the way a
1:56:58
small circle sits inside a larger one. And someday, almost certainly,
1:57:04
relativity and quantum mechanics will themselves be enveloped by some still deeper theory that unifies them. Because
1:57:11
right now, remarkably, those two great pillars of modern physics don't quite
1:57:16
fit together. And reconciling them is the great unfinished business of physics. The puzzle the best minds alive
1:57:24
are still working on tonight. And when that deeper theory finally arrives, it
1:57:30
too will contain everything that came before as a special case still true
1:57:36
within its domain. Isn't that a lovely thing to fall asleep on? That understanding doesn't destroy itself and
1:57:43
start over. It accumulates. It nests. Every generation of curious, stubborn,
1:57:50
sometimes wrong, sometimes brilliant people adds a layer to this extraordinary structure. And the old
1:57:58
layers don't crumble. They just find their proper place inside something larger. Galileo's rolling ball is still
1:58:06
rolling correctly inside Newton's mechanics. Newton's mechanics is still working
1:58:12
perfectly inside Einstein's relativity. And all of it, every layer, was built by
1:58:18
human beings. People with difficult childhoods and bitter rivalries and
1:58:24
crushing griefs. People who worked for free or died thinking they'd failed or
1:58:31
went blind under house arrest or hauled thermometers up waterfalls on their honeymoons.
1:58:37
ordinary flawed mortal people who nevertheless managed to reach out with
1:58:42
nothing but their minds and a bit of mathematics and read the hidden rule book of the universe. So as you lie
1:58:50
there now with the weight of the blankets settling over you and your breathing slowing down, let all the
1:58:57
names and dates and equations gently blur and fade. You don't need to hold on
1:59:03
to any of them. What's worth keeping is just this quiet astonishment that the
1:59:09
universe has a deep order and that the order is knowable and that we small,
1:59:16
brief, curious creatures on a rocky little planet actually came to know a
1:59:21
good part of it. The very same laws we've been tracing all night are working
1:59:27
silently and perfectly all around you and inside you at this very moment.
1:59:34
Gravity is holding you to your bed exactly as it holds the moon in the sky.
1:59:39
The same rule all the way up and all the way down. Just as we said at the very
1:59:45
beginning, every jewel of energy in your warm resting body is being conserved,
1:59:53
traded from one form to another, and never lost. Obeying the first law,
1:59:59
entropy is climbing ever so gently, carrying you forward from this moment
2:00:05
into the next, giving your night its direction, its arrow toward morning.
2:00:11
Electromagnetic waves are drifting invisibly through the dark of your room. And the electrons in every atom of you
2:00:18
are arranged just so by the same forces Faraday and Maxwell described. It's all
2:00:24
real. It's all happening. It's all obeying the rules effortlessly, the way
2:00:30
it has since long, long before anyone was here to write them down. And now you
2:00:35
know a little more about those rules and about the remarkable imperfect people
2:00:40
who found them. That's a good thing to carry down into sleep. That the world is
2:00:46
far more orderly and far more wonderful than it ever needs to be. So let the
2:00:52
equations fade. Let your thoughts go loose and quiet. And let the oldest,
2:00:58
gentlest law of all, the one that pulls everything softly downward toward rest,
2:01:05
carry you the rest of the way. Sleep well.
