And the uncomfortable truth is that real people often don't. Starting in the
1:42:27
1970s, experimental economists began running laboratory experiments to see
1:42:33
how people actually play games. And the results were surprising. Take the
1:42:38
ultimatum game, one of the simplest and most revealing experiments in all of behavioral economics.
1:42:45
Player one is given $10 and must propose a split with player two. Player two can
1:42:51
accept, in which case both keep their shares, or reject, in which case nobody
1:42:56
gets anything. Standard game theory says player one should offer the minimum $1,
1:43:02
and player two should accept because a dollar is better than nothing. But in
1:43:08
actual experiments across cultures and continents, the most common offer is
1:43:14
around $4 or $5. and offers below about $3 are frequently rejected. People would
1:43:22
rather walk away with nothing than accept a deal they perceive as unfair,
1:43:28
spite, or rather a sense of justice, overrides material self-interest. This
1:43:34
doesn't mean game theory is broken. It means that people's utility functions,
1:43:39
the things they're actually maximizing, are richer than simple dollar amounts.
1:43:45
People care about fairness. They care about reciprocity. They care about status, about punishment, about not
1:43:53
being taken for a sucker. The field of behavioral game theory developed by
1:43:58
researchers like Colin Camera and deeply influenced by the pioneering work of
1:44:03
Daniel Conaman and Amos Tverki on cognitive biases and decision-making
1:44:09
under uncertainty has enriched game theory by taking human psychology
1:44:15
seriously. It builds models of how real people actually think and choose with all their
1:44:22
biases and emotional reactions and social preferences rather than assuming
1:44:27
the frictionless rationality of textbook economic agents. The result is a richer,
1:44:34
more realistic and ultimately more useful science. And there are other
1:44:39
fascinating examples. The beauty contest game, for instance. A group of people
1:44:45
each choose a number between zero and 100. The winner is the person whose
1:44:51
number is closest to 2/3 of the group's average. Now, if everyone in the group
1:44:56
were perfectly rational and knew that everyone else was perfectly rational, the only equilibrium would be for
1:45:03
everyone to choose zero. Here's why. If the average were say 50, you'd want to
1:45:10
pick 33, 2/3 of 50. But if everyone thinks that way, the average drops to
1:45:18
33. So you'd want to pick 22. But if everyone thinks that way, the logic
1:45:24
drives the number all the way down to zero. But in actual experiments with
1:45:30
real people, the most common choices cluster around 33. one round of
1:45:35
strategic thinking or 22 two rounds. Almost nobody picks zero. People are
1:45:43
strategic but boundedly so. They think a step or two ahead, not all the way to
1:45:49
the logical conclusion. Understanding these cognitive limits is crucial if you
1:45:54
want your game theoretic models to describe what actually happens, not just
1:45:59
what a race of hypothetical super rational beings would do. And now in the
1:46:04
21st century, game theory has entered what might be its most important arena
1:46:10
yet, the digital world. The internet, seen through a game theoretic lens, is a
1:46:17
vast continuous multiplayer strategic interaction unfolding at
1:46:22
incomprehensible speed. Every time you type a search query, the ads that appear
1:46:28
beside your results got there through an auction, a realtime auction that runs in
1:46:34
milliseconds, with advertisers bidding on keywords, and a sophisticated
1:46:40
mechanism determining which ads appear, in what order, and at what price.
1:46:46
Google's ad auction, which generates the majority of the company's revenue, is
1:46:51
essentially a victory inspired mechanism. Biders don't pay their own bid. They pay just enough to beat the
1:46:58
next highest bidder, adjusted for ad quality. The entire economic engine of
1:47:03
the modern internet runs on game theoretic mechanism design invisibly
1:47:09
billions of times a day and it goes deeper. A whole sub field called
1:47:14
algorithmic game theory pioneered by researchers like Noam Nissan, Tim
1:47:20
Ruffgarden, and Ava Tardos studies what happens when the players in a game are
1:47:26
algorithms or when the games themselves are computational problems. How
1:47:32
efficient is internet routing when each network operator acts in their own
1:47:37
interest? How much value is lost when selfish agents interact without
1:47:42
coordination? Ruff Garden developed a concept called the price of anarchy, a
1:47:48
precise measure of the gap between the equilibrium outcome and the socially optimal one. And it turns out in many
1:47:56
important settings, the gap is surprisingly small. Selfish routing,
1:48:01
selfish caching, selfish resource allocation. They often produce outcomes
1:48:07
that, while not perfect, aren't too far from what a benevolent central planner
1:48:12
would achieve. That's quietly reassuring given that nobody runs the internet. And
1:48:18
then there's the intersection of game theory and artificial intelligence, which brings the whole story full circle
1:48:25
in a way that feels almost destined. In March 2016, Google Deep Minds Alph Go
1:48:31
program defeated Lee Sadal, one of the greatest Go players in history in a
1:48:36
five-game match in Seoul. Go is a two-player zero sum game of perfect
1:48:42
information. Exactly the kind of game Zerlo and Vonomanyman theorized about.
1:48:48
But it's so complex, more possible board positions than atoms in the observable
1:48:53
universe that brute force miniax is utterly impossible. AlphaGo used deep
1:48:59
neural networks and Monte Carlo tree search to learn which positions were
1:49:04
strong and which moves were promising. It was in effect doing what the miniax
1:49:11
theorem proved was possible. playing at or near the optimal level, but using
1:49:16
pattern recognition and statistical learning rather than exhaustive calculation. And then came Alph Go Zero,
1:49:25
which learned Go entirely from scratch with no human knowledge at all. It
1:49:31
started knowing nothing but the rules of the game, played millions of games against itself, and emerged playing at a
1:49:38
superhuman level, discovering strategies that human masters had never found in
1:49:44
thousands of years. Think about what that is. It's a machine discovering game
1:49:49
theoretic equilibria through a process not unlike the natural selection that
1:49:54
Maynard Smith described. variation, testing, selection, [clears throat]
1:50:00
improvement over and over until the strategies converge on something
1:50:05
extraordinary. The game theory is the same. Only the mechanism of discovery
1:50:11
has changed. And so here we are. Game theory started as an abstract
1:50:17
mathematical curiosity. Fonyman proving theorems about parlor games in 1928.
1:50:24
It became the framework for thinking about nuclear armageddon at Rand. It became the foundation of modern
1:50:30
economics through Nash. It explained the ritualized violence of the animal
1:50:36
