Everything around us is made of materials — stone, copper, bronze, iron, steel, polymers, composites, and even futuristic self-healing materials.

In this video, we explore the quiet but incredible story of materials science: how humans went from shaping stone tools to controlling metals, understanding steel, designing plastics, and engineering materials at the atomic level. Along the way, we look at key ideas like hardness, toughness, alloys, crystal structures, polymers, composites, fracture, and why the “stuff” around us behaves the way it does.

Resources:
MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Solid-State Chemistry
https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-091-int...

NIST – Materials Science and Engineering Division
https://www.nist.gov/mml/materials-sc...

ASM International – Materials Resources
https://www.asminternational.org/mate...

Imperial College London – What is Materials Science?
https://www.imperial.ac.uk/engineerin...
Cosmo Explains the evolution of human craftsmanship, from early stone tool selection to the innovative use of metals. This journey highlights how ancient practices, like quenching and forging, relied on an intuitive grasp of atomic structures long before science officially defined these complex physical properties.
