Artificial superintelligence could become one of the most powerful—and unsettling—technologies humanity has ever created.

In this slow, sleep-friendly deep dive, we explore how AI evolved from early neural networks to modern transformers, why larger systems can develop surprising new abilities, and the risks researchers discuss around alignment, autonomous agents, recursive self-improvement, containment, and control.

From AGI and the intelligence explosion to AI safety and the challenge of keeping systems aligned with human values, this video explores what could happen if machines eventually become far more intelligent than us.

Resources:

Stanford HAI — 2026 AI Index: Technical Performance
https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/202...

Attention Is All You Need — Transformer Research Paper
https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762

Anthropic — Constitutional AI Research
https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073

METR — AI Evaluation & Autonomous Capabilities Research
https://metr.org/research/
Cosmo Explains examines the history, technical mechanisms, and theoretical risks of advanced artificial intelligence. The discussion explores concepts like the alignment problem, recursive self-improvement, and the potential for emergent capabilities, offering a thought-provoking look at why experts are increasingly concerned about the future of autonomous systems.
