Everything About the Psychology of Being Alone Explained Slowly (For Sleep)
KI


Cosmo Explains Psychology und Cosmo Explains

Abonniert

12





535 Aufrufe  12.09.2026
What really happens in your mind when you're alone?

This video explores the psychology of being alone, from the difference between solitude and loneliness to the way social disconnection can affect the brain and body. We look at attachment, social pain, the default mode network, rumination, creativity, mindfulness, and why chosen solitude can sometimes be deeply restorative.

Settle in and explore what psychology and neuroscience reveal about loneliness, solitude, and learning to feel comfortable in your own company.

Resources:

U.S. Surgeon General — Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation
https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/fil...

PLOS Medicine — Social Relationships and Mortality Risk
https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed....

National Library of Medicine — The Neural Bases of Social Pain
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles...

National Library of Medicine — Meditation Experience and the Default Mode Network
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles...
