0:000 SekundenRight now, as you're lying here, your brain is doing something it almost never gets credit for. It's running a kind of
0:099 Sekundenquiet background scan, checking for the presence of other people.
0:1414 SekundenNot consciously, not in any way you'd notice. But somewhere beneath the surface, your mind
0:2121 Sekundenis keeping a kind of social ledger, a running tally of how connected you feel to the people in your life, how recently
0:2828 Sekundenyou've been around them, how close or how far away they seem. And even though you're alone right now, probably in a
0:3636 Sekundendark room, probably with your eyes closed or closing, your brain is still doing it, still checking, still keeping
0:4444 Sekundenscore. That's one of the strangest things about being a person. You can be perfectly comfortable, perfectly safe,
0:5252 Sekundenlying in a warm bed with nowhere to be, and some part of your mind is still quietly monitoring your social position
0:5959 Sekundenin the world. Whether you feel alone or simply by yourself, whether the solitude you're in right now is something you
1:071 Minute, 7 Sekundenchose or something that was handed to you. And the difference between those two, between being alone and feeling alone, turns out to be one of the most
1:161 Minute, 16 Sekundenimportant distinctions in all of psychology. Tonight is about that, the whole of it.
1:231 Minute, 23 SekundenWhat happens in the mind when you're alone, why it feels the way it does, where the ache of loneliness actually
1:311 Minute, 31 Sekundencomes from, and what it was originally built to do. why some people crave solitude and others dread it and what
1:391 Minute, 39 Sekundendecades of careful research have revealed about what being alone actually does to the brain, the body, and the
1:461 Minute, 46 Sekundensense of who you are. It's a subject that touches almost every corner of psychology, from evolution to
1:531 Minute, 53 Sekundenneuroscience to philosophy to the replication crisis. And it's one of those rare topics where the science is genuinely more interesting than the
2:022 Minuten, 2 Sekundenpopular version. So, settle in. There's nothing you need to hold on to here. No test at the end. No steps to remember.
2:112 Minuten, 11 SekundenIf you drift off somewhere in the middle, that's fine. The ideas will still be here.
2:172 Minuten, 17 SekundenLet's start with something that might seem obvious, but turns out to be surprisingly hard to pin down.
2:242 Minuten, 24 SekundenWhat do we actually mean when we say someone is alone? because the word does at least three different jobs in English
2:312 Minuten, 31 Sekundenand psychology has spent a long time trying to pull them apart.
2:362 Minuten, 36 SekundenThere's solitude which is the physical state of being by yourself. No one else in the room, no one on the phone, no one
2:452 Minuten, 45 Sekundenexpecting anything from you. Solitude is a condition like temperature.
2:512 Minuten, 51 SekundenIt's measurable. You can count the hours someone spends alone in a day. And researchers have done exactly that with
2:592 Minuten, 59 Sekundentime use diaries and experience sampling and more recently with smartphones that ping you at random intervals and ask you
3:073 Minuten, 7 Sekundento report who you're with and how you feel. The numbers are interesting. The average American adult spends somewhere
3:143 Minuten, 14 Sekundenaround 6 to 7 hours a day alone. Though that varies enormously by age, by personality, by whether you live with
3:223 Minuten, 22 Sekundenother people, by culture, by a dozen other things. But the point is that solitude is just a description of the situation.
3:323 Minuten, 32 SekundenIt doesn't tell you how the person feels about it. Then there's loneliness, which is not a situation at all. Loneliness is
3:423 Minuten, 42 Sekundena feeling, a subjective experience, and it's defined in the research literature as the distressing feeling that arises
3:493 Minuten, 49 Sekundenfrom a perceived gap between the social connections you have and the social connections you want. That word
3:573 Minuten, 57 Sekundenperceived is doing a lot of work in that sentence. You can be surrounded by people and feel desperately lonely. You
4:054 Minuten, 5 Sekundencan be entirely by yourself and feel perfectly content.
4:104 Minuten, 10 SekundenLoneliness is not about how many people are around you. It's about whether the connections you have feel like enough
4:174 Minuten, 17 Sekundenand whether they feel like the right kind. And then there's a third thing which doesn't have a single clean word
4:254 Minuten, 25 Sekundenin English, though the Germans, as usual, got there first.
4:294 Minuten, 29 SekundenThere's a kind of chosen aloneeness, a deliberate withdrawal from the social world that isn't about feeling
4:364 Minuten, 36 Sekundendisconnected, but about wanting, maybe needing, a stretch of time with no one else's needs pressing against yours.
4:444 Minuten, 44 SekundenSome researchers call this the capacity for solitude. And it was a British psychoanalyst named Donald Winnott who
4:524 Minuten, 52 Sekundenfirst made a serious case back in the late 1950s that this capacity is not just normal but essential to
4:594 Minuten, 59 Sekundenpsychological health. Winnott is worth spending a moment with because his idea was genuinely counterintuitive.
5:075 Minuten, 7 SekundenHe was a pediatrician before he was a psychoanalyst, which meant he spent years watching mothers and infants before he ever sat
5:165 Minuten, 16 Sekundenbehind a couch listening to adults. And what he noticed, what he built a whole theory around was that the ability to be
5:265 Minuten, 26 Sekundencomfortably alone doesn't come from being left alone.
5:315 Minuten, 31 SekundenIt comes from the opposite. It comes from having been reliably accompanied.
5:385 Minuten, 38 SekundenHere's what he meant. A very young child, maybe a year old, sitting on the floor playing with blocks while the
5:465 Minuten, 46 Sekundenmother is in the room, reading or folding laundry, not interacting with the child, but present.
5:545 Minuten, 54 SekundenThe child is in a meaningful sense alone, absorbed in the blocks, not looking at
6:026 Minuten, 2 Sekundenthe mother, not needing anything from her in that moment. But the child can only be alone like that because the
6:096 Minuten, 9 Sekundenmother is there. The safety of her presence is what makes the aloneeness possible.
6:166 Minuten, 16 SekundenWinnott called this the capacity to be alone in the presence of the other. and he argued that it was one of the most
6:246 Minuten, 24 Sekundenimportant developmental achievements of early life.
6:286 Minuten, 28 SekundenThe idea was that if you had enough of that experience, enough safe accompanied aloneeness as a small child, you
6:376 Minuten, 37 Sekundeninternalized the sense of safety. You carried it with you and eventually you could be alone without anyone in the
6:456 Minuten, 45 Sekundenroom at all because you had a kind of internal companion, a felt sense that you were held, that someone existed, who knew you and to whom you could return.
6:586 Minuten, 58 SekundenNow, Winnott was a psychoanalyst, and psychoanalytic ideas are notoriously hard to test in a lab. You can't
7:077 Minuten, 7 Sekundenrandomize infants into enough accompanied solitude and not enough accompanied solitude groups and then check back in 30 years.
7:187 Minuten, 18 SekundenBut the core intuition that early attachment security predicts later comfort with being alone has actually
7:257 Minuten, 25 Sekundenheld up reasonably well in the developmental research that came after him.
7:317 Minuten, 31 SekundenAttachment theory, which was developed by John Bulby and then operationalized brilliantly by Mary Ainsworth in the
7:397 Minuten, 39 Sekunden1960s and 70s, gave researchers a way to measure exactly the kind of thing Winnott was describing. Ainsworth's
7:487 Minuten, 48 Sekundenstrange situation experiment where a toddler is briefly separated from the caregiver in an unfamiliar room and then
7:567 Minuten, 56 Sekundenreunited was designed to reveal how the child had organized their expectations about closeness and safety.
8:058 Minuten, 5 SekundenAnd one of the consistent findings over decades of follow-up work is that securely attached children, the ones who
8:138 Minuten, 13 Sekundenprotested the separation, but then settled quickly when the caregiver returned, tend to grow into adults who
8:208 Minuten, 20 Sekundenare more comfortable with solitude. They seek out alone time without anxiety.
8:268 Minuten, 26 SekundenThey don't interpret being by themselves as evidence that something has gone wrong. Think about what that means for a second.
8:358 Minuten, 35 SekundenThe capacity to enjoy being alone, which we tend to think of as a personality trait, something you either have or you
8:438 Minuten, 43 Sekundendon't, might actually be something that was built in the first few years of your life by someone else's presence.
8:528 Minuten, 52 SekundenYour comfort with your own company might be in part a gift from someone who was reliably there when you were too young
9:009 Minutento remember. That's the Winnott thread and we'll come back to it because it connects to something much larger about
9:099 Minuten, 9 Sekundenhow the mind handles the absence of other people. But there's a completely different way into this question and it
9:179 Minuten, 17 Sekundenstarts not with infants but with evolution.
9:229 Minuten, 22 SekundenBecause if you step back far enough, the really puzzling thing about loneliness is not that it exists but that it hurts.
9:319 Minuten, 31 SekundenWhy should the mere absence of other people produce something that feels in the body remarkably similar to physical pain?
9:419 Minuten, 41 SekundenThat's not a metaphor. By the way, Naomi Eisenberger, a neuroscientist at UCLA,
9:489 Minuten, 48 Sekundenpublished a study in 2003 that became one of the most cited papers in social neuroscience.
9:559 Minuten, 55 SekundenShe and her colleagues had people lie in a brain scanner and play a simple ball tossing game on a screen. The game was
10:0310 Minuten, 3 Sekundenrigged. At a certain point, the other two players, who were actually just a computer program, stopped throwing the
10:1110 Minuten, 11 Sekundenball to the participant. They just left them out. And when Eisenberger looked at what happened in the brain during that
10:1910 Minuten, 19 Sekundenexclusion, she found activation in the dorsal anterior singulate cortex and the anterior insula regions that are also
10:2810 Minuten, 28 Sekundenactive during physical pain. The overlap wasn't perfect, and later work has refined the picture considerably. The
10:3710 Minuten, 37 Sekundenrelationship between social pain and physical pain is more nuanced than the early headlines suggested. But the basic
10:4610 Minuten, 46 Sekundenfinding that social exclusion activates some of the same neural architecture as a physical injury has been replicated in
10:5410 Minuten, 54 Sekundenvarious forms and remains one of the more striking results in the field.
11:0011 MinutenAnd it raises the obvious question, why would the brain wire social disconnection into the pain system?
11:0811 Minuten, 8 SekundenWhat's the evolutionary logic?
11:1211 Minuten, 12 SekundenThis is where a psychologist named John Kaciopo enters the story and he's going to be a central figure tonight because
11:2011 Minuten, 20 Sekundenhe spent the better part of three decades trying to answer that question.
11:2511 Minuten, 25 SekundenKaciopo was at the University of Chicago and he was more than anyone else the person who turned loneliness from a soft
11:3411 Minuten, 34 Sekundenvaguely literary topic into a hard science. He founded what he called social neuroscience, the study of how
11:4311 Minuten, 43 Sekundensocial processes play out in the brain and body. And his particular obsession was loneliness, not as a character flaw
11:5211 Minuten, 52 Sekundenor a lifestyle problem, but as a biological signal.
11:5711 Minuten, 57 SekundenHis argument, which he laid out in a series of papers and then in a book called loneliness, published in 2008, went something like this.
12:0812 Minuten, 8 SekundenFor most of human evolutionary history, being separated from the group was genuinely dangerous.
12:1612 Minuten, 16 SekundenNot emotionally dangerous, physically dangerous. A human alone on the savannah was a human who couldn't share the work
12:2412 Minuten, 24 Sekundenof finding food, couldn't benefit from collective vigilance against predators, couldn't reproduce, and couldn't survive
12:3212 Minuten, 32 Sekundenillness or injury. Isolation for most of the history of our species was a death
12:3812 Minuten, 38 Sekundensentence. And so the brain evolved a warning system, a signal that would fire
12:4512 Minuten, 45 Sekundenwhen your social connections dropped below a safe threshold, a signal unpleasant enough to motivate you to do
12:5312 Minuten, 53 Sekundensomething about it. That signal is loneliness.
12:5812 Minuten, 58 SekundenKaciopo compared it to hunger. Hunger is not a disease. Hunger is a signal that
13:0513 Minuten, 5 Sekundenyou need to eat. It's uncomfortable by design because if it weren't uncomfortable, you wouldn't bother to
13:1213 Minuten, 12 Sekundenfind food and you'd die. Loneliness, in his model, works the same way. It's not
13:1913 Minuten, 19 Sekundena disorder. It's not a weakness. It's a biological alarm calibrated by evolution, telling you that your level
13:2713 Minuten, 27 Sekundenof social connection has dropped below what your brain considers safe and that you need to do something to restore it
13:3513 Minuten, 35 Sekundenand that reframing changes everything about how you think about the experience.
13:4213 Minuten, 42 SekundenIf loneliness is a signal rather than a failing, then feeling lonely doesn't mean there's something wrong with you.
13:5013 Minuten, 50 SekundenIt means the system is working.
13:5413 Minuten, 54 SekundenYour brain noticed a gap and it's telling you about it. The same way it tells you you're thirsty or cold.
14:0214 Minuten, 2 SekundenThe problem isn't the signal. The problem is what happens when the signal stays on for too long. When loneliness
14:1114 Minuten, 11 Sekundenbecomes chronic, when the alarm keeps ringing and you can't find a way to turn it off.
14:1814 Minuten, 18 Sekundenbecause that's when things start to go sideways. Not just emotionally, but physically in ways that Kachiopo and
14:2714 Minuten, 27 Sekundenothers spent years documenting with a precision that genuinely surprised the medical community.
14:3514 Minuten, 35 SekundenBut before we get to what chronic loneliness does to the body and the findings there are striking enough that
14:4214 Minuten, 42 Sekundenthey changed public health policy in at least two countries. There's something else worth understanding first.
14:5114 Minuten, 51 SekundenSomething about how the lonely brain starts to see the world differently. how it begins to shift the way it processes
14:5814 Minuten, 58 Sekundenfaces, voices, and intentions in ways that can make the loneliness harder to escape.
15:0615 Minuten, 6 SekundenKaciopo called it the hypervigilance model. And it starts with something your brain is doing right now. Even as you
15:1315 Minuten, 13 Sekundenlie here in the dark, even in the quiet, your brain is scanning. It does this constantly, monitoring the environment for anything that might matter.
15:2515 Minuten, 25 Sekundenanything that might be a threat, anything that might need your attention.
15:3115 Minuten, 31 SekundenAnd most of the time, you're not aware of it at all. It's running in the background like a program you never
15:3815 Minuten, 38 Sekundenopened, sorting through sounds and shadows and small shifts in the air, deciding what to flag and what to let
15:4615 Minuten, 46 Sekundenpass. In a social context, this same machinery is doing something remarkably specific.
15:5415 Minuten, 54 SekundenIt's reading faces.
15:5615 Minuten, 56 SekundenIt's listening to tone of voice. It's picking up on posture, on the timing of a reply, on whether someone's smile
16:0416 Minuten, 4 Sekundenreaches their eyes or stops at their mouth. And it's doing all of this in fractions of a second, long before the
16:1316 Minuten, 13 Sekundenconscious reasoning part of your mind has weighed in. What Kachiopo found, and this is the part that makes chronic
16:2016 Minuten, 20 Sekundenloneliness so insidious, is that when someone has been lonely for a sustained period,
16:2716 Minuten, 27 Sekundenthis scanning system doesn't shut down, it speeds up. It becomes more sensitive,
16:3516 Minuten, 35 Sekundenmore reactive, and critically more biased toward threat. The lonely brain doesn't stop wanting connection. it
16:4516 Minuten, 45 Sekundenstarts expecting rejection. And because this happens at the level of automatic processing, at the speed of perception
16:5316 Minuten, 53 Sekundenitself, the person experiencing it usually has no idea it's happening. Here's what that looks like in practice.
17:0217 Minuten, 2 SekundenKaciopo and his colleagues ran a series of studies in the early 2000s where they showed participants photographs of
17:0917 Minuten, 9 Sekundenfaces, some expressing warmth or friendliness, some neutral, some hostile or disapproving.
17:1717 Minuten, 17 SekundenThe faces flashed quickly, sometimes for just a few hundred milliseconds, barely long enough to register consciously.
17:2617 Minuten, 26 SekundenWhat they found was that people who scored high on chronic loneliness were significantly faster at detecting the
17:3317 Minuten, 33 Sekundennegative faces. Their brains locked onto disapproval, onto hostility, onto social
17:3917 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthreat with a speed and accuracy that the nonlonely participants didn't match.
17:4617 Minuten, 46 SekundenBut here's the flip side, and it's the one that matters. The lonely participants were also slower to
17:5317 Minuten, 53 Sekundenregister the positive faces, warmth, friendliness, openness. These signals didn't land as quickly, didn't carry the
18:0218 Minuten, 2 Sekundensame weight, didn't get the same priority in the brain's sorting system.
18:0818 Minuten, 8 SekundenThink about what that means for a moment. If your perceptual system is tuned to spot rejection faster than acceptance, then every social interaction becomes subtly skewed.
18:2018 Minuten, 20 SekundenYou walk into a room and your brain is already scanning for the person who doesn't want you there. For the micro expression that confirms you don't
18:2818 Minuten, 28 Sekundenbelong, for the pause in conversation that might mean you've said the wrong thing. and the warm signals, the genuine
18:3718 Minuten, 37 Sekundensmile, the invitation to sit down, the laugh that means someone actually enjoyed what you said. Those arrive a
18:4518 Minuten, 45 Sekundenbeat late, muffled, less convincing. You might register them, but they don't land with the same force. They don't feel as real. And this is where the trap closes.
18:5918 Minuten, 59 SekundenBecause if you're reading social situations as more threatening than they actually are, you start to behave
19:0519 Minuten, 5 Sekundendifferently. You hold back. You don't initiate. You leave early. You interpret
19:1319 Minuten, 13 Sekundenambiguity as rejection rather than indifference or even friendliness.
19:1919 Minuten, 19 SekundenAnd the people around you who have no idea any of this is happening inside your head start to respond to the version of you that's pulling away.
19:3019 Minuten, 30 SekundenThey give you space because you seem to want it. They stop inviting you because you keep saying no. And now the
19:3919 Minuten, 39 Sekundenloneliness deepens, which makes the hypervigilance worse, which makes the social world look even more hostile, which makes you pull back further.
19:5019 Minuten, 50 SekundenKaciopo described this as a self-reinforcing loop, and it's one of the most elegant and one of the most
19:5819 Minuten, 58 Sekundenheartbreaking feedback mechanisms in all of psychology.
20:0220 Minuten, 2 SekundenNow, you might be thinking, well, can't you just decide to see things differently?
20:1020 Minuten, 10 SekundenCan't you just override the bias? And the honest answer is it's harder than it sounds. Precisely because so much of this operates below conscious awareness.
20:2120 Minuten, 21 SekundenBy the time you're sitting at a dinner party feeling like nobody really wants you there, the perceptual work has already been done. The faces have
20:3020 Minuten, 30 Sekundenalready been sorted. The threat signals have already been flagged. Your conscious mind is receiving a summary
20:3820 Minuten, 38 Sekundenthat's already been edited. And you experience that edited summary as reality. You don't experience it as a bias.
20:4720 Minuten, 47 SekundenYou experience it as the truth about how people feel about you. That's what makes it so difficult to correct from the inside.
20:5620 Minuten, 56 SekundenThere's a study that illustrates this beautifully, though it comes from a slightly different angle.
21:0221 Minuten, 2 SekundenNaomi Eisenberger, the same researcher who did the brain scanning work on social pain that we touched on earlier,
21:1021 Minuten, 10 Sekundenran an experiment where participants played a simple ball tossing game on a computer. The game was called Cyber
21:1821 Minuten, 18 SekundenBall, and it was designed to look like you were playing catch with two other people online, except the other players weren't real.
21:2921 Minuten, 29 SekundenThey were programmed.
21:3121 Minuten, 31 SekundenAnd at a certain point in the game, the two other players started throwing the ball only to each other, leaving the
21:3721 Minuten, 37 Sekundenparticipant out. Just a silly computer game, a cartoon ball bouncing between cartoon figures.
21:4621 Minuten, 46 SekundenBut the brain scans told a different story. The same regions that activate during physical pain, the dorsal
21:5321 Minuten, 53 Sekundenanterior singulate cortex and the anterior insula lit up when people were excluded from this trivial meaningless
22:0222 Minuten, 2 Sekundengame with strangers who didn't even exist. And here's the thing, participants who reported higher baseline loneliness showed stronger
22:1122 Minuten, 11 Sekundenactivation in those pain regions. The exclusion hurt more if you were already lonely. The alarm was louder because the
22:1922 Minuten, 19 Sekundensystem was already primed. So, how do we actually know that this isn't just correlation? That loneliness isn't
22:2822 Minuten, 28 Sekundensimply co-occurring with a more sensitive temperament or a more anxious disposition.
22:3422 Minuten, 34 SekundenThis is where Kaciopo's longitudinal work becomes important. He and his team, particularly working with Louise Hawkley
22:4222 Minuten, 42 Sekundenat the University of Chicago, followed large groups of people over years, measuring loneliness, social behavior,
22:4922 Minuten, 49 Sekundenhealth markers, and cognitive patterns at regular intervals. And what the longitudinal data showed was that
22:5722 Minuten, 57 Sekundenloneliness predicted changes in social cognition over time. It wasn't that anxious people happened to become
23:0423 Minuten, 4 Sekundenlonely. It was that loneliness itself sustained over months and years shifted
23:1123 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthe way the brain processed social information.
23:1523 Minuten, 15 SekundenThe hypervigilance wasn't a pre-existing trait. It was a consequence. The alarm left ringing started to change the
23:2323 Minuten, 23 Sekundenwiring. And this is genuinely important because it means chronic loneliness isn't just an unpleasant feeling that
23:3123 Minuten, 31 Sekundensits there passively while you wait for it to pass. It's an active process. It reshapes perception. It alters behavior.
23:4123 Minuten, 41 SekundenIt narrows the social world, not by removing opportunities, but by making those opportunities look more dangerous
23:4823 Minuten, 48 Sekundenthan they are. And all of this happens gradually, quietly, in a way that feels less like something going wrong and more
23:5723 Minuten, 57 Sekundenlike the world simply being the way it is. Let's sit with that for a second because there's something almost
24:0324 Minuten, 3 Sekundenparadoxical about it. The system that evolved to push you toward reconnection.
24:1024 Minuten, 10 SekundenThe loneliness alarm is the same system that when it stays on too long makes reconnection harder. It's like a smoke
24:1924 Minuten, 19 Sekundendetector that after going off for hours starts to make you afraid of your own kitchen. The signal is correct in its origin.
24:2824 Minuten, 28 SekundenThere is a gap. You do need connection.
24:3224 Minuten, 32 SekundenBut the downstream effects of the signal itself start to work against the very thing the signal is trying to achieve.
24:3924 Minuten, 39 SekundenKaciopo was careful to point out that this doesn't mean lonely people are doing something wrong or that they're somehow responsible for their own isolation.
24:4924 Minuten, 49 SekundenThe hypervigilance model is descriptive, not prescriptive. It's describing what happens in the brain under sustained
24:5724 Minuten, 57 Sekundensocial deprivation. The same way a nutritionist might describe what happens in the body under sustained caloric deprivation.
25:0625 Minuten, 6 SekundenYou don't blame a starving person for becoming obsessed with food. And you don't blame a lonely person for becoming
25:1325 Minuten, 13 Sekundenhyperattuned to social threat. Both are the brain doing exactly what it was built to do under conditions of deficit.
25:2225 Minuten, 22 SekundenBut knowing this, understanding the mechanism does open a door. Because if the problem isn't just I need more
25:3025 Minuten, 30 Sekundenfriends, but also my perceptual system has been recalibrated by months or years of isolation and it's now filtering
25:3925 Minuten, 39 Sekundensocial reality in a way that confirms the isolation. Then the intervention looks different. It's not just about
25:4725 Minuten, 47 Sekundenincreasing social contact, though that matters. It's about addressing the cognitive shift that chronic loneliness
25:5425 Minuten, 54 Sekundenproduces. And this is exactly where some of the more recent therapeutic approaches have focused, particularly
26:0126 Minuten, 1 Sekundework on what's sometimes called maladaptive social cognition, helping people recognize and gently correct the
26:0926 Minuten, 9 Sekundenbiased threat detection that loneliness installs.
26:1326 Minuten, 13 SekundenWhich brings us to the body. Because Kaciopo's hypervigilance model explains how loneliness perpetuates itself psychologically.
26:2226 Minuten, 22 SekundenBut the story doesn't stop at perception and behavior. The same sustained alarm state that reshapes how you see faces
26:3026 Minuten, 30 Sekundenand read intentions also reaches deep into physiology, into the immune system, into
26:3726 Minuten, 37 Sekundencardiovascular function, into the way your genes are expressed. And the findings here are honestly some of the
26:4626 Minuten, 46 Sekundenmost striking in all of health psychology.
26:4926 Minuten, 49 SekundenWhen Kashiopo and Steve Cole, a genomics researcher at UCLA, started looking at what chronic loneliness does at the
26:5726 Minuten, 57 Sekundencellular level, what they found was so pronounced that it forced a conversation about whether loneliness should be
27:0527 Minuten, 5 Sekundentreated not just as a psychological experience, but as a public health concern on the level of smoking or obesity.
27:1327 Minuten, 13 SekundenAnd what Cole found starting in the mid 2000s was that loneliness doesn't just feel bad, it changes which genes your body decides to use.
27:2627 Minuten, 26 SekundenNow, that sentence needs a little unpacking because gene expression is one of those phrases that sounds either
27:3327 Minuten, 33 Sekundenterrifyingly complicated or vaguely meaningless depending on how much biology you've encountered.
27:4127 Minuten, 41 SekundenSo, let's slow down. You have roughly 20,000 genes, give or take. And at any given moment, most of them are not
27:4927 Minuten, 49 Sekundenactive. They're sitting there in every cell, but they're switched off, quiet, like apps on your phone that are
27:5627 Minuten, 56 Sekundeninstalled, but not running. What determines which genes get switched on, which ones get expressed, is largely a matter of context.
28:0728 Minuten, 7 SekundenWhat signals is the body receiving? what does it think it needs right now? And those signals come from hormones, from
28:1528 Minuten, 15 Sekundenthe immune system, from stress responses, from all the regulatory machinery that keeps you alive. So when
28:2328 Minuten, 23 SekundenCole looked at the white blood cells of chronically lonely people and compared them to the white blood cells of socially connected people, he wasn't
28:3128 Minuten, 31 Sekundenlooking at whether lonely people had different genes. They didn't. He was looking at whether the same genes were
28:3828 Minuten, 38 Sekundenbeing used differently and they were dramatically.
28:4328 Minuten, 43 SekundenWhat Cole found and published in a landmark paper in 2007 was a distinct pattern of gene expression that he eventually called the
28:5228 Minuten, 52 Sekundenconserved transcriptional response to adversity or CT.
28:5828 Minuten, 58 SekundenIn lonely individuals, genes involved in inflammation were upregulated, meaning they were more active than normal,
29:0529 Minuten, 5 Sekundencranking out more of the proteins associated with the body's inflammatory response. And at the same time, genes
29:1229 Minuten, 12 Sekundeninvolved in antiviral defense were downregulated, dialed back, quieter than they should have been. So the immune
29:2129 Minuten, 21 Sekundensystem was essentially being reconfigured.
29:2429 Minuten, 24 Sekundenmore inflammation, less antiviral protection. And this pattern wasn't subtle. It was consistent enough to show
29:3329 Minuten, 33 Sekundenup across multiple studies in different populations in different countries.
29:3929 Minuten, 39 SekundenHere's the thing about that pattern. It makes a strange kind of sense if you think about it from an evolutionary
29:4629 Minuten, 46 Sekundenangle, which is exactly how Cole and Kachiopo interpreted it. If you're a social animal and you find yourself
29:5329 Minuten, 53 Sekundenisolated from the group, what are the most likely threats you're going to face? Not viruses which spread through close contact with other individuals.
30:0430 Minuten, 4 SekundenYou're alone, so viral exposure drops, but physical danger, wounds, bacterial
30:1130 Minuten, 11 Sekundeninfection from injuries, those go up because you don't have the group to protect you. So the body reading the
30:1930 Minuten, 19 Sekundensustained signal of social isolation shifts its immune priorities.
30:2530 Minuten, 25 SekundenIt ramps up the inflammatory response which is good for fighting bacteria that enter through wounds and it dials back
30:3330 Minuten, 33 Sekundenthe antiviral response which is less needed if you're not around other people.
30:4030 Minuten, 40 SekundenThe immune system is essentially preparing for a life lived alone in the wild. And you've almost certainly never thought about your immune system this way. But it's quietly remarkable.
30:5230 Minuten, 52 SekundenYour body is not just responding to pathogens it encounters. It's anticipating which pathogens it's likely to encounter based on your social environment.
31:0331 Minuten, 3 SekundenIt's making a bet. And for most of human history, that bet would have been a good one.
31:1131 Minuten, 11 SekundenThe problem, of course, is that modern loneliness doesn't actually mean you're alone in the wilderness. You might be
31:1831 Minuten, 18 Sekundenlonely in a crowded city, riding the subway, working in an open plan office, surrounded by people whose germs you're
31:2631 Minuten, 26 Sekundenabsolutely exposed to. So, the immune reconfiguration that made sense on the savannah is now actively counterproductive.
31:3531 Minuten, 35 SekundenYou're getting more inflammation, which over time contributes to cardiovascular disease, to type 2 diabetes, to
31:4331 Minuten, 43 Sekundenneurodeenerative conditions, and you're getting less antiviral protection in a world where viruses are,
31:5031 Minuten, 50 Sekundenif anything, more of a threat than they were to a solitary hunter gatherer.
31:5631 Minuten, 56 SekundenThis is where the public health argument starts to sharpen. Because inflammation is not just an immune response. Chronic
32:0332 Minuten, 3 Sekundenlow-grade inflammation, the kind that the CT pattern promotes, is one of the most welldocumented risk factors for a whole range of serious illnesses.
32:1432 Minuten, 14 SekundenJulianne Holt Lunstad, a psychologist at Brigham Young University, pulled together a massive metaanalysis in 2010,
32:2232 Minuten, 22 Sekundendrawing on data from over 300,000 participants across 148 studies and found that people with stronger social
32:3132 Minuten, 31 Sekundenrelationships had a 50% greater likelihood of survival over the study periods than those with weaker connections. 50%.
32:4232 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd when she followed that up with another metaanalysis in 2015, this one looking specifically at
32:4932 Minuten, 49 Sekundenloneliness, social isolation, and living alone as risk factors for mortality. The numbers were stark enough that she
32:5732 Minuten, 57 Sekundencompared the effect size to smoking 15 cigarettes a day, not smoking in general, 15 cigarettes a day.
33:0733 Minuten, 7 SekundenNow, you should hold that comparison lightly because metaanalyses are complicated things and effect sizes
33:1433 Minuten, 14 Sekundenacross very different studies don't translate into neat equivalences.
33:2033 Minuten, 20 SekundenHolt Lunad herself has been careful to say that the comparison is meant to convey magnitude, not to suggest that
33:2733 Minuten, 27 Sekundenloneliness and smoking operate through identical pathways. But the broader point stands.
33:3533 Minuten, 35 SekundenLoneliness is not just an unpleasant feeling. It is associated with measurably increased risk of early death
33:4233 Minuten, 42 Sekundenand the mechanisms through which it operates. The cardiovascular strain, the inflammatory profile, the disrupted
33:5033 Minuten, 50 Sekundensleep, the elevated cortisol are physiologically real and increasingly well documented. And this is where the
33:5933 Minuten, 59 Sekundenconversation started to shift in the mid2010s from academic research into something closer to policy.
34:0834 Minuten, 8 SekundenIn 2018, the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness, the first governmental position of its kind,
34:1734 Minuten, 17 Sekundenprompted in part by a report from the Joe Cox Commission on Loneliness that drew heavily on the kind of research we've been talking about.
34:2534 Minuten, 25 SekundenJapan followed with its own minister of loneliness in 2021, partly in response to rising isolation
34:3334 Minuten, 33 Sekundenduring the pandemic, but building on concerns that predated it. The surgeon general of the United States, Vivvec
34:4134 Minuten, 41 SekundenMurthy, issued an advisory in 2023 explicitly framing loneliness and social
34:4834 Minuten, 48 Sekundendisconnection as a public health crisis, citing Hol Lunstad's work and the broader evidence base.
34:5634 Minuten, 56 SekundenThese are not symbolic gestures. They represent a genuine reclassification of what loneliness is from a private
35:0335 Minuten, 3 Sekundenemotional state to a population level health risk that warrants systemic intervention.
35:1135 Minuten, 11 SekundenThink about what that means for a second. For most of the 20th century, if you were lonely, the implicit cultural message was that this was your problem.
35:2235 Minuten, 22 SekundenYou needed to be more outgoing, more likable, more willing to put yourself out there.
35:2835 Minuten, 28 SekundenThe framing was almost entirely individual, almost entirely about personality or effort. And what the
35:3535 Minuten, 35 Sekundenresearch has done piece by piece is dismantle that framing. Loneliness is
35:4135 Minuten, 41 Sekundennot a character flaw. It's a biological state with measurable physiological consequences driven by an evolved alarm
35:5035 Minuten, 50 Sekundensystem that can become self-reinforcing through mechanisms the person experiencing it cannot simply override. by trying harder.
35:5935 Minuten, 59 SekundenThat's a fundamentally different picture and it demands a fundamentally different response.
36:0536 Minuten, 5 SekundenBut here's where it gets genuinely complicated. And honestly, this is the part of the loneliness literature that I find most interesting. Because if you
36:1436 Minuten, 14 Sekundenaccept that loneliness is a public health issue, you need interventions.
36:1936 Minuten, 19 Sekundenand the obvious intervention just increase social contact just get lonely people into more social situations
36:2736 Minuten, 27 Sekundenturns out to be surprisingly ineffective on its own. Christopher MSI working with Kaciopo published a metaanalysis in 2011
36:3736 Minuten, 37 Sekundenlooking at the effectiveness of different types of loneliness interventions and the results were counterintuitive.
36:4536 Minuten, 45 SekundenPrograms that simply provided more opportunities for social interaction didn't do much. Programs that taught social skills didn't do much either.
36:5636 Minuten, 56 SekundenPrograms that enhanced social support showed modest effects.
37:0137 Minuten, 1 SekundeThe interventions that worked best by a meaningful margin were the ones that targeted maladaptive social cognition,
37:0837 Minuten, 8 Sekundenthe biased threat detection and negative interpretation patterns that chronic loneliness installs.
37:1537 Minuten, 15 SekundenWhich brings us back to the hypervigilance model we talked about earlier because this is where the mechanism and the treatment connect. If
37:2337 Minuten, 23 Sekundenchronic loneliness re-calibrates your social perception so that you're reading threat into neutral faces and expecting
37:3137 Minuten, 31 Sekundenrejection from ambiguous situations, then simply putting you in a room with friendly people doesn't fix the underlying problem.
37:4037 Minuten, 40 SekundenYou'll attend to the one person who didn't smile at you. You'll interpret the pause in conversation as discomfort rather than ease.
37:5037 Minuten, 50 SekundenYou'll leave feeling confirmed in your belief that connection is difficult and people are unreliable.
37:5837 Minuten, 58 SekundenThe room full of friendly people can't help you if your perceptual system is filtering them out. So the cognitive
38:0738 Minuten, 7 Sekundenintervention, the work of helping someone notice and gently question the interpretive biases that loneliness has
38:1438 Minuten, 14 Sekundeninstalled turns out to be the more effective lever.
38:1938 Minuten, 19 SekundenNot because social contact doesn't matter. It absolutely does. But because the cognitive shift has to happen first
38:2738 Minuten, 27 Sekundenor at least alongside for the social contact to land properly.
38:3238 Minuten, 32 SekundenAnd there's something quietly hopeful in that finding even though it sounds clinical. Because what it means is that
38:4038 Minuten, 40 Sekundenthe self-reinforcing loop we described, the one where loneliness biases perception, which prevents connection,
38:4738 Minuten, 47 Sekundenwhich deepens loneliness, that loop has a point of entry. It's not a closed system. The perceptual bias is real, but
38:5738 Minuten, 57 Sekundenit's also addressable. It was learned in the sense that the brain adapted to a sustained signal. And what was learned
39:0439 Minuten, 4 Sekundencan with patience and the right kind of support be unlearned.
39:0939 Minuten, 9 SekundenNot overnight and not by force of will, but through the kind of gradual recalibration that the brain is actually
39:1739 Minuten, 17 Sekundenquite good at when the conditions allow it. So that's the body. That's what loneliness does when it moves past the
39:2639 Minuten, 26 Sekundenpsychological and into the physiological.
39:3039 Minuten, 30 SekundenWhen the alarm that was meant to last hours or days, runs for months or years
39:3739 Minuten, 37 Sekundenand starts reshaping the immune system, the cardiovascular system, the very genes your cells choose to express.
39:4639 Minuten, 46 SekundenAnd the picture it paints is one where the mind and the body are not separate systems responding to loneliness
39:5439 Minuten, 54 Sekundenindependently, but a single integrated system in which the social world reaches all the way down to the cellular level.
40:0440 Minuten, 4 SekundenBut we've been talking about loneliness as though it's always the problem, as though being alone is always the thing
40:1240 Minuten, 12 Sekundenthat needs fixing. And that's only half the story. maybe less than half because there's another experience of being
40:2040 Minuten, 20 Sekundenalone that looks completely different that operates through different mechanisms and produces different
40:2740 Minuten, 27 Sekundenoutcomes. And that psychology took a surprisingly long time to take seriously.
40:3440 Minuten, 34 SekundenSolitude, chosen, voluntary, even nourishing solitude. And the question of what makes
40:4240 Minuten, 42 Sekundenthe difference, what turns a loneliness into loneliness for one person and into something restorative for another is one
40:4940 Minuten, 49 Sekundenof the most interesting questions in this whole territory. It takes us back to Winnott and forward into some
40:5740 Minuten, 57 Sekundenresearch that really only started gaining traction in the last couple of decades. Because for most of psychologyy's history, solitude was
41:0541 Minuten, 5 Sekundentreated as a problem to be solved, or at best as a neutral absence of social contact rather than as a psychological
41:1441 Minuten, 14 Sekundenstate with its own texture, its own value, and its own internal architecture.
41:2141 Minuten, 21 SekundenWinnott, as we touched on earlier, was one of the first to see it differently.
41:2641 Minuten, 26 SekundenHis idea published back in 1958 was that the capacity to be alone is one
41:3341 Minuten, 33 Sekundenof the most important signs of emotional maturity. Not the desire to be alone, not the preference for it, but the
41:4141 Minuten, 41 Sekundencapacity for it. The ability to sit in a room by yourself and feel neither anxious nor empty. And his explanation
41:5041 Minuten, 50 Sekundenfor how that capacity develops is one of those ideas that sounds paradoxical until you think about it and then it sounds obvious.
41:5941 Minuten, 59 SekundenHe said the capacity to be alone is learned in the presence of another person. A child playing on the floor while the mother reads nearby.
42:0942 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe child is alone in the sense that they're absorbed in their own activity, not seeking interaction, not performing
42:1742 Minuten, 17 Sekundenfor an audience, but they're alone in the company of someone who is reliably there. And what they're learning without knowing it is that aloneeness is safe.
42:2942 Minuten, 29 SekundenThat the world doesn't collapse when attention is withdrawn.
42:3442 Minuten, 34 Sekundenthat they can inhabit their own inner life without needing someone else to constantly confirm it. Now, Winnott was
42:4342 Minuten, 43 Sekundena psychoanalyst, so his language is clinical and his evidence is observational rather than experimental.
42:5142 Minuten, 51 SekundenBut the core insight has held up remarkably well.
42:5642 Minuten, 56 SekundenThe idea that secure attachment doesn't just make you better at being with people, but also makes you better at being without them. That's been tested
43:0443 Minuten, 4 Sekundenand supported in a number of ways since then.
43:0743 Minuten, 7 SekundenMario Mcculinser and Philip Shaver, two researchers who spent decades studying attachment in adults, published work in
43:1643 Minuten, 16 Sekundenthe 2000s, showing that securely attached adults report more comfort with solitude, less anxiety when alone, and a
43:2543 Minuten, 25 Sekundengreater ability to use time alone productively.
43:2943 Minuten, 29 SekundenThey don't need constant social contact to feel okay. They carry, as Michelinser put it, an internalized sense of
43:3743 Minuten, 37 Sekundensecurity that functions even when no one else is around.
43:4243 Minuten, 42 SekundenThink about what that means for a second. It means the experience of being alone is not determined by the fact of being alone.
43:5143 Minuten, 51 SekundenTwo people in identical circumstances, same empty apartment, same quiet evening, same absence of company, can be
44:0144 Minuten, 1 Sekundehaving completely different psychological experiences. One feels peaceful, maybe even restored.
44:0944 Minuten, 9 SekundenThe other feels a creeping unease that sharpens into something like dread.
44:1544 Minuten, 15 SekundenAnd the difference between them isn't about personality in any simple sense.
44:1944 Minuten, 19 SekundenAnd it's not about introversion versus extroversion, though we'll get to that.
44:2544 Minuten, 25 SekundenThe difference is partly about what their nervous system learned very early on about what aloneeness means, whether
44:3244 Minuten, 32 Sekundenit was paired with safety or with absence, whether the quiet was a comfortable quiet or an abandoned one.
44:4144 Minuten, 41 SekundenAnd this connects to something that Abraham Maslo was circling around in the 1960s, though he came at it from a very different direction.
44:5044 Minuten, 50 SekundenMaslo, the psychologist best known for his hierarchy of needs, spent the later part of his career studying what he
44:5744 Minuten, 57 Sekundencalled self-actualizing people, individuals who seemed to be functioning at a particularly high level of
45:0445 Minuten, 4 Sekundenpsychological health. And one of the characteristics he kept noticing in these people was a comfort with solitude that went beyond mere tolerance.
45:1545 Minuten, 15 SekundenThey actively sought it out. They used it for reflection, for creativity, for what Maslo described as a kind of direct encounter with their own experience.
45:2645 Minuten, 26 SekundenHe saw it as a feature of psychological maturity, not a symptom of withdrawal.
45:3245 Minuten, 32 SekundenNow, Maslo's methods were not rigorous by modern standards. His sample was tiny and handpicked, and the whole concept of
45:4145 Minuten, 41 Sekundenself-actualization has been criticized for being culturally specific and hard to operationalize.
45:4945 Minuten, 49 SekundenBut the observation itself that psychologically healthy people tend to have a different relationship with solitude than psychologically struggling
45:5745 Minuten, 57 Sekundenpeople has been supported by more careful work since.
46:0246 Minuten, 2 SekundenChristopher Long and James Aval, psychologists at the University of Massachusetts, published a paper in 2003
46:1146 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthat tried to pull apart the different ways people experience solitude.
46:1646 Minuten, 16 SekundenThey found that the key variable wasn't how much time someone spent alone, but how they construed that time.
46:2446 Minuten, 24 SekundenPeople who experience solitude as chosen and meaningful reported it as restorative. People who experienced it
46:3146 Minuten, 31 Sekundenas imposed or as evidence of social failure reported it as distressing.
46:3746 Minuten, 37 SekundenSame behavior, same observable state, completely different inner experience.
46:4346 Minuten, 43 SekundenAnd here's where it gets genuinely interesting because this distinction between chosen and unchosen solitude turns out to have measurable physiological coralates.
46:5546 Minuten, 55 SekundenTui Vinguan, a psychologist who has done some of the most careful recent work on solitude, published a series of studies
47:0347 Minuten, 3 Sekundenstarting around 2014, showing that even brief periods of solitude, as short as 15 minutes, reliably reduce high arousal emotions, both positive and negative.
47:1647 Minuten, 16 SekundenPeople who spent time alone reported feeling less anxious but also less excited, less angry, but also less enthusiastic.
47:2647 Minuten, 26 SekundenSolitude in her data acts as a kind of emotional thermostat, bringing everything toward the middle.
47:3347 Minuten, 33 SekundenShe called this effect deactivation, a dampening of emotional intensity across the board. And this is important
47:4247 Minuten, 42 Sekundenbecause it tells you something about what solitude is actually doing at the level of the nervous system.
47:4847 Minuten, 48 SekundenIt's not making you happy exactly. It's not a positive experience in the way that a good conversation or a satisfying
47:5747 Minuten, 57 Sekundenmeal is positive. What it's doing is reducing arousal. It's giving the system a chance to come down from the constant
48:0548 Minuten, 5 Sekundenlow-level activation that social interaction requires.
48:0948 Minuten, 9 SekundenBecause being around other people, even people you love, even people who make you feel safe, is work for the brain.
48:1748 Minuten, 17 SekundenYou're tracking facial expressions, monitoring tone of voice, managing your own self-presentation,
48:2448 Minuten, 24 Sekundenpredicting what the other person is thinking, adjusting your behavior in real time. All of that is cognitively
48:3248 Minuten, 32 Sekundenexpensive. And solitude, when it's chosen and when it feels safe, is a break from that expense. It's the
48:4048 Minuten, 40 Sekundenpsychological equivalent of taking off a heavy backpack you forgot you were wearing. You've almost certainly felt this yourself.
48:4948 Minuten, 49 SekundenThat moment when the last guest leaves or when you finally close the door after a long day of being around people and something in you just settles.
49:0149 Minuten, 1 SekundeNot because the people were bad or the interactions were unpleasant, but because the system that was managing
49:0749 Minuten, 7 Sekundenall of that contact can finally stand down. That settling is real. It's measurable.
49:1649 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd it's one of the reasons why even highly social, deeply extroverted people sometimes need to be alone, even if they
49:2549 Minuten, 25 Sekundenwouldn't describe themselves as needing solitude.
49:2949 Minuten, 29 SekundenWhich brings us to introversion and extraversion.
49:3349 Minuten, 33 SekundenBecause these are the concepts most people reach for when they think about why some people need more solitude than
49:4049 Minuten, 40 Sekundenothers. And the story here is more complicated than the popular version suggests. The popular version, the one
49:4849 Minuten, 48 Sekundenyou'll find in most personality quizzes and TED talks, says that introverts recharge by being alone and extroverts
49:5649 Minuten, 56 Sekundenrecharge by being around people. As though social energy is a battery that drains in different directions depending
50:0550 Minuten, 5 Sekundenon your type. And there's something to that. It captures a real pattern that people recognize in themselves.
50:1350 Minuten, 13 SekundenBut the underlying mechanism is not about social energy in any simple sense.
50:2050 Minuten, 20 SekundenHans Is the psychologist who did more than almost anyone to put introversion and extroversion on a scientific footing
50:3050 Minuten, 30 Sekundenproposed back in the 1960s that the difference was fundamentally about baseline arousal in the brain.
50:3850 Minuten, 38 SekundenIntroverts in his model have a higher baseline level of cortical arousal, meaning their brains are already running
50:4650 Minuten, 46 Sekundena bit hot neurologically speaking, even at rest. So they reach their optimal level of stimulation sooner, and social
50:5650 Minuten, 56 Sekundeninteraction, which is one of the most stimulating things a human brain can encounter, tips them into over stimulation faster. Extroverts, by
51:0551 Minuten, 5 Sekundencontrast, have a lower baseline. So they need more input to reach their sweet spot and social contact is one of the
51:1351 Minuten, 13 Sekundenmost reliable ways to get there. Now that's a simplification and later research has complicated it considerably.
51:2251 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe neuroscience of introversion and extroversion involves differences in dopamine sensitivity, in the relative
51:3051 Minuten, 30 Sekundenactivity of different neural pathways, and in how the brain responds to reward and novelty.
51:3751 Minuten, 37 SekundenColand Young and others have shown that extraversion is particularly linked to the sensitivity of the dopamine reward
51:4651 Minuten, 46 Sekundensystem. So it's not just about arousal but about how rewarding social interaction feels at the neurochemical
51:5451 Minuten, 54 Sekundenlevel. But the basic insight holds the need for solitude is not a personality flaw or a social deficit.
52:0452 Minuten, 4 SekundenIt's a reflection of how your particular nervous system is calibrated, how much stimulation it needs, and how quickly it
52:1152 Minuten, 11 Sekundenreaches its limit. And here's the thing, even this framing, introvert versus extrovert,
52:1952 Minuten, 19 Sekundenchosen versus unchosen, can make solitude sound like it's only about recovery, like it's the absence of
52:2752 Minuten, 27 Sekundensomething rather than the presence of something.
52:3152 Minuten, 31 SekundenAnd that misses what might be the most interesting part of the whole picture.
52:3752 Minuten, 37 SekundenBecause solitude doesn't just restore you. It enables certain kinds of psychological work that are very
52:4552 Minuten, 45 Sekundendifficult, maybe impossible to do in the presence of others.
52:5052 Minuten, 50 SekundenEsther Bholtz, a psychoanalyst and psychologist who wrote extensively about solitude in the 1990s,
52:5852 Minuten, 58 Sekundenargued that the need for solitude is not secondary to the need for connection, but is a parallel equally fundamental drive.
53:0853 Minuten, 8 SekundenShe called it the drive for alone time, deliberately one word, to distinguish it from loneliness or isolation.
53:1753 Minuten, 17 SekundenIn her view, solitude is where certain kinds of self-nowledge become possible.
53:2353 Minuten, 23 SekundenWhere creativity has room to operate, where the mind can do the slow, associative, nonlinear processing that
53:3253 Minuten, 32 Sekundengets crowded out by social demands. And she traced this need all the way back to infancy, noting that even very young
53:4153 Minuten, 41 Sekundenbabies cycle between periods of social engagement and periods of gaze aversion.
53:4753 Minuten, 47 Sekundenturning away, seeking a kind of mini solitude even in the midst of interaction.
53:5453 Minuten, 54 SekundenThis idea that solitude has its own productive function rather than being merely the absence of company connects to some fascinating work on creativity.
54:0454 Minuten, 4 SekundenAnd the research here goes back further than you might expect to a question that psychologists have been asking since at least the 1950s, which is whether people are more creative alone or in groups.
54:1854 Minuten, 18 SekundenThe answer which has been replicated so consistently that it's one of the more reliable findings in organizational
54:2654 Minuten, 26 Sekundenpsychology is that people generate more ideas and more original ideas when they work alone than when they brainstorm in
54:3554 Minuten, 35 Sekundengroups. This was first demonstrated by Donald Taylor and his colleagues at Yale back in 1958
54:4254 Minuten, 42 Sekundenand it's been confirmed dozens of times since.
54:4654 Minuten, 46 SekundenGroups feel more creative. Participants consistently report that the group session was more productive. But when
54:5354 Minuten, 53 Sekundenyou actually count and rate the ideas, solitary work wins every time.
55:0155 Minuten, 1 SekundeAnd the reasons for this tell you something important about what solitude provides.
55:0755 Minuten, 7 SekundenIn a group, you're monitoring other people's reactions. You're anchoring on ideas that have already been said.
55:1455 Minuten, 14 SekundenYou're managing your social standing.
55:1655 Minuten, 16 SekundenYou're unconsciously filtering for ideas that will be wellreceived rather than ideas that are genuinely novel. Alone,
55:2555 Minuten, 25 Sekundenall of that falls away. The mind can wander more freely, make stranger connections, follow threads that would
55:3355 Minuten, 33 Sekundenbe embarrassing or half-formed in front of an audience. Solitude in this sense is not just rest.
55:4255 Minuten, 42 SekundenIt's a different mode of cognition.
55:4555 Minuten, 45 SekundenA mode where the default network of the brain, that constellation of regions that activates when you're not focused
55:5155 Minuten, 51 Sekundenon an external task, can do its quiet, wandering, associative work without interruption. And this default network,
56:0156 Minuten, 1 Sekundesometimes called the default mode network, is worth pausing on because it turns out to be deeply
56:0856 Minuten, 8 Sekundenrelevant to what happens in your mind when you're alone.
56:1256 Minuten, 12 SekundenIt was discovered almost by accident by Marcus Rachel and his colleagues at Washington University in the late 1990s.
56:2156 Minuten, 21 SekundenThey noticed that certain brain regions were consistently more active when people were resting between tasks than
56:2856 Minuten, 28 Sekundenwhen they were performing the tasks themselves.
56:3256 Minuten, 32 SekundenWhich was strange because the assumption had always been that the resting brain was just idling, doing nothing in
56:3956 Minuten, 39 Sekundenparticular. But Rachel's data showed the opposite. The resting brain was busy. It
56:4656 Minuten, 46 Sekundenwas running its own agenda. And that agenda involved a set of processes that look from the outside a lot like the
56:5456 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthings you do when you're alone with your own thoughts. Self-reerential thinking, mental time travel,
57:0257 Minuten, 2 Sekundenimagining other people's perspectives, replaying the past, rehearsing possible futures.
57:1057 Minuten, 10 SekundenThe default mode network, it turned out, is not the brain doing nothing. It's the brain doing something it considers so
57:1857 Minuten, 18 Sekundenimportant that it fires up the moment you stop giving it a task.
57:2357 Minuten, 23 SekundenThink about what that means for a second.
57:2657 Minuten, 26 SekundenYour brain has a system, a whole coordinated network of regions spanning the medial prefrontal cortex, the
57:3457 Minuten, 34 Sekundenposterior singulate, the angular gyrus, the hippocample formation that springs to life the moment external demands
57:4257 Minuten, 42 Sekundenquiet down. It's as if the mind has a project it's been waiting to get back to. And the instant the meeting ends, it
57:5057 Minuten, 50 Sekundenopens the file again. And the project, broadly speaking, is you, your story,
57:5757 Minuten, 57 Sekundenyour relationships, your plans, the simulation of what might happen next, and the editing of what already
58:0558 Minuten, 5 Sekundenhappened. Rachel initially called it a default mode because it seemed to be what the brain defaulted to, but that
58:1358 Minuten, 13 Sekundenword underells it. Default sounds passive, like a screen saver.
58:1958 Minuten, 19 SekundenWhat the network actually does is anything but passive. Randy Buckner working at Harvard in the mid 2000s
58:2758 Minuten, 27 Sekundenhelped map the default mode network's functions more precisely and found that it's deeply involved in autobiographical
58:3558 Minuten, 35 Sekundenmemory retrieval. introspection, which is the technical term for imagining future scenarios and in what
58:4258 Minuten, 42 Sekundenpsychologists call theory of mind, the ability to model what another person might be thinking or feeling. So, here's
58:5058 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthis network that is most active when you're sitting quietly, staring out a window, lying in bed, walking without a destination.
59:0059 MinutenAnd what it's doing is weaving together your sense of self across time, running social simulations,
59:0759 Minuten, 7 Sekundenand quietly planning. It's doing the narrative work of being a person. And it does this work best, or at least most
59:1659 Minuten, 16 Sekundenfreely, when nobody is asking anything of you. This is where solitude and the default mode network become deeply intertwined.
59:2659 Minuten, 26 SekundenWhen you're in conversation, when you're performing a task, when you're navigating a social environment, the
59:3259 Minuten, 32 Sekundenbrain's task positive network, sometimes called the executive control network, tends to suppress default mode activity.
59:4159 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe two networks are roughly anti-correlated, meaning when one is highly active, the other tends to quiet down. So the
59:5059 Minuten, 50 Sekundenrichest most unconstrained default mode processing happens precisely in those moments of solitude and rest. The mind
1:00:001 Stundefreed from external demands turns inward and starts doing its integrative associative autobiographical work. And
1:00:091 Stunde, 9 Sekundenyou've felt this. You know what it's like to be in the shower and suddenly understand something about a conversation you had 3 days ago. Or to
1:00:181 Stunde, 18 Sekundenbe lying in bed and have a solution to a problem you weren't consciously working on just arrive fully formed as if
1:00:271 Stunde, 27 Sekundensomeone else solved it and handed you the answer.
1:00:301 Stunde, 30 SekundenThat's the default mode network. That's what it does when you give it room. It connects things that your focused
1:00:391 Stunde, 39 Sekundentaskoriented mind would never put together because focused attention is narrow by design. It has to be. You
1:00:491 Stunde, 49 Sekundencan't solve a math problem while simultaneously reconsidering your relationship with your father.
1:00:551 Stunde, 55 SekundenBut the default network can hold both of those loosely at the same time and find the thread between them if there is one.
1:01:041 Stunde, 1 Minute, 4 SekundenNow, this doesn't mean that all solitude is automatically rich default mode processing. There's an important caveat
1:01:131 Stunde, 1 Minute, 13 Sekundenhere, and it has to do with what you fill your alone time with. A study led by Timothy Wilson at the University of
1:01:201 Stunde, 1 Minute, 20 SekundenVirginia, published in 2014, became briefly famous for a striking finding.
1:01:271 Stunde, 1 Minute, 27 SekundenParticipants were left alone in a room for 6 to 15 minutes with nothing to do but think. No phone, no book, no task,
1:01:361 Stunde, 1 Minute, 36 Sekundenjust their own minds. And a significant number of them, about twothirds, found the experience unpleasant.
1:01:451 Stunde, 1 Minute, 45 SekundenMore remarkably, when given the option to administer a mild electric shock to themselves, about a quarter of the women
1:01:521 Stunde, 1 Minute, 52 Sekundenand 2/3 of the men chose to shock themselves rather than sit quietly with their thoughts.
1:02:001 Stunde, 2 MinutenThe study made headlines because it seemed to confirm something slightly alarming about modern minds that we'd
1:02:071 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 7 Sekundenrather hurt ourselves than be alone with our own thinking. But here's the thing, that study is more nuanced than the
1:02:151 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 15 Sekundenheadlines suggested. And it actually tells you something important about the conditions under which solitude becomes restorative versus aversive.
1:02:241 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe participants weren't practiced at undirected thought. They were placed in a bare lab room, which is quite different from choosing to sit in your
1:02:331 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 33 Sekundengarden. They had no context for the experience, no framework for what they were supposed to be doing, no sense that
1:02:411 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 41 Sekundenthis was something valuable. And the shock option was novel, a button right there in front of them, and novelty
1:02:491 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 49 Sekundenseeking is a powerful motivator, especially when you're bored.
1:02:541 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 54 SekundenWilson himself was careful to note that the study didn't mean people can't enjoy solitude. It meant that undirected
1:03:021 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 2 Sekundenthinking is a skill and that modern life saturated with stimulation gives people very little practice at it.
1:03:121 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 12 SekundenThis connects to something that contemplative traditions have known for centuries and that psychology is only recently beginning to measure.
1:03:221 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe ability to sit with your own mind without immediately reaching for distraction is something that can be cultivated. It's not a fixed trait.
1:03:311 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 31 SekundenMeditation research, particularly work on mindfulness-based practices, has shown that training in sustained
1:03:391 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 39 Sekundennon-reactive attention changes the relationship between the default mode network and the brain's salance network,
1:03:471 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 47 Sekundenthe system that decides what deserves your attention.
1:03:521 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 52 SekundenExperienced meditators, Judson Brewer's lab at Yale showed, have default mode networks that are active but less noisy,
1:04:011 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 1 Sekundeless prone to the ruminative, self-critical loops that make undirected thought unpleasant for many people.
1:04:081 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 8 SekundenTheir minds still wander, but the wandering is less sticky. It doesn't snag on worry the way it does for
1:04:161 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 16 Sekundensomeone whose only experience of being alone with their thoughts is lying awake at 3 in the morning. And this is a crucial distinction.
1:04:251 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 25 SekundenThe default mode network is not inherently positive or negative. It's a tool. And like any tool, what it
1:04:331 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 33 Sekundenproduces depends on the conditions. For someone caught in the hypervigilant threat scanning mode that Katyopo
1:04:401 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 40 Sekundendescribed, the loneliness loop default mode activity during solitude might look like rumination,
1:04:481 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 48 Sekundenreplaying social failures, imagining rejection, scanning memories for evidence that you're unwanted.
1:04:561 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 56 SekundenThe network is doing the same thing it always does. autobiographical processing, social simulation,
1:05:051 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 5 Sekundenprospection. But the emotional context colors everything it touches. The same
1:05:121 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 12 Sekundenmachinery that in a secure and rested mind produces creative insight and peaceful
1:05:201 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 20 Sekundenself-reflection can in an anxious and lonely mind produce a grinding spiral of self-focused worry.
1:05:291 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 29 SekundenSo the question of whether being alone is good for you is really a question about what your default mode network does when it's given the room to run.
1:05:401 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 40 SekundenAnd that depends on attachment security which we talked about earlier with Winnott and Mikulinser.
1:05:481 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 48 SekundenIt depends on whether the solitude is chosen or imposed.
1:05:521 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 52 SekundenIt depends on practice, on whether you've built the capacity to let your mind wander without immediately trying to control or suppress what comes up.
1:06:041 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 4 SekundenAnd it depends honestly on how much unresolved distress you're carrying because the default network will process whatever is most emotionally salient.
1:06:151 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 15 SekundenAnd if what's most salient is pain, that's where it will go.
1:06:201 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 20 SekundenThis brings us to something that researchers have started calling the solitude paradox. Though the term is
1:06:271 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 27 Sekundenused loosely across different papers rather than being one person's coinage.
1:06:331 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe paradox is this. The people who would benefit most from solitude. Those who are socially exhausted,
1:06:411 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 41 Sekundenoverstimulated, running on empty are often the same people who find solitude most difficult because their default
1:06:501 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 50 Sekundenmode processing tends toward rumination rather than restoration.
1:06:551 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 55 SekundenAnd the people who find solitude easiest and most nourishing, those with secure attachment, low neuroticism, and a
1:07:041 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 4 Sekundenpracticed relationship with their own inner life, are often the people who are also perfectly comfortable in social settings.
1:07:141 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 14 SekundenThey don't need solitude as an escape.
1:07:171 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 17 SekundenThey choose it as an enrichment. The skill of being alone well in other words
1:07:241 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 24 Sekundenis built on a foundation of being connected well which is almost exactly what Winnott said in 1958
1:07:321 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 32 Sekundenjust in different language. There's a developmental angle to this that's worth following for a moment because it
1:07:401 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 40 Sekundenchanges how you think about adolescence and the enormous amount of time teenagers now spend alone in their rooms
1:07:471 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 47 Sekundenand whether that's something to worry about.
1:07:511 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 51 SekundenReed Larson, a developmental psychologist at the University of Illinois, did some of the most careful
1:07:571 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 57 Sekundenwork on adolescent solitude in the 1990s using experience sampling, where you buzz people at random intervals
1:08:051 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 5 Sekundenthroughout the day and ask them what they're doing and how they feel.
1:08:101 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 10 SekundenWhat Larsson found was that adolescents who spent a moderate amount of time alone, not too much, not too little,
1:08:181 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 18 Sekundenshowed better emotional adjustment than those at either extreme.
1:08:221 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 22 SekundenAnd critically, the solitude needed to be voluntary.
1:08:281 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 28 SekundenTeenagers who were alone because they chose to be reported using that time for identity work, for processing emotions,
1:08:371 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 37 Sekundenfor the kind of self-reflection that is genuinely necessary during a period when you're trying to figure out who you are apart from your family.
1:08:471 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 47 SekundenTeenagers who were alone because they were excluded or isolated showed the opposite pattern. more depression, more anxiety, worse adjustment.
1:08:591 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 59 SekundenLarsson's work reinforced the same principle that keeps surfacing. Solitude is not one thing. It's a context, and
1:09:091 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 9 Sekundenwhat happens inside it depends almost entirely on whether the person chose it, whether they feel they could return to
1:09:161 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 16 Sekundenconnection if they wanted to, and what their mind does with the quiet. The room
1:09:221 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 22 Sekundenis the same, the silence is the same, but the experience is completely
1:09:301 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 30 Sekundendifferent depending on what you bring into it. And this is where the conversation about solitude starts to
1:09:371 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 37 Sekundenintersect with something broader and harder to measure, something that philosophers have spent more time with than psychologists have.
1:09:471 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 47 Sekundenthe question of what it means to have a relationship with yourself, whether that's even a coherent idea or
1:09:551 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 55 Sekundenwhether it's a metaphor that sounds deep but doesn't actually point to anything real.
1:10:011 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 1 SekundeBecause when we say someone is comfortable being alone, what we're really saying is that their internal monologue, their default mode
1:10:101 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 10 Sekundenprocessing, their felt sense of who they are is stable enough and warm enough to be good company. And that raises the
1:10:201 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 20 Sekundenquestion of who exactly is keeping whom company in there. The self that observes
1:10:271 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 27 Sekundenand the self that is observed, the narrator and the character, the mind watching itself think.
1:10:371 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 37 SekundenThis is territory that William James was already exploring in the 1890s when he drew his famous distinction between the
1:10:451 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 45 SekundenI and the me, the self is knower and the self is known.
1:10:501 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 50 SekundenAnd it's territory that modern neuroscience is circling back to because the default mode network, the very
1:10:591 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 59 Sekundensystem that activates in solitude is also the system most consistently associated with self-reerential processing.
1:11:081 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 8 SekundenThe network that builds your sense of being a continuous person across time.
1:11:141 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 14 SekundenWhich means that solitude, real solitude, undistracted and unhurried, might be the condition under which the
1:11:221 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 22 Sekundenself does its most fundamental maintenance work. Not just resting, not just recovering from social effort, but
1:11:311 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 31 Sekundenactively constructing and revising the story of who you are. And if that's true, then the modern epidemic of never
1:11:391 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 39 Sekundenbeing truly alone, of filling every quiet moment with a podcast or a scroll or a notification,
1:11:471 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 47 Sekundenisn't just a matter of missing out on rest. It's a matter of interrupting a process that the brain clearly considers
1:11:551 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 55 Sekundenessential, given how eagerly it fires up the moment you stop giving it something else to do. The default network doesn't
1:12:031 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 3 Sekundenwait politely. It activates within seconds of task disengagement.
1:12:091 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 9 SekundenIt wants to do this work. And the question that a growing number of researchers are beginning to ask, though the data is still early and the causal
1:12:181 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 18 Sekundenarrows are hard to draw, is what happens to a generation that almost never lets it run freely? That's the question
1:12:271 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 27 Sekundenhovering at the edge of all this research. And it's worth sitting with for a moment because the honest answer is that nobody knows yet. Not really.
1:12:381 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 38 SekundenThe studies that would tell us, the ones tracking default mode network development in children and adolescence who grew up with smartphones from age 8 or 9 versus those who didn't.
1:12:491 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 49 SekundenThose studies are still being run or haven't been designed yet or are producing results too preliminary to
1:12:561 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 56 Sekundenbuild a confident story on. What we have instead are fragments, suggestive patterns, the kind of early signal that
1:13:051 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 5 Sekundenmakes researchers nervous enough to keep looking but not confident enough to make declarations.
1:13:121 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 12 SekundenHere's what we can say. There's a developmental window roughly from early adolescence through the mid20s
1:13:201 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 20 Sekundenduring which the default mode network is still maturing.
1:13:251 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 25 SekundenIts connectivity patterns are still being refined.
1:13:281 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthe medial prefrontal cortex, the posterior singulate, the angular gyrus.
1:13:341 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 34 SekundenThese regions are still strengthening the links between them, still learning to work as a coordinated system.
1:13:421 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd this happens to be the same window during which identity formation is doing its heaviest lifting. Eric Ericson
1:13:501 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 50 Sekundenmapped this decades before anyone had heard of the default mode network, but the overlap is hard to ignore.
1:13:591 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 59 SekundenThe period when the brain's self-referential system is most actively under construction is also the period
1:14:061 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwhen the psychological task of figuring out who you are is most urgent. and Reed
1:14:121 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 12 SekundenLarson's experience sampling work. The studies we touched on earlier where adolescence carried beepers and reported
1:14:191 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 19 Sekundenwhat they were doing and how they felt showed that the teenagers who spent moderate amounts of time alone
1:14:261 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 26 Sekundenvoluntarily by choice were the ones who showed the best emotional adjustment over time. Not the most alone, not the
1:14:351 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 35 Sekundenleast alone, but the ones who had regular access to solitude and weren't afraid of it.
1:14:421 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 42 SekundenNow layer onto that the reality of how most adolescents today actually spend their nominally alone time. The bedroom
1:14:501 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 50 Sekundendoor is closed. The room is quiet. But the phone is active. The group chat is running. The feed is refreshing. The
1:15:001 Stunde, 15 Minutenbrain is receiving a more or less continuous stream of lowgrade social information. Not demanding enough to
1:15:071 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 7 Sekundenfeel like socializing, but present enough to keep the task positive network engaged, or at least to keep the default
1:15:141 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 14 Sekundenmode network from settling into its deeper, more self-reerential patterns.
1:15:201 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 20 SekundenAnd the question is whether that partial engagement, that never quite alone state is functionally equivalent to genuine
1:15:291 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 29 Sekundensolitude for the purposes of whatever the default mode network is trying to do during those years. The honest answer again is we don't know.
1:15:411 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 41 SekundenBut there are reasons to suspect it isn't.
1:15:451 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 45 SekundenOne of those reasons comes from a line of research that isn't about screens at all. It's about mind wandering, which is
1:15:521 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 52 Sekundenone of the default mode networks signature outputs.
1:15:561 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 56 SekundenWhen the network is running freely, the mind wanders. It drifts from thought to thought without external direction. And
1:16:041 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 4 Sekundenwhile that can feel aimless, there's growing evidence that it serves several important functions. Jonathan Schooler,
1:16:131 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 13 Sekundena psychologist at the University of California, Santa Barbara, has spent years studying mind wandering and has
1:16:211 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 21 Sekundenargued that it plays a role in creative problem solving, future planning, and what he calls the incubation of insight.
1:16:331 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe idea is that when you stop actively working on a problem and let the mind drift, the default mode network
1:16:401 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 40 Sekundencontinues processing the problem in the background, making associative connections that the more focused task
1:16:481 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 48 Sekundenpositive mode would never stumble across.
1:16:531 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 53 SekundenAnd there's experimental support for this.
1:16:561 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 56 SekundenIn studies where people are given a creative task, then given a break that involves either a demanding task, an
1:17:041 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 4 Sekundeneasy task, or nothing at all. The group that does the easy task, the one most likely to allow mind wandering, tends to
1:17:131 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 13 Sekundenshow the biggest improvement when they return to the creative problem. The mind wasn't idle during that break. It was
1:17:211 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 21 Sekundenworking, just not in a way that feels like working. But here's the thing. Mind wandering requires a certain tolerance
1:17:301 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 30 Sekundenfor unstructured experience. It requires being willing to sit in a moment that has no particular content, no particular direction, no particular reward.
1:17:431 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 43 SekundenAnd that tolerance, like most psychological capacities, appears to be something that develops
1:17:501 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthrough practice or fails to develop through lack of it. If every moment of
1:17:561 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 56 Sekundenpotential boredom is immediately filled with a swipe, a tap, a refresh,
1:18:031 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 3 Sekundenthen the muscle that allows you to sit with an undirected mind, which is really the muscle that allows the default mode
1:18:101 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 10 Sekundennetwork to do its deeper work never gets trained. Timothy Wilson's electric shock study comes back here. Those
1:18:201 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 20 Sekundenparticipants who chose to shock themselves rather than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes weren't broken. They were unpracticed.
1:18:301 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 30 SekundenThey had never learned to be comfortable in the space where the default mode network does its thing. And so that
1:18:371 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 37 Sekundenspace felt aversive rather than generative.
1:18:411 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 41 SekundenAnd this connects to something that the clinical literature has been noticing for a while now, which is that the inability to be alone, the deep
1:18:501 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 50 Sekundendiscomfort with unstructured solitude, shows up as a feature of several psychological difficulties, not as a cause necessarily, but as a marker.
1:19:021 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 2 SekundenPeople with anxious attachment styles, as we talked about earlier, tend to find solitude threatening because it
1:19:101 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 10 Sekundenactivates their attachment systems alarm without providing any reassurance.
1:19:161 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 16 SekundenPeople with certain personality disorders, borderline personality disorder being the most studied example,
1:19:231 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 23 Sekundenoften describe an intolerable emptiness when alone, a feeling that the self begins to dissolve without the mirror of
1:19:311 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 31 Sekundenanother person. And while these are clinical populations, the underlying dynamic, the idea that some people's
1:19:401 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 40 Sekundensense of self is not stable enough to survive the absence of external input, maps onto a broader spectrum that all of
1:19:491 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 49 Sekundenus sit somewhere on. You've probably felt a milder version of it. The restlessness that comes when you're alone and unstimulated.
1:20:001 Stunde, 20 MinutenThe urge to check your phone not because you're expecting anything, but because the silence feels like it needs filling.
1:20:081 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 8 SekundenThat urge is the felt experience of a self that isn't quite sure it can sustain itself without input.
1:20:171 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 17 SekundenWhich brings us back to Winnott because this is essentially what he was describing in 1958 when he wrote about
1:20:241 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 24 Sekundenthe capacity to be alone. He argued that this capacity is one of the most important signs of emotional maturity
1:20:331 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 33 Sekundenand that it develops in childhood through the experience of being alone in the presence of the mother.
1:20:401 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 40 SekundenThe child plays by itself while the caregiver is nearby but not actively engaging.
1:20:481 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 48 SekundenThe child learns that it can generate its own experience, sustain its own interest, maintain its own sense of self
1:20:561 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 56 Sekundenwithout the caregivers's active participation.
1:21:001 Stunde, 21 MinutenAnd from that experience gradually comes the ability to be alone without the caregiver present at all.
1:21:081 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 8 SekundenThe internal sense of security becomes portable. You carry it with you into empty rooms, into quiet evenings, into
1:21:171 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 17 Sekundenthe long stretches of life where nobody is watching.
1:21:211 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 21 SekundenNow, Winnott was writing in the language of object relations theory, which is a particular psychoanalytic framework, and
1:21:291 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 29 Sekundennot everything in that framework has held up equally well under empirical scrutiny. But this particular idea that
1:21:371 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 37 Sekundenthe capacity for solitude is rooted in early relational experience and develops as a kind of internalized security has
1:21:461 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 46 Sekundenfound surprising support in the attachment literature. Mickey Linser and Shaver's work which we looked at earlier
1:21:541 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 54 Sekundenessentially confirmed the core of Winnott's claim using modern methods.
1:22:001 Stunde, 22 MinutenSecurely attached, adults are more comfortable alone, find solitude more restorative, and show less of the
1:22:061 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 6 Sekundenhypervigilant social monitoring that characterizes loneliness.
1:22:111 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 11 SekundenThey can let the default mode network run without it spiraling into threat detection or rumination because the baseline emotional tone of their
1:22:201 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 20 Sekundenself-referential processing is warm enough to be tolerable. good enough as Winnott might have said.
1:22:291 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 29 SekundenSo there's a through line here that runs from early attachment through the development of a stable internal world
1:22:361 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthrough the capacity to tolerate and even enjoy unstructured solitude through the default mode network's ability to do
1:22:441 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 44 Sekundenits maintenance work on the self. all the way to the question of what kind of mind you end up with if that whole chain is supported versus if it's interrupted.
1:22:571 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 57 SekundenAnd it's a throughine that suggests solitude isn't just a preference or a lifestyle choice. It's a developmental
1:23:051 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 5 Sekundenachievement, something the mind has to learn how to do and something it needs ongoing practice to maintain.
1:23:131 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 13 SekundenThis is why the conversation about being alone can't be reduced to a simple prescription.
1:23:201 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 20 SekundenYou can't just tell people to spend more time alone and expect good things to happen. For someone with a secure
1:23:261 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 26 Sekundeninternal world, a rich default mode network, and a history of chosen solitude, an afternoon alone might be genuinely restorative.
1:23:371 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 37 SekundenFor someone with an insecure attachment style, a harsh inner critic, and a default mode network that tends toward
1:23:451 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 45 Sekundenrumination, that same afternoon might be genuinely painful. Same room, same silence, completely different
1:23:531 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 53 Sekundenexperience. The variable isn't the aloneeness, it's what the mind does with it. And that's something that can
1:24:011 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 1 Sekundechange. That's one of the more hopeful findings in all of this. Judson Brewer's meditation work showed that the default
1:24:101 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 10 Sekundenmode networks activity patterns can shift with training, becoming less caught in ruminative loops and more open to present moment awareness.
1:24:191 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 19 SekundenCognitive behavioral approaches, the same ones that Masi and Kaciopo found most effective for loneliness, work
1:24:271 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 27 Sekundenpartly by changing the interpretive habits that make solitude feel threatening.
1:24:321 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 32 SekundenEven simple exposure, gradually spending time alone in low stakes settings, building up tolerance for unstructured
1:24:401 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 40 Sekundenexperience seems to help. The muscle metaphor isn't perfect, but it captures
1:24:471 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 47 Sekundensomething real. The capacity to be alone is not fixed at birth or sealed in childhood.
1:24:541 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 54 SekundenIt's something you can build slowly the way you build any skill by doing it badly at first and then doing it less
1:25:031 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 3 Sekundenbadly and eventually discovering that the thing you were afraid of is actually a room you can furnish. There's a
1:25:111 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 11 Sekundenphilosopher who captured this rather beautifully though he was writing long before any of this research existed.
1:25:191 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 19 SekundenMichelle de Montana in the 1580s wrote an essay called on solitude in which he
1:25:261 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 26 Sekundenargued that the point of withdrawing from the world is not to escape other people but to recover yourself. He wrote
1:25:341 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 34 Sekundenabout the need to have what he called a backshop an area boutique a room behind the room where the public self operates.
1:25:441 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 44 Sekundena private space not physical but psychological where you can be with your own thoughts without performing for anyone. And he
1:25:541 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 54 Sekundenwarned that most people who seek solitude fail at it because they bring their anxieties and their ambitions and
1:26:021 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 2 Sekundentheir need for approval with them into the empty room. They are alone but not with themselves.
1:26:101 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 10 SekundenThe body is solitary, but the mind is still in the crowd. That's a psychological observation dressed in
1:26:171 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 17 SekundenRenaissance pros, and it holds up remarkably well against what the neuroscience is now showing. The default
1:26:251 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 25 Sekundenmode network can be running. You can be physically alone. And yet, the quality
1:26:311 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 31 Sekundenof that solitude depends entirely on what the network is doing. Is it constructing and revising your sense of
1:26:401 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 40 Sekundenself, integrating memories, imagining futures, doing the quiet work of being a
1:26:461 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 46 Sekundencontinuous person? Or is it rehearsing social threats, replaying embarrassments, scanning for dangers
1:26:541 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthat aren't there? Same network, same solitude, different weather inside. And
1:27:011 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 1 Sekundethis is where the story of being alone starts to shade into something larger.
1:27:071 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 7 SekundenSomething about the relationship between the self and its own company.
1:27:131 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 13 SekundenBecause there's a paradox buried in all of this that we haven't quite named yet.
1:27:181 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 18 SekundenThe mind that suffers most in solitude is the mind that most needs what solitude provides. The person whose
1:27:261 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 26 Sekundendefault mode network spirals into rumination when left alone is the person who most needs to develop a different
1:27:351 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 35 Sekundenrelationship with their own inner monologue.
1:27:391 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 39 SekundenAnd the person who fills every quiet moment with distraction is the person whose self-referential processing most
1:27:481 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 48 Sekundenneeds the space to run. the avoidance and the need point in opposite directions and bridging that gap.
1:27:561 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 56 SekundenLearning to sit with yourself when sitting with yourself is hard might be one of the quieter but more
1:28:021 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 2 Sekundenconsequential psychological skills a person can develop.
1:28:071 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 7 SekundenWhich raises a question that psychology has only recently started taking seriously. One that contemplative traditions have been asking for a very
1:28:161 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 16 Sekundenlong time. What does it actually mean to be comfortable in your own mind?
1:28:221 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 22 SekundenNot happy, not entertained, not distracted, but genuinely at ease with the experience of being yourself.
1:28:331 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 33 SekundenJust yourself with nothing else playing, nothing else pulling at your attention, just the hum of your own consciousness
1:28:411 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 41 Sekundendoing what it does when nobody is asking it to do anything in particular.
1:28:471 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 47 SekundenIt's a question that sounds almost too simple to be worth asking.
1:28:511 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 51 SekundenBut the more you look at it, the more you realize how rarely most people experience it and how much of the psychological literature has been
1:29:001 Stunde, 29 Minutenquietly circling it without ever landing on it directly. Because psychology for most of its modern history has been a
1:29:091 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 9 Sekundenscience of problems. It studies what goes wrong. It measures distress, dysfunction, disorder. It has
1:29:171 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 17 Sekundenextraordinarily detailed models of what happens when someone cannot be alone, when solitude curdles into loneliness, when the inner monologue turns hostile.
1:29:281 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 28 SekundenBut the positive version of that experience, the state of being genuinely, quietly comfortable inside your own head, has been much harder to
1:29:371 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 37 Sekundenpin down. Partly because it's hard to measure. Partly because the people who experience it most reliably tend not to
1:29:461 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 46 Sekundenshow up in clinical settings and partly because until fairly recently the field
1:29:531 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 53 Sekundendidn't have a good framework for thinking about it. The contemplative traditions did though and this is one of
1:30:011 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 1 Sekundethose places where thousands of years of practice got somewhere that controlled experiments are only now catching up to.
1:30:081 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 8 SekundenBuddhism, for instance, has a concept that doesn't translate neatly into English, but is sometimes rendered as equinimity.
1:30:171 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 17 SekundenIt's not happiness, not calm exactly, not the absence of feeling. It's more like a steady, non-reactive awareness of
1:30:251 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 25 Sekundenwhatever is happening in the mind, pleasant or unpleasant, without the impulse to chase the pleasant or push away the unpleasant.
1:30:341 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe poly word is upeka and it shows up in some of the oldest meditation manuals as one of the highest states a
1:30:421 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 42 Sekundenpractitioner can develop. Not bliss, not transcendence, but something more like being at home in your own experience
1:30:501 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 50 Sekundenregardless of what that experience contains.
1:30:541 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 54 SekundenThe Stoics had a version of it too, what they called apithea, which doesn't mean apathy in the modern sense, but
1:31:011 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 1 Sekundesomething closer to freedom from being jerked around by your own reactions. and the Christian contemplative tradition,
1:31:081 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 8 Sekundenthe desert fathers and mothers who went out into the Egyptian wilderness in the 3rd and fourth centuries developed an
1:31:151 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 15 Sekundenentire psychology of solitude, including a remarkably detailed taxonomy of the mental states that arise when you're
1:31:231 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 23 Sekundenalone for long stretches. They had a word, aidia, for the particular restless, listless, can't settle feeling
1:31:311 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 31 Sekundenthat hits around midday when you've been alone in a cell with nothing but your own thoughts. They called it the noonday
1:31:391 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 39 Sekundendemon. It's not depression exactly, and it's not boredom exactly. It's the mind
1:31:451 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 45 Sekundenrebelling against its own company. Now, you might be thinking that this is interesting as history, but not really science.
1:31:551 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 55 SekundenAnd that's fair.
1:31:571 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 57 SekundenBut here's the thing. Starting in the late 1990s and accelerating through the 2000s, a handful of researchers began
1:32:051 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 5 Sekundendoing something that would have seemed odd a generation earlier. They started putting experienced meditators into brain scanners.
1:32:141 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 14 SekundenRichard Davidson at the University of Wisconsin was one of the first to do this systematically.
1:32:201 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 20 SekundenHe'd been interested in the neuroscience of emotion for years. And in the early 2000s, he began working with Mati Ricar,
1:32:281 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 28 Sekundena French-born Tibetan Buddhist monk who had spent tens of thousands of hours in meditation practice. What Davidson's
1:32:361 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 36 Sekundenteam found was striking. During compassion meditation, Ricard's brain showed gamma wave activity that was off
1:32:431 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 43 Sekundenthe charts compared to novice meditators, particularly in areas associated with attention, interception, and emotional regulation.
1:32:531 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe prefrontal cortex was highly active, but in a pattern that looked nothing like the anxious, ruminative prefrontal activity you'd see in someone worrying.
1:33:041 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 4 SekundenIt was organized, coherent, almost musical in its synchronization.
1:33:101 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 10 SekundenAnd here's what matters for our story about being alone.
1:33:141 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 14 SekundenWhen Ricard and other long-term practitioners were scanned at rest, just sitting quietly with no specific
1:33:211 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 21 Sekundenmeditation instruction, their default mode networks looked different, too. Not
1:33:281 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 28 Sekundenshut down, not suppressed, but quieter in the regions associated with self-referential rumination and more
1:33:361 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 36 Sekundenactive in regions associated with present moment awareness and intraception. the sense of the body from the inside.
1:33:451 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 45 SekundenAntoine Lutz working with Davidson published some of the key papers on this in the mid 2000s and the findings have
1:33:531 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 53 Sekundenbeen broadly supported since though with the usual caveats.
1:33:581 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 58 SekundenThese are small samples. The practitioners are unusual people. You can't easily separate the effects of
1:34:061 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 6 Sekundenmeditation from the effects of being the kind of person who devotes their life to meditation.
1:34:141 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 14 SekundenBut the pattern has held up across multiple labs and multiple contemplative traditions.
1:34:211 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 21 SekundenSomething about sustained practice in sitting with your own mind changes the way the default mode network behaves
1:34:301 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 30 Sekundenwhen you're alone. And this connects back to something we touched on earlier, Judson Brewer's work at Yale.
1:34:381 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 38 SekundenBrewer, the psychiatrist who studied meditators and found that experienced practitioners showed reduced activity in
1:34:461 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 46 Sekundenthe posterior singulate cortex, one of the major hubs of the default mode network.
1:34:541 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 54 SekundenThat's the region most associated with mind wandering and self-reerential thought. The MI center, the part that's always narrating your life back to you.
1:35:061 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 6 SekundenIn experienced meditators, it was still active, but it was more tightly coupled with regions involved in cognitive control.
1:35:161 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe interpretation, and Brewer has been careful about this, is not that meditation silences the default mode
1:35:231 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 23 Sekundennetwork. It's that it changes the relationship between the network and the rest of the brain. The inner monologue
1:35:311 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 31 Sekundenstill runs, but it runs with less stickiness, less of that quality where a thought grabs you and pulls you down a
1:35:401 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 40 Sekundencorridor of worry before you even notice you've left the room.
1:35:451 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 45 SekundenThink about what that means for the experience of being alone.
1:35:491 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 49 SekundenIf the default mode network is the engine of solitude, the thing that fills the silence when external input drops
1:35:571 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 57 Sekundenaway, then the quality of your solitude depends enormously on how that engine runs. For someone whose default mode
1:36:061 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 6 Sekundentends toward rumination, toward replaying social failures, rehearsing future catastrophes,
1:36:131 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 13 Sekundenpicking at old wounds, solitude feels like being locked in a room with an unfriendly narrator. For someone whose
1:36:221 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 22 Sekundendefault mode has been trained, or perhaps was temperamentally inclined to observe its own contents without getting
1:36:301 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 30 Sekundentangled in them, solitude feels like something else entirely.
1:36:351 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 35 SekundenNot empty, not quiet exactly, but spacious.
1:36:411 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe thoughts still come. They just don't stick as hard. And this is where the contemplative insight and the
1:36:491 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 49 Sekundenneuroscience converge on something genuinely useful.
1:36:531 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 53 SekundenBecause the skill that meditation traditions have been teaching for millennia, the ability to notice a thought without becoming it, to let the
1:37:021 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 2 Sekundeninner monologue run without believing every word of it, turns out to map surprisingly well onto what the brain
1:37:101 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 10 Sekundenimaging is showing. The default mode network isn't the enemy. Rumination isn't the default mode network's only
1:37:201 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 20 Sekundensetting. The network can run in a mode that's integrative, creative, restorative.
1:37:281 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 28 SekundenAll the things we talked about when we discussed the positive architecture of solitude. The question is whether you can let it do that or whether every
1:37:371 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 37 Sekundenmoment of quiet triggers the threat scanning socially anxious pattern that Kaciopo's work described so well.
1:37:471 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 47 SekundenWhich brings us to something practical and something that the clinical literature has started to take seriously.
1:37:541 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 54 SekundenMindfulness-based interventions which are essentially secularized stripped down versions of contemplative attention
1:38:021 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 2 Sekundentraining have shown consistent effects on exactly this dimension.
1:38:071 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 7 SekundenZindel Seagel working in Toronto in the late 1990s developed mindfulness-based
1:38:141 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 14 Sekundencognitive therapy or MBCT originally as a relapse prevention tool for depression.
1:38:221 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe core insight was simple but powerful.
1:38:261 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 26 SekundenPeople who've been depressed aren't just sad. They've developed a cognitive habit
1:38:331 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 33 Sekundenwhere a small dip in mood triggers a cascade of ruminative self-referential thought. Why do I always feel this way?
1:38:421 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 42 SekundenWhat's wrong with me? This will never change. And that cascade deepens the dip into a full episode. MBCT teaches people
1:38:511 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 51 Sekundento notice the cascade starting and to step back from it, to see the thoughts as thoughts rather than as facts about
1:38:591 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 59 Sekundenthemselves. And it works. Multiple randomized controlled trials have shown it reduces relapse rates in people with
1:39:071 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 7 Sekundenrecurrent depression by roughly 40 to 50% compared to usual care, which is a
1:39:151 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 15 Sekundensubstantial effect for a psychological intervention.
1:39:191 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 19 SekundenBut here's what's interesting for our purposes. The mechanism isn't about being with other people. It isn't social
1:39:271 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 27 Sekundensupport. It isn't distraction. It isn't restructuring the content of the thoughts. It's about changing the
1:39:351 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 35 Sekundenperson's relationship with their own inner experience. It's teaching them in a very concrete and practiced way to be
1:39:441 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmore comfortable alone with their own mind. To sit with a difficult thought without it becoming a crisis, to let the
1:39:521 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 52 Sekundendefault mode network do its thing without every activation becoming a spiral.
1:39:581 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 58 SekundenAnd you've almost certainly felt the difference between these two modes yourself.
1:40:041 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 4 SekundenEven without any formal training, there are evenings when you're alone and the silence feels like a gift. When your
1:40:131 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 13 Sekundenmind wanders in pleasant associative ways, when you find yourself remembering something funny, or imagining something
1:40:211 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 21 Sekundenyou'd like to build, or noticing the texture of the light in the room. And there are evenings when the same silence feels oppressive.
1:40:311 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 31 SekundenWhen every thought seems to lead back to something you're worried about.
1:40:361 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 36 SekundenWhen you reach for your phone, not because you want to see anything in particular, but because the alternative,
1:40:431 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 43 Sekundenjust sitting there with whatever your mind is serving up, feels intolerable.
1:40:491 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 49 SekundenSame person, same room, same default mode network, different weather inside.
1:40:551 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 55 SekundenAs we said, and the difference isn't random. It tracks with how tired you are, how socially nourished or depleted
1:41:031 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 3 Sekundenyou feel, how much unresolved stress is sitting in the queue.
1:41:081 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 8 SekundenBut it also tracks, and this is the part that matters, with practice, with how much experience you have noticing what
1:41:161 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 16 Sekundenyour mind is doing and choosing gently not to follow every thread it offers.
1:41:231 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 23 SekundenSo the picture that's emerging from the neuroscience, from the clinical work, from the contemplative traditions is
1:41:311 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 31 Sekundenthat being comfortable alone isn't a personality trait you either have or you don't. It's closer to a skill or maybe a
1:41:401 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 40 Sekundencapacity that exists on a spectrum and can be developed.
1:41:451 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 45 SekundenWinnott saw it as a developmental achievement rooted in early attachment.
1:41:511 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 51 SekundenThe attachment researchers confirmed that secure attachment makes it easier.
1:41:561 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 56 SekundenBut easier isn't only the clinical evidence suggests that even people who didn't start with that secure
1:42:041 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 4 Sekundenbase can learn to relate differently to their own inner experience.
1:42:091 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 9 SekundenIt takes work. It takes practice. It often takes help. But the capacity isn't fixed at birth or sealed in childhood.
1:42:191 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 19 SekundenAnd there's something quietly hopeful about that because it means the paradox we named earlier, the one where the people who
1:42:271 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 27 Sekundenmost need solitude are the ones who find it hardest, isn't a trap. It's a starting point. The discomfort isn't a
1:42:361 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 36 Sekundensign that you're broken or that solitude isn't for you. It's a sign that the skill hasn't been practiced yet, that
1:42:441 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 44 Sekundenthe default mode network is running in its threat scanning mode because that's the mode it knows best, and that a
1:42:521 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 52 Sekundendifferent mode is available. One where being alone with yourself feels less like confinement and more like company.
1:43:011 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 1 SekundeThe kind of company that doesn't ask anything of you, doesn't need you to perform or respond,
1:43:081 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 8 Sekundenjust the quiet company of a mind that has been yours all along and is when you let it settle surprisingly okay to sit
1:43:171 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 17 Sekundenwith. And that word company is worth pausing on because one of the stranger things about the psychology of being
1:43:251 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 25 Sekundenalone is that the language we reach for almost always frames it as an absence.
1:43:311 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 31 Sekundenalone, isolated, solitary, without. The words describe
1:43:371 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 37 Sekundenwhat's missing. the other people, the conversation, the warmth of a shared room, but the experience of comfortable
1:43:461 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 46 Sekundensolitude, the kind that Winnott described, the kind that the contemplative traditions have been cultivating for thousands of years, the
1:43:551 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 55 Sekundenkind that shows up in the brain as a calmer, less sticky default mode network. That experience isn't really
1:44:021 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 2 Sekundenabout absence at all. It's about presence.
1:44:071 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 7 SekundenBeing present to your own thinking, your own feeling, your own quiet sense of existing in a body, in a room on a
1:44:161 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 16 Sekundenparticular night. The philosopher William James back in the 1890s made a distinction that still holds up. He
1:44:241 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 24 Sekundentalked about the difference between the self as knower and the self as known.
1:44:301 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 30 Sekundenthe I that does the experiencing and the me that gets experienced.
1:44:361 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 36 SekundenWhen you're comfortable alone, both of those are in the room. You're not just a mind rattling around in silence. You're
1:44:441 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 44 Sekundena mind aware of itself and finding that awareness, if not always pleasant, at least bearable, at least interesting.
1:44:541 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd this is where the science and the contemplative traditions land in roughly the same place, which doesn't happen as
1:45:011 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 1 Sekundeoften as you'd think. The Buddhist concept of mindfulness, stripped of its spiritual framing and studied in the
1:45:091 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 9 Sekundenlab, turns out to describe something quite precise.
1:45:141 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 14 SekundenIt's the capacity to observe your own mental states without being consumed by them. To notice that you're anxious without becoming the anxiety.
1:45:251 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 25 SekundenTo notice that the silence feels heavy without deciding that the heaviness means something is wrong. Zindel
1:45:321 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 32 SekundenSeagel's work with mindfulness-based cognitive therapy which we touched on showed that this capacity can be trained
1:45:401 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 40 Sekundenand the training doesn't require a monastery or a decade of practice. It requires at its most basic level
1:45:481 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 48 Sekundenrepeated gentle exposure to your own inner weather which is when you think about it exactly what solitude provides.
1:45:571 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 57 SekundenSolitude is the practice room. It's where the training happens. Whether you call it meditation or just sitting quietly with yourself for a while.
1:46:081 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 8 SekundenNow, there's a modern wrinkle to all of this that's worth being honest about because we're living through a period in
1:46:151 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 15 Sekundenwhich being alone and being truly alone are becoming different things.
1:46:211 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 21 SekundenYou can be physically by yourself in a room and never actually experience solitude because your phone is offering
1:46:281 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 28 Sekundenyou an endless stream of other minds to engage with. texts, feeds, notifications,
1:46:361 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthe low hum of digital connection that fills the space where quiet used to be.
1:46:421 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd there's nothing wrong with that in itself. Social connection is a genuine human need, and digital tools can serve it.
1:46:521 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 52 SekundenBut the question the research raises, and it's a question more than an answer at this point, is whether constant
1:46:581 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 58 Sekundenlow-grade digital contact is giving us the benefits of social connection or just preventing us from ever sitting
1:47:061 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwith ourselves long enough to develop the capacity for solitude. Sherry Turkl, the MIT sociologist who has been
1:47:151 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 15 Sekundenstudying the relationship between technology and selfhood since the 1980s, makes a careful argument about this.
1:47:231 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 23 SekundenShe's not a technophobe, and she's not saying screens are destroying us. What she's saying is subtler. She suggests
1:47:321 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthat when we reach for the phone, every time we feel the first flicker of boredom or discomfort, we're interrupting a process. the process of
1:47:411 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 41 Sekundenlearning to be alone with our own thoughts.
1:47:451 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe process that Winnott described as a developmental achievement and that the neuroscience now shows involves the
1:47:521 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 52 Sekundendefault mode network settling into a less reactive, more integrative pattern.
1:47:591 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 59 SekundenTurkl calls it the flight from conversation, but it might be more accurate to call it the flight from the inner conversation,
1:48:071 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 7 Sekundenthe one you have with yourself when there's nothing else to respond to. And this connects back to something Reed
1:48:141 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 14 SekundenLarson found in his experience sampling work with adolescence. Remember, he showed that teenagers who spent moderate
1:48:221 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 22 Sekundenamounts of time alone voluntarily showed better emotional adjustment than those who spent either too much or too little.
1:48:311 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe key word was voluntary.
1:48:341 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 34 SekundenChosen solitude in manageable doses seemed to help young people develop something, a tolerance for their own
1:48:421 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 42 Sekundeninner life, a familiarity with the rhythms of their own thinking. And you can see how constant digital stimulation
1:48:501 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 50 Sekundenmight interfere with that. Not dramatically, not catastrophically, but quietly by never letting the silence last long enough to become comfortable.
1:49:011 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 1 SekundeThis isn't a moral argument. It's a developmental one.
1:49:051 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 5 SekundenThe capacity for solitude seems to require at some point actual solitude.
1:49:121 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 12 SekundenNot permanent withdrawal, not a rejection of connection, just enough uninterrupted time with your own mind
1:49:191 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthat the default mode network can do what it does, which is wander and connect and process. And sometimes when the conditions are right, create.
1:49:331 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe research on creativity and solitude, the work that traces back to schooler and Smallwood studies on mind wandering
1:49:421 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 42 Sekundensuggests that some of the most useful cognitive work happens precisely when we're not trying to do anything. When
1:49:501 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthe mind is left to its own devices, it doesn't just idle. It recombines. It finds connections between things that
1:49:591 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 59 Sekundenseemed unrelated. It rehearses possible futures. It digests the past. And all of
1:50:061 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 6 Sekundenthat requires a kind of cognitive space that constant input, however pleasant, tends to fill. So here's the picture, as
1:50:151 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 15 Sekundenhonestly as the evidence allows us to draw it. Being alone is not one thing.
1:50:221 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 22 SekundenIt never has been. It's a space. And what happens in that space depends on what you bring to it. Bring a mind
1:50:311 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 31 Sekundenthat's been starved of connection, that's running Kaciopo's hypervigilance program, scanning every silence for threat, and the space will feel hostile.
1:50:431 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 43 SekundenBring a mind that's been gently trained through secure relationships, through practice, through the slow accumulation
1:50:511 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 51 Sekundenof experience with its own inner weather. And the same space becomes something else, not paradise, not bliss,
1:51:011 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 1 Sekundejust a room with someone in it you know reasonably well and don't mind spending time with. And the beautiful thing, the
1:51:101 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 10 Sekundengenuinely hopeful thing is that the research suggests movement between those two experiences is possible at almost
1:51:191 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 19 Sekundenany point in a life. The neural pathways aren't set in stone. The attachment patterns, while sticky, aren't destiny.
1:51:291 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 29 Sekundenthe default mode network, that restless self-reerential system that Rashel stumbled onto and Buckner mapped and
1:51:371 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 37 SekundenBrewer watched change in meditators. It responds to experience. It learns
1:51:441 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 44 Sekundenslowly, sometimes frustratingly, slowly, but it learns. The mind that finds
1:51:511 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 51 Sekundensilence unbearable at 25 may find it nourishing at 40.
1:51:561 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 56 SekundenNot because the silence changed, but because the mind did.
1:52:021 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 2 SekundenBecause somewhere along the way, through relationships, through reflection, through the ordinary accumulation of
1:52:111 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 11 Sekundenself-nowledge that comes from paying attention to your own life, the inner landscape became a little more familiar,
1:52:191 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 19 Sekundena little less threatening, a little more like home.
