2.025 Aufrufe  25.09.2026
How reliable are the memories we trust most?

In this video, we explore the strange science of false memories and why remembering is more like reconstruction than replay. From Elizabeth Loftus and the misinformation effect to eyewitness testimony, false confessions, sleep, the Mandela Effect, and the connection between memory and identity, we look at how the brain can reshape the past while still making those memories feel completely real.

Settle in for a slow journey through one of the most fascinating parts of human psychology.

Resources:

Elizabeth Loftus — Misinformation and the Malleability of Memory
https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16027...

Daniel Schacter — Constructive Episodic Simulation
https://dschacter.scholars.harvard.ed...

NIH / PMC — Sleep-Dependent Memory Consolidation and Reconsolidation
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles...

American Psychological Association — Reconstructive Memory & Eyewitness Testimony
https://www.apa.org/ed/precollege/top...
