47.301 Aufrufe  02.09.2026
How does memory actually work—and why does it sometimes fail us? In this slow, relaxing deep dive, we explore how the brain forms, stores, retrieves, and reshapes memories. From the hippocampus and synapses to working memory, sleep, forgetting, false memories, and Alzheimer’s disease, this video explains the fascinating science behind the memories that shape who we are.

Perfect for relaxing, learning, or falling asleep while exploring one of the brain’s most mysterious abilities.

Resources:

NCBI Bookshelf — Human Memory
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NB...

Nobel Prize — Eric Kandel and the Biology of Memory
https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/med...

National Library of Medicine — Sleep-Dependent Memory Consolidation
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles...

National Institute on Aging — What Is Alzheimer’s Disease?
https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/alzhei...
