would achieve. That's quietly reassuring given that nobody runs the internet. And
1:48:18
then there's the intersection of game theory and artificial intelligence, which brings the whole story full circle
1:48:25
in a way that feels almost destined. In March 2016, Google Deep Minds Alph Go
1:48:31
program defeated Lee Sadal, one of the greatest Go players in history in a
1:48:36
five-game match in Seoul. Go is a two-player zero sum game of perfect
1:48:42
information. Exactly the kind of game Zerlo and Vonomanyman theorized about.
1:48:48
But it's so complex, more possible board positions than atoms in the observable
1:48:53
universe that brute force miniax is utterly impossible. AlphaGo used deep
1:48:59
neural networks and Monte Carlo tree search to learn which positions were
1:49:04
strong and which moves were promising. It was in effect doing what the miniax
1:49:11
theorem proved was possible. playing at or near the optimal level, but using
1:49:16
pattern recognition and statistical learning rather than exhaustive calculation. And then came Alph Go Zero,
1:49:25
which learned Go entirely from scratch with no human knowledge at all. It
1:49:31
started knowing nothing but the rules of the game, played millions of games against itself, and emerged playing at a
1:49:38
superhuman level, discovering strategies that human masters had never found in
1:49:44
thousands of years. Think about what that is. It's a machine discovering game
1:49:49
theoretic equilibria through a process not unlike the natural selection that
1:49:54
Maynard Smith described. variation, testing, selection, [clears throat]
1:50:00
improvement over and over until the strategies converge on something
1:50:05
extraordinary. The game theory is the same. Only the mechanism of discovery
1:50:11
has changed. And so here we are. Game theory started as an abstract
1:50:17
mathematical curiosity. Fonyman proving theorems about parlor games in 1928.
1:50:24
It became the framework for thinking about nuclear armageddon at Rand. It became the foundation of modern
1:50:30
economics through Nash. It explained the ritualized violence of the animal
1:50:36
kingdom through Maynard Smith. It showed how cooperation can emerge from pure
1:50:42
selfishness through Axelrod. It became a practical engineering tool through
1:50:48
mechanism design, auctioning spectrum, matching medical residents, saving lives
1:50:54
through kidney exchanges. It entered the digital age through algorithmic game
1:50:59
theory, and it's now intertwined with artificial intelligence, with machines
1:51:05
discovering strategies that exceed human imagination. That's an extraordinary arc for a set of
1:51:11
ideas that began with a Hungarian prodigy wondering whether there was a
1:51:17
mathematics of poker bluffing. And lying here thinking about all of this as you settle deeper into your
1:51:24
pillow and the details begin to soften at the edges. There's something quietly profound about the whole enterprise.
1:51:31
Because game theory at bottom isn't really about games. It's about interdependence.
1:51:38
It's about the simple inescapable fact that almost nothing you do in life happens in a vacuum. Your choices ripple
1:51:46
outward. They affect other people and their choices affect you and the fabric
1:51:53
of social life. All the cooperation and competition and trust and betrayal and
1:51:59
negotiation and compromise that make up a human existence is woven from these
1:52:05
threads of mutual influence. The Prisoner's Dilemma shows you the trap.
1:52:10
Axelrod's Tournament shows you the escape. Mechanism design shows you how
1:52:16
to build the exit door into the architecture of the game itself. And the Nash equilibrium, that quiet, stubborn
1:52:24
concept, the resting point where nobody can do better alone, shows you where
1:52:29
things tend to end up for better or worse. Maybe the deepest lesson is the
1:52:34
simplest. You are always in a game in the technical sense. Not because life is
1:52:40
frivolous. Not because people are merely opponents to be beaten, but because your
1:52:45
outcomes depend on others and theirs depend on you. You can't opt out of that
1:52:51
interdependence. You're already playing. The only question is whether you play
1:52:56
with awareness, whether you see the incentives clearly, whether you recognize when you're in a prisoner's
1:53:03
dilemma and look for ways to change the game, to introduce repetition,
1:53:08
transparency, trust, institutions that align self-interest with the common
1:53:14
good. Game theory won't tell you what to value. It won't tell you what's right or
1:53:19
wrong. But it will show you the consequences of your choices given the
1:53:25
choices of others. It will show you where cooperation is possible and where
1:53:30
it's fragile. It will show you that the game itself, the rules, the structure,
1:53:36
the institutions that frame the interaction often matters more than the
1:53:42
intentions of the players. And sometimes that clarity, that ability to see the
1:53:47
structure beneath the chaos is the most valuable thing in the world. And there's
1:53:53
one more thing worth mentioning as you lie here letting all of this settle. One
1:53:58
of the most important lessons game theory teaches is that seeing the game clearly is itself a form of power. Most
1:54:07
of the worst traps in strategic interaction, the prisoners dilemmas, the
1:54:12
races to the bottom, the tragedies of the commons, persist partly because the
1:54:17
people caught in them don't recognize the structure of the situation they're in. They think the problem is that the
1:54:24
other side is unreasonable or that the system is unfair or that people are just
1:54:30
bad. And sometimes those things are true. But often the real problem is
1:54:36
structural. The game itself is set up in a way that makes bad outcomes inevitable
1:54:42
regardless of the intentions of the players. Once you see that, once you
1:54:47
genuinely internalize that the structure of the interaction matters as much as or
1:54:53
more than the character of the people involved, you gain something incredibly
1:54:59
valuable. You gain the ability to ask, "Can we change the game? Can we add
1:55:05
repetition so that cooperation becomes sustainable? Can we add transparency so
1:55:12
that cheaters get caught? Can we redesign the rules the way Vicy
1:55:17
redesigned auctions or Roth redesigned matching markets so that the equilibrium
1:55:23
shifts from a bad one to a good one? That's the promise of game theory in the
1:55:28
end. Not that it makes the world simple. It doesn't. Strategic interaction is
1:55:35
irreducibly complex. And game theory is honest about that complexity in a way
1:55:41
that most other frameworks aren't. But it gives you a vocabulary for the complexity. It gives you tools for
1:55:48
seeing patterns and it gives you in the best cases a blueprint for design, for
1:55:55
building systems and institutions and rules that make the world a little bit more like the one we'd all prefer to
1:56:02
live in. It's been quite a journey tonight. from Sunzu to Spectrum Auctions. From a Hungarian child prodigy
1:56:11
multiplying eight-digit numbers in his head to a 21-year-old in Princeton
1:56:16
proving that every game has an equilibrium. From the terrifying logic of nuclear standoffs to the quiet
1:56:23
elegance of an algorithm matching kidney donors with patients who need them. And
1:56:29
the through line, the thread that connects all of it is this simple recognition. We are all in this
1:56:37
together. Your fate is tangled with the fates of others. And the great project
1:56:43
of civilization, the thing we've been working on, however imperfectly, for
1:56:48
thousands of years, is figuring out how to make that entanglement work. So sleep
1:56:54
well tonight. The games go on in your markets, in your neighborhoods, in the
1:57:01
vast digital networks humming around the planet, in the slow evolutionary
1:57:06
tournament of life itself. Vonoyman saw the mathematics. Nash found the
1:57:12
equilibrium. Maynard Smith found it in nature. Axelrod showed that kindness
1:57:18
could win. Roth and Mgrim showed that the right game design could change the
1:57:24
world. And all of them across decades and disciplines and continents were
1:57:30
grappling with the same question you'll face again tomorrow morning when you step out the door and into the glorious
1:57:37
complicated strategic mess of other people. When your fate depends on others
1:57:43
and theirs depends on you. What do you do? The math has some suggestions, but
1:57:49
the choice, as always, is yours. Good night.
